window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"u":"bolt-rifle.html","t":"Bolt Rifle Shooting","s":[{"id":"","h":"Bolt Rifle Shooting","x":"Firearm training · Professional Updated Oct 27, 2026 Bolt Rifle Shooting The bolt-action rifle is the precision and hunting tool: one round at a time, a fixed barrel and action, and usually a magnified optic. This page covers how the action works, cartridges, mounting and setting up a scope, positions with bipod, bag, and sling, running the bolt without breaking position, zeroing and keeping data, the ballistic calculator, wind at distance, and maintenance. Built on U.S. Army TC 3-22.10 Sniper , TC 3-22.9 Rifle and Carbine , and common precision-rifle practice. Torque values and load data are typical figures; your rifle's manufacturer and your own chronograph come first. Right-handed shooter assumed. The fundamentals that every rifle shares (natural point of aim, trigger, MOA and mils) are covered in more depth on the Rifle page."},{"id":"safety","h":"Safety","x":"01 Safety The four rules Treat every firearm as if it's loaded. Never point the muzzle at anything you aren't willing to destroy. Keep your finger off the trigger until your sights are on the target and you've decided to fire. Be sure of your target and what's beyond it. Bolt-rifle specific Full-power rifle cartridges travel 3 to 4 miles. Every shot needs a backstop you can see, and a scope narrows what you see around the target. The safety blocks the trigger or firing pin, not the shooter. Some older triggers have fired when the safety was moved to \"fire\" with no finger on the trigger, so keep the muzzle in a safe direction whenever you touch it. On a three-position safety, the middle position lets you cycle rounds out with the safety on. Learn which position your rifle uses for unloading. Clear a bolt gun by opening the bolt, removing the magazine or emptying the internal magazine through the floorplate, and checking the chamber by eye and finger. Leave the bolt open, or remove it, on the line. Never use a scope as a pair of binoculars on anything you wouldn't shoot. See On the range on the Rifle page for range commands and clearing the gun before you step off the line."},{"id":"action","h":"The action","x":"02 The action Lifting the bolt handle rotates the bolt, unlocking its lugs from recesses behind the chamber. On most modern actions the same lift cocks the firing pin (\"cock on open\"). Pulling the bolt back extracts and ejects the fired case; pushing it forward strips the next round from the magazine and chambers it, and turning the handle down locks it. Feature Options What it means for you Feeding Push feed (Remington 700, Savage, Tikka) or controlled-round feed (Mauser 98, Winchester Model 70 Classic) With controlled feed, the extractor grips the case rim as soon as it leaves the magazine, so a round can't double-feed if you short-stroke the bolt. Push feed is simpler and usually more accurate out of the box. Magazine Blind internal, hinged floorplate, or detachable box Detachable boxes are faster to reload and unload. Internal magazines are topped off from the top, one round at a time, or emptied through the floorplate. Safety Two-position, three-position (Model 70, many customs), or tang-mounted Know whether \"safe\" also locks the bolt closed. Three-position safeties add a middle setting that locks the trigger but lets you unload. Trigger Fixed or user-adjustable; single-stage or two-stage A clean 2.5–4 lb break suits most field and precision shooting. Adjust only within the manufacturer's range. Length Short action (.308 family, 6.5 Creedmoor) or long action (.30-06, .300 Win Mag) Long actions need a longer bolt throw. The cartridge, not preference, decides which one you need."},{"id":"cart","h":"Cartridges","x":"03 Cartridges Cartridge Typical bullets Velocity Recoil Common use .223 Rem / 5.56 55–77 gr 2,750–3,200 ft/s Very light Training, varmint, low-cost practice to 600 yd 6.5 Creedmoor 120–147 gr 2,650–2,900 ft/s Light Precision competition, deer-size game, long-range practice .308 Win / 7.62 NATO 150–178 gr 2,550–2,850 ft/s Moderate General purpose, law-enforcement and military marksman rifles, deer and elk .30-06 Springfield 150–180 gr 2,700–2,900 ft/s Moderate All North American game; ammunition is everywhere .300 Win Mag 180–215 gr 2,850–2,960 ft/s Heavy Long range, elk and larger game; shorter barrel life Recoil matters more than most people admit. A cartridge you can shoot without flinching, and afford to practice with, will put more rounds where you want them than a bigger one you dread. For learning the fundamentals, a .223 or 6.5 Creedmoor bolt gun teaches more per dollar than a magnum."},{"id":"scope","h":"Scope setup","x":"04 Scope setup Mounting Degrease the base and ring screws. Torque base screws and ring screws to the manufacturer's numbers, commonly 15–25 in-lb for ring caps and higher for base screws. Tighten ring caps evenly in a cross pattern so the gaps on each side match. A canted (20 or 30 MOA) base gives you more elevation travel for long range. Use the lowest rings that let the objective clear the barrel. A lower scope is easier to get a consistent cheek weld behind. Eye relief, level, and parallax Eye relief: in your shooting position with the scope at highest power, slide the scope until the full picture appears with no dark ring, then tighten the rings. Level: level the rifle first, then the reticle to it. A canted reticle moves impacts sideways at distance. A bubble level on the scope tube shows cant while you shoot. Parallax: adjust the side focus until the reticle doesn't move against the target when you shift your head slightly. Then the reticle and target are in the same focal plane. Choice Options Notes Focal plane First focal plane (FFP) or second (SFP) In FFP the reticle grows with magnification, so hash marks are correct at every power. In SFP they're correct at one power only, usually maximum. Units Mil or MOA Match the turrets to the reticle: mil/mil or MOA/MOA. Mixed units force conversions under pressure. Magnification 3–9×, 4–16×, 5–25× Most field shooting happens at 6–12×. More magnification shows more wobble and less of the surrounding area."},{"id":"pos","h":"Positions and support","x":"05 Positions and support Accuracy comes from the position more than the rifle. The rifle should rest on support and bone, not on muscle, and point at the target naturally ( natural point of aim ) before you touch the trigger. Prone with bipod Body slightly offset behind the rifle, firing-side knee drawn up a little to lift the chest off the ground. Load the bipod : lean gently forward into it so the legs are preloaded. Recoil then comes straight back and the rifle tracks the same way every shot. Rear bag The support hand squeezes a rear bag under the toe of the stock. Squeezing raises the muzzle and relaxing lowers it, so you can fine-tune elevation without muscling the rifle. Sling A loop sling on the support arm turns the arm into a tensioned strut for prone and sitting. A hasty sling wrapped around the forearm is faster and still tightens the position. Neither takes extra gear. Field supports A pack, a fence rail, a tree, or a tripod. Pad hard surfaces with a glove or bag, and never rest the barrel itself on anything, because contact shifts the point of impact."},{"id":"bolt","h":"Running the bolt","x":"06 Running the bolt Keep your cheek on the stock and your eye behind the scope. Only the firing hand moves. Lift, pull straight back hard to the stop, push forward hard, and turn down, in one motion. A firm, full stroke ejects and feeds reliably; a timid one causes most bolt-gun malfunctions. Return the hand to the grip, finger straight, and reacquire the reticle before deciding on the next shot. Watch the impact or the trace while you cycle. A shooter who stays in the scope sees their own miss and can correct. Short-stroking and other stoppages Short-stroke: the bolt wasn't pulled far enough to pick up the next round, or (with push feed) wasn't pulled back far enough to eject before a new round fed. Open fully, clear the case or round, and feed again. Failure to extract: a stuck case usually means a pressure sign or a dirty chamber. Stop shooting that ammunition and look at the case head and primer. Hard bolt lift on a fired case: often a sign of high pressure. Stop, check the load and the chamber, and don't keep firing to \"see what happens.\""},{"id":"shot","h":"The shot process","x":"07 The shot process Each shot follows the same order: position and natural point of aim, sight picture, a breath held at the natural pause, smooth trigger press straight to the rear, and follow-through. Hold the trigger to the rear and keep your eye in the scope until the rifle settles, then call the shot: say where the reticle was when it broke before you look at the target. Called shots that match impacts mean your fundamentals are working; called shots that don't mean the rifle, ammunition, or data needs attention."},{"id":"zero","h":"Zeroing and data","x":"08 Zeroing and data Zero Bore-sight at 25 yd, or look through the bore with the bolt removed and center the target, then adjust the reticle to match. At 100 yd, fire a three- or five-shot group from a solid prone position. Measure from the group center to the aim point and dial the correction (1 MOA ≈ 1.047 in at 100 yd; 0.1 mil = 0.36 in at 100 yd). Confirm with another group, then set the turret's zero stop. Data (DOPE) Record every string: date, temperature, altitude, distance, elevation and wind dialed, and where the rounds hit. Chronograph your ammunition. Velocity printed on the box is from a test barrel and often runs high. Shoot at real distances and correct (true) the calculator's velocity or BC to match actual drops. Track the cold-bore shot separately. The first shot from a clean, cold barrel can land away from the rest of the group."},{"id":"calc","h":"Ballistic calculator","x":"09 Ballistic calculator Pick a cartridge and load, or enter your own chronographed velocity and the bullet's published G1 ballistic coefficient. Sight height for a scoped bolt gun is usually 1.5–2.0 inches (bore center to scope center). Set \"Hold at\" for the exact elevation and wind correction at a given distance."},{"id":"wind","h":"Wind and distance","x":"10 Wind and distance Past about 300 yards, wind usually causes more misses than elevation. Read it at the target, midway, and at your position: flags, grass, dust, and mirage (the heat shimmer you see through a scope focused just short of the target) all show direction and rough speed. Wind closest to you deflects the bullet most, because it acts on the bullet over the rest of its flight. Effect What it does When it matters Crosswind Full value at 3 and 9 o'clock, about half at 1, 5, 7, and 11 Every distance; dominates past 300 yd Spin drift The spinning bullet drifts toward the direction of twist (right with right-hand twist) Past about 600 yd, a few inches to a foot or more Angle Shooting steeply up or down, the bullet drops as if the target were closer: hold for the horizontal distance Mountains, rooftops, steep valleys Density altitude Thinner, warmer air means less drag and less drop Long range and large changes in altitude or temperature"},{"id":"maint","h":"Maintenance","x":"11 Maintenance Clean from the breech with a bore guide and a one-piece coated rod so the crown and throat aren't damaged. Clean the chamber and lug recesses, which brushes miss. Check the action screws and scope mounts before each trip. Torque action screws to the stock maker's specification; loose action screws move zero. Note the round count. Barrels wear fastest at the throat; accuracy often declines after a few thousand rounds in hot cartridges such as the .300 Win Mag and 6.5 Creedmoor. Keep a light film of oil on the bolt lugs and cocking cam, and a dry bore before you shoot. Store in a dry place with the bolt open or removed."},{"id":"drills","h":"Drills and standards","x":"12 Drills and standards Drill Setup Standard Builds Cold bore First shot of the day at 100 yd on a 1-inch dot, before any warm-up Inside 1 MOA of your zero; log it every time A known first-shot point of impact 5-shot group Prone, bipod and rear bag, 100 yd Under 1 MOA with match ammunition Fundamentals and the rifle's baseline Bolt manipulation Five shots on a 2 MOA target at 100 yd, cycling from the shoulder All hits in 30 seconds, eye never leaves the scope Running the bolt without breaking position KD ladder Steel at 200, 300, 400, 500 yd First-round hit at each using your data book Data and wind calls Positional Two shots each from prone, sitting with sling, kneeling off a pack, and standing off a barricade on a 10-inch plate at 200 yd 6 of 8 hits in 2 minutes Building stable field positions fast More drills, scored and timed, are on the Common Practice Drills page."},{"id":"errors","h":"Common errors","x":"13 Common errors Muscling the rifle onto the target instead of moving the body to the natural point of aim. The rifle returns to where your body points it as it recoils. Lifting the head to cycle or watch the target. Stay in the scope. Unequal cheek pressure or eye relief between shots, which shows up as vertical stringing. Ignoring parallax and cant, which move impacts without any other mistake. Trusting the box velocity instead of a chronograph and real drops. Resting the barrel on a support instead of the stock."}]},{"u":"calculator.html","t":"Ballistics Calculator","s":[{"id":"","h":"Ballistics Calculator","x":"Firearm training · Ballistics Updated Oct 26, 2026 Ballistics Calculator Drop, holds in MOA and mils, wind drift, velocity, energy, and time of flight for 36 cartridges and more than 130 common loads: rimfire, handgun and pistol-caliber carbine, intermediate rifle, hunting and long-range rifle, lever-action and straight-wall, and shotgun shot, buckshot, and slugs. A point-mass solver using the standard G1 drag model. Load data are typical published figures; your barrel length, ammunition lot, and conditions change them. Chronograph your own gun and confirm every hold on paper before you rely on it."},{"id":"calc","h":"Calculator","x":"01 Calculator"},{"id":"use","h":"How to use it","x":"02 How to use it Pick the cartridge, then the load closest to what you shoot. The fields fill in with typical values. Replace the muzzle velocity with your own chronograph reading. It matters more than any other input. Measure your sight height: the distance from the center of the bore to the center of the sight or optic. Set your zero distance, your temperature and altitude, and the wind. Type a distance into \"Hold at\" for the exact elevation and wind correction there. The table and chart update as you type. Shoot at real distances and adjust the velocity until the calculator matches your hits. That's called truing."},{"id":"inputs","h":"What the inputs mean","x":"03 What the inputs mean Input What it is Effect Bullet weight In grains (7,000 per pound) Used for energy. Drop comes from velocity and BC. Muzzle velocity Speed leaving the barrel, in ft/s The biggest single factor in drop. Shorter barrels are slower. G1 BC Ballistic coefficient: how well the bullet keeps its speed against air drag Higher BC means less drop and drift at long range. Sight height Bore center to sight center, in inches Matters most at close range and for the near zero distance. About 2.6\" on an AR, 1.5–2\" on a scoped bolt gun, 0.5–0.6\" on a handgun. Zero The distance where the bullet crosses the line of sight Sets where you're on, and how high the bullet rises between. Wind Speed in mph and the clock direction it comes from 3 and 9 o'clock are full value; 12 and 6 have no drift. Temperature and altitude Air density Warmer, higher air is thinner: less drop and drift."},{"id":"limits","h":"Limits","x":"04 Limits G1 vs. G7. Long, boat-tailed match bullets are better described by the G7 model. Past about 800 yards, G1 figures can be off by several inches; use the bullet maker's own calculator for long-range work. Shot and buckshot are plotted as one pellet with an estimated drag figure for a round ball. Use the result for drop and retained energy, not precision. Patterning your gun matters more. Not modeled: spin drift, Coriolis effect, shooting at steep angles, and the transonic instability some bullets show as they slow through the speed of sound. Subsonic loads (.300 and 8.6 Blackout, standard-velocity .22 LR) drop steeply past 100 yards. The calculator shows it, but small range errors become large misses. The Rifle , Shotgun , Handgun , and Bolt Rifle pages each have the same calculator set up for their own cartridges."}]},{"u":"cards.html","t":"Printable Cards","s":[{"id":"","h":"Printable Cards","x":"Preparedness · Printable cards Updated Nov 27, 2026 Printable Cards One-page cards to print, fill in, and keep where you'll need them: in the go-bag, on the fridge, in the glovebox, by the radio. Each one prints on its own page in plain black and white. Use the Print button on a card to print just that card, or your browser's print command to print all of them (one per page). Fill them in by hand; nothing you write leaves the paper."},{"id":"card-bleed","h":"Bleeding control","x":"01 Bleeding control Print Make the scene safe. Call 911. Gloves on if you have them. Then work in order: M · Massive bleeding Arm or leg: tourniquet 2–3 in above the wound on bare skin, never over a joint (high and tight over clothing if under threat or you can't see the wound); tighten until bleeding stops and no pulse below; write the time. Add a second above the first if needed. Groin, armpit, neck: pack the wound tight, then hold hard pressure: 3+ min with hemostatic gauze, about 10 min with plain gauze or cloth. A · Airway Unconscious but breathing: roll onto the side (recovery position). R · Respiration Look for chest wounds; seal any open wound with a vented chest seal, or a non-vented seal or plastic taped on all four sides. C · Circulation Recheck your bleeding control. Look for other wounds, including under the body. H · Hypothermia / head Keep them warm and off the ground. Protect a possible head or spine injury. Don't loosen a tourniquet once it's on. Don't trade working pressure for a loose wrap. Tell the medics what happened, what you found, what you did, and when. Reference, not training: take a Stop the Bleed class. More on the Field Medical page."},{"id":"card-family","h":"Family emergency plan","x":"02 Family emergency plan Print Meeting place, near home Meeting place, outside the neighborhood Out-of-area contact (name, phone) Evacuation route 1 / route 2 Water shut-off location Gas shut-off location (only if you smell gas) Electrical panel / main breaker Go-bags and home kit are kept Name Phone Medical needs / medications / allergies Doctor / pharmacy Schools / workplaces and their plans Pets (names, vet, food) Insurance (company, policy #) Keep one copy in every go-bag and one at home. Based on Ready.gov's family emergency plan."},{"id":"card-comms","h":"Communications plan","x":"03 Communications plan Print PACE: try each method in order. Move to the next if it fails or after the agreed time. Method Details (number, channel, frequency, location) Primary Alternate Contingency Emergency Radio channel / name Frequency Service (GMRS, MURS, FRS, ham, CB) Use Check-in times If no contact by ___, go to Weather / news station Assume radio traffic is public. Follow FCC licensing for each service. More on the Analogue Comms page."},{"id":"card-water","h":"Water treatment","x":"04 Water treatment Print Cloudy? Let it settle and strain through cloth or a coffee filter first. Possibly chemical (fuel, floodwater)? Don't drink it; nothing at home fixes that. Method How Boil (best) Rolling boil for 1 minute; 3 minutes above 6,500 ft. Unscented bleach, 6% 2 drops per quart · 8 drops (1/8 tsp) per gallon · wait 30 min Unscented bleach, 8.25% 2 drops per quart · 6 drops per gallon · wait 30 min Unscented bleach, 5–9% (incl. 7.5%) CDC simple rule: 8 drops (a little under 1/8 tsp) per gallon · 16 drops (1/4 tsp) if cloudy · wait 30 min Check Should smell faintly of chlorine. If not, dose again and wait 15 more minutes. Filter + tablet Filter removes bacteria and protozoa; chlorine dioxide tablet adds viruses. Follow the package wait time. Water in the house: water heater tank (power or gas off first), pipes (main off, open the top faucet, drain the lowest), toilet tank (not the bowl). Plan: 1 gallon per person per day, two weeks. From CDC and EPA emergency disinfection guidance. More on the Water page."},{"id":"card-load","h":"Backup power load sheet","x":"05 Backup power load sheet Print List what you'll run in an outage. Watts are on the appliance label; energy = watts × hours ÷ 1000. Load Running watts Starting watts Hours / day kWh / day Totals (largest only) Generator / inverter needed Running total + largest starting surge = ______ W Battery needed kWh/day × days ÷ 0.8 = ______ kWh Fuel on hand / runtime Generators outside only, 20+ ft from the house. CO alarms inside. More on the Off-Grid Configurations page."}]},{"u":"comms.html","t":"Analogue Comms","s":[{"id":"","h":"Analogue Comms","x":"Preparedness · Communications Updated Nov 27, 2026 Analogue Comms Keeping local, regional, and long-distance communication going when the internet, the cell network, or the power grid is down: radio simplex, field telephones and wired Morse, HF radio, receive-only news and weather, independent power, and the procedures that make it all work. This is a reference, not legal advice. Radio transmission in the U.S. is licensed and regulated by the FCC: check which service each radio is authorized for, get the license your service requires, and verify the current rules before you transmit. Receiving is generally unrestricted; transmitting is not."},{"id":"principles","h":"Design principles","x":"01 Design principles The goal is communication that keeps working when the infrastructure doesn't: no internet, no cell towers, no grid power. Every choice below follows from a few rules. Direct links first Prefer links that depend on nothing in between: radio in simplex, one set talking straight to another, and field wire you installed yourself. Own your power Give each essential station its own battery and a way to recharge it that doesn't need the grid. Two ways to every contact Keep at least two different ways to reach each important contact, where practical, so one failure doesn't cut them off. Paper, not screens Keep printed channel plans, operating instructions, contact schedules, maps, and logs where every operator can reach them. Test in your terrain Manufacturer range claims are not planning figures. Find out what actually works by testing with your own equipment, on your own ground. Stay legal Follow the FCC licensing and equipment-certification rules for every radio service you transmit on."},{"id":"layers","h":"Communications layers","x":"02 Communications layers No single system covers every distance and every failure, so the plan is built in layers. Each one has its own job and its own weak point. Layer Equipment Primary use Depends on Local GMRS, MURS, FRS, or properly licensed amateur VHF/UHF radios Property, vehicles, nearby contacts Batteries and compatible radios; no repeater in simplex Local / road CB radio Vehicles and nearby public contacts Power, an antenna, and other CB operators Fixed-point Two field telephones with two-conductor cable House to shop, or other fixed stations Intact cable, compatible sets, local power as required Fixed-point messaging Wired Morse key and sounder or buzzer Simple point-to-point messages A dedicated circuit, a receiver, a battery Regional / long distance HF amateur radio, SSB voice and CW Morse Contacts beyond local radio range Power, an antenna, licensed operators, suitable propagation Information AM/FM/shortwave and NOAA weather radio News, warnings, outside information Broadcast stations still on the air Last resort Flags, signal lamps, written messages Line-of-sight or hand-delivered messages Visibility or physical access"},{"id":"handhelds","h":"Handheld radios in simplex","x":"03 Handheld radios in simplex Handheld radios such as Baofengs work without internet, cell towers, GPS, or grid power. In simplex , one radio transmits straight to another on the same frequency. All they need is charged batteries, matching frequencies and settings, and a usable radio path between them. A repeater extends range, but it is optional, and it is one more thing that can fail. Range planning Environment Illustrative handheld-to-handheld range Dense woods, uneven terrain About 0.5 to 2 miles Rolling rural terrain About 1 to 5 miles Open ground About 3 to 8 miles Favorable elevated-to-elevated path 10 to 20+ miles possible These are rough examples, not guaranteed performance. Real range depends heavily on line of sight, hills, vegetation, antenna height, interference, and how the radio is set up. Survey the area you need to cover with the real equipment. Handheld checklist Know your radios Write down the exact model of every unit and check which radio service each one is authorized to transmit on. Program for simplex Program compatible, authorized simplex channels and don't build the plan around repeaters. Label every radio and keep a printed channel plan that matches it. Walk the coverage Test from the house, the workshop, the property boundaries, the roads, and the low spots. The low spots are where it fails. Antenna before power A properly installed, elevated base antenna usually buys more range than a higher-power handheld. Fix the antenna first. Batteries and charging Keep spare batteries and a 12 V or solar-backed way to charge them. Assume you're heard Use plain-language procedures and assume anyone with a receiver can hear your traffic. Radio is not private."},{"id":"license","h":"Licensing","x":"04 Licensing Common UV-5R-type radios are usually amateur-service transceivers. They are not automatically authorized to transmit on GMRS, FRS, or MURS, even though they can tune those frequencies. Service License Notes Amateur (ham) Individual license, by exam Each operator needs their own license. Technician covers local VHF/UHF; General opens most HF voice privileges. GMRS FCC license, no exam One license covers eligible family members. Radios must be certified for GMRS. FRS None Use compliant, certified FRS radios; low power, fixed antennas. MURS None Use compliant, certified MURS radios on the five VHF MURS channels. CB None Use certified CB radios within the power limits. Frequencies and power limits Service Band and channels Maximum power Key rules FRS UHF 462/467 MHz · 22 channels 2 W (ch 1–7, 15–22) · 0.5 W (ch 8–14) No license · fixed antenna · no repeaters GMRS UHF 462/467 MHz · 22 channels shared with FRS, plus 8 repeater inputs 5 W (ch 1–7) · 0.5 W (ch 8–14) · 50 W (ch 15–22, mobile or base) FCC license, no exam, covers immediate family · repeaters allowed MURS VHF · 5 channels: 151.820, 151.880, 151.940, 154.570, 154.600 MHz 2 W No license · external antenna allowed (max 60 ft above ground or 20 ft above the structure) · no repeaters CB HF 26.965–27.405 MHz · 40 channels (ch 9 emergency, ch 19 highway) 4 W AM/FM · 12 W PEP SSB No license · certified CB radio Rules change. Verify the current FCC rules for your service and equipment before you transmit."},{"id":"wired","h":"Wired communications","x":"05 Wired communications A wire you lay yourself can't be jammed, doesn't depend on propagation, and doesn't need a phone company. Field telephones Military-style field telephones such as the TA-312/PT set up a point-to-point voice circuit over two-conductor field wire, with no phone company or internet service involved. Turning the crank rings the other station; internal batteries typically power the speech circuit. Wired telegraph A wired Morse circuit needs a key, a battery, the wire and its return circuit, and a sounder or buzzer at the far end. A Morse key by itself can't reach a distant station: it's only the switch."},{"id":"hf","h":"HF and Morse","x":"06 HF and Morse An HF transceiver with a suitable antenna can reach regional or long-distance contacts by SSB voice or CW Morse, well past anything a handheld can do. Range and reliability change with frequency, time of day, season, and ionospheric conditions, so a contact that works at night may not work at noon. NVIS (near-vertical incidence skywave) techniques send the signal almost straight up so it comes down over a wide area around you, which can fill the gap between handheld range and long-distance skip for some regional contacts. A General-class amateur license is the practical target for broad U.S. HF voice privileges; check the band-specific rules. CW Morse gets through on weaker signals and simpler equipment than voice, which is why it's worth learning."},{"id":"receive","h":"Receive-only information","x":"07 Receive-only information Keep a standalone AM/FM/shortwave receiver and a NOAA weather-alert radio. A scanner can add local situational awareness where those transmissions continue. None of these need the internet to receive, but all of them depend on the stations they listen to staying on the air."},{"id":"power","h":"Power and physical resilience","x":"08 Power and physical resilience One standard Standardize fixed stations on fused 12 V DC where practical, so radios, chargers, and lights share one power system. Battery and solar Use a suitably rated LiFePO4 battery with a compatible charger and a solar charge controller. Handheld spares Keep independent spare handheld batteries and the charging cables to go with them. Antennas and lightning Install outdoor antennas with proper grounding, lightning protection, and safe cable routing. Protected documents Store printed documentation and essential spares somewhere protected that you can still get to."},{"id":"procedures","h":"Operating procedures","x":"09 Operating procedures Equipment matters, but prearranged contacts, procedures, and regular tests are what make a working network. Agree in advance Set primary and backup channels, scheduled check-in times, contact priorities, and simple message formats before you need them. Log it Keep written logs of contacts, times, and what was passed. Train everyone Every intended operator should be able to power up, find the right channel, and pass a message without help. Test it dark Run a monthly test with phones and internet deliberately unavailable, and test on battery power only from time to time."},{"id":"checklist","h":"Readiness checklist","x":"10 Readiness checklist Work down the list in order: the first steps make what you already own reliable before you buy anything new. # Step 1 Inventory your handheld models, batteries, chargers, and antennas. 2 Verify which service each radio is authorized for, and get operator licenses. 3 Set up and test a local simplex radio network. 4 Improve base-station antenna placement and weather protection. 5 Build a two-station wired field-telephone circuit where it's useful. 6 Add independent AM/FM/shortwave and NOAA weather reception. 7 Set up fused battery and solar-backed power. 8 Add CB if nearby vehicle or community contacts justify it. 9 Work toward an amateur HF license, an HF station, and Morse proficiency. 10 Prepare passive signaling and printed message procedures. 11 Run a full off-grid communications exercise and write down the coverage gaps. Priority order First Make the handheld radios you already have reliable in simplex: batteries, matched programming, and an elevated base antenna. Second Set up a fixed wired link and receive-only news and weather. Third Add HF radio and CW for longer-range contacts."}]},{"u":"cover.html","t":"Cover and Concealment","s":[{"id":"","h":"Cover and Concealment","x":"Medical & fieldcraft · Cover and concealment Updated Oct 26, 2026 Cover and Concealment Cover stops the rounds being fired at you. Concealment only hides you. Both are useful, they aren't interchangeable, and confusing the two is one of the most common and costly mistakes in a gunfight. This page collects the definitions, what common materials actually stop, and how to use cover indoors, around vehicles, and on open ground, whatever you're carrying. Built on U.S. Army soldier common-task doctrine (TC 3-21.75) and common defensive and law-enforcement training. Material ratings are general guidance, not test data: construction, angle, and the specific round all change the result. When in doubt, treat it as concealment."},{"id":"defs","h":"Cover vs. concealment","x":"01 Cover vs. concealment Same rounds, two hiding places Cover Something solid enough to stop incoming fire: an engine block, a large tree, earth, thick masonry. Fight from behind it, reload behind it, fix malfunctions behind it, or wait behind it for help. It's temporary: repeated hits wear it down, and an attacker can move to a new angle. Concealment Something that keeps you from being seen but won't stop a bullet: drywall, doors, car doors, hedges, furniture, darkness, smoke. It still helps, because you can't be aimed at if you can't be seen. Use it to move, reposition, or break contact. Expect fire through it at where you were last seen. Cover depends on the threat's weapon, not yours. A brick wall that stops a 9mm may give way to a rifle after a few hits. Judge every position against what's being fired at you."},{"id":"materials","h":"What stops what","x":"02 What stops what What stops what Material Handgun rounds Rifle rounds Interior walls (drywall) Concealment Rounds pass through several Concealment Exterior wood-frame walls Concealment Concealment Wood doors, fences, furniture Concealment Concealment Appliances, filled bookcases Partial Inconsistent Concealment Car doors, body panels, glass Concealment Some rounds deflect; don't count on it Concealment Engine block, wheel and axle Cover Cover for most rounds Brick wall Cover for several hits Short-term Breaks down under repeated hits Hollow concrete block Cover for several hits Short-term Poured concrete, solid masonry Cover Cover for a while Large tree trunk Cover Depends on diameter and wood Utility pole Cover for a narrow target Partial Sheet-metal buildings, hay, brush Concealment Concealment Earth: berms, ditches, ground Cover Cover with 1–2 ft of dirt Shotgun slugs behave much like heavy handgun rounds against most materials; buckshot pellets penetrate less than either, but still pass through interior walls."},{"id":"principles","h":"Principles","x":"03 Principles Don't hug hard surfaces Stand back from it A few feet back lets you see more around the edge with less of your body showing, and keeps your muzzle from sticking out past the edge where it can be seen or grabbed. Three to six feet when space allows. Don't hug hard surfaces A bullet that hits a wall, a car, or the ground at a shallow angle tends to skip along it a few inches off the surface, straight into someone pressed against it. Cover is directional A wall protects you from one direction only. Know where the threat is, and expect it to move to an angle where your cover no longer helps. Cover buys time Use it for the things that take time and attention: reloading, clearing a malfunction, calling for help, treating a wound, deciding your next move."},{"id":"shooting","h":"Shooting from cover","x":"04 Shooting from cover Exposure at a corner Work the edge in small steps (slicing the pie), so you see the threat a little at a time instead of stepping out into the open. Shoot around the side rather than over the top when you can; it exposes less of your head. Get low behind low cover. Kneeling or prone behind an engine block or a berm hides far more of you than standing. Mind the mechanical offset with a long gun. The optic sits above the bore: the dot can be clear of a low edge while the barrel is still behind it, and the round hits your own cover. Switch shoulders or hands to match the side of cover if you've trained it. It cuts exposure on the support-side corner. Don't stay in one spot longer than you have to. Once the threat knows where you are, change position or angle when you can."},{"id":"moving","h":"Moving between cover","x":"05 Moving between cover Rushing between cover Know where you're going before you leave, and the route to it. Move in short rushes. The Army's rule of thumb is about three to five seconds in the open: \"I'm up, he sees me, I'm down.\" Reload before you move , not halfway across. Use concealment along the way to hide the movement, and come up somewhere other than where you went down. In a group, one moves while another covers. Say it out loud: \"Moving,\" \"Move.\""},{"id":"indoors","h":"Indoors","x":"06 Indoors Inside a house Inside most homes and offices, almost nothing is cover. Interior walls, doors, and furniture are concealment, and rounds fired inside pass through several rooms. Position matters more than walls. Pick a spot where a threat has to come to you through a doorway or hallway, ideally with something solid behind them. Kitchens and bathrooms may have appliances, cabinets, and tile that slow rounds, but don't rely on them. Know what's behind your target. Your own rounds pass through the same walls; family members' rooms, neighbors, and the street are all downrange. A long barrel crowds doorways. Stay back from the frame with a rifle or shotgun so the muzzle doesn't lead you around the corner."},{"id":"vehicles","h":"Vehicles","x":"07 Vehicles Using a vehicle Where the cover is The engine block , and the wheels and axles . Everything else, doors, body panels, seats, and glass, is concealment. Using it Get low behind the front wheel and engine. Keep your feet behind a wheel; rounds fired under the car hit ankles and feet. Stay a few feet back from the body; rounds skip along it. Glass deflects bullets unpredictably, both coming in and going out."},{"id":"outdoors","h":"Open ground","x":"08 Open ground Open ground Earth is the best cover outdoors: a ditch, a berm, a dip in the ground, a road embankment. Prone behind a fold of dirt beats standing behind a fence. Large trees and stone walls are cover; brush, fences, and sheet-metal outbuildings are not. Hay bales stacked deep slow handgun rounds but don't reliably stop rifle rounds. Low ground can hide you completely; learn to see the dead space a threat can't shoot into."},{"id":"concealment","h":"Using concealment","x":"09 Using concealment After you're seen Break line of sight to move. A threat that can't see you can't aim at you, and has to guess where you'll reappear. Darkness is concealment. Use light only when you need it, then move; the light shows where you were. Don't stay behind concealment once you've been seen. Expect rounds through it at that spot. Concealment is not a firing position. If you have to shoot from behind it, move afterward."},{"id":"errors","h":"Common errors","x":"10 Common errors Cover or concealment? Treating car doors, interior walls, or fences as cover. Hugging cover, which exposes you to skipping rounds and pushes the muzzle past the edge. Staying in one spot after the threat knows where you are. Leaving cover without knowing where you're going next. Reloading or fixing a malfunction in the open when cover is a few steps away. Forgetting that cover against a handgun may not be cover against a rifle. See the defensive pages for each platform, rifle , shotgun , handgun , and bolt rifle , for how cover works with that gun."}]},{"u":"cqb.html","t":"Recon & CQB Primer","s":[{"id":"recon","h":"Recon fundamentals","x":"01 Recon fundamentals Recon answers the leader's questions before the team commits. You go to see the ground, the structures, and the people, and you report what you find quickly and accurately. Recon elements avoid a decisive fight. Once they're pinned down, they stop collecting. Seven fundamentals Ensure continuous reconnaissance before, during, and after the operation Don't keep recon assets in reserve ; idle recon gathers nothing Orient on the recon objective : the terrain, the building, or the threat you were sent for Report rapidly and accurately ; old information loses value fast Retain freedom of maneuver ; always have a way out Gain and maintain contact once found, by eyes or sensors Develop the situation rapidly so the leader can decide SLLS at every halt Stop in cover, weapons oriented outward Look near to far, scanning in overlapping strips about 150 feet deep Listen for engines, voices, doors, dogs, generators Smell for cooking, fuel, smoke, tobacco, fresh paint Hold 1–5 minutes so your senses adjust and the area settles after your arrival. Longer at night. SALUTE report Element What to send Example S Size Number of people or vehicles 4 personnel, 1 pickup A Activity What they're doing Loading crates, 2 on overwatch L Location Grid or clear reference point Gray two-story, NE corner of 3rd & Elm U Unit / uniform Markings, clothing, identifiers Dark work clothes, no markings T Time When observed, not when sent 1415 local E Equipment Weapons, radios, vehicles, tools 2 long guns, handheld radios Send what you saw, not what you think it means. If you add an assessment, label it as one (\"assess they are preparing to leave\"). Report negative information too. \"No movement on sides 2 and 3 for 40 minutes\" is useful."},{"id":"planning","h":"Planning the recon","x":"02 Planning the recon A recon without clear questions comes back with a lot of observations and few answers. Start from what the leader needs to know, then plan routes, observation points, and timings to answer it. Information requirements Write the specific questions the recon must answer. Each one should be answerable by observation. Which entry point has the shortest covered approach? How many people are in the building, and on which floors? Do adjacent buildings overlook the approach? Which way do the exterior doors swing? METT-TC check Mission : what the recon is for and when the answers are due Enemy or threat: what's known, what's likely Terrain and weather : routes, cover, light, noise Troops : who goes, who provides security Time : hard time to be back or to report Civilian considerations : who else is in the area Leader's recon and the contingency plan When the leader leaves the team to look at the objective, they issue a five-point contingency plan before departing. The acronym is GOTWA . Element Example G Where the leader is going \"Moving to the hedge line at the NW corner of the objective.\" O Others going along \"Taking #3 for security.\" T Time the leader will be gone \"Back in 30 minutes.\" W What to do if the leader doesn't return \"After 45 minutes, move to the alternate rally point and report.\" A Actions on contact, for both elements \"If we're hit, we break contact east. If you're hit, move to the alternate rally point.\" Actions at the objective: the fan method The team halts at an objective rally point (ORP) out of sight and sound of the objective. A security element holds the ORP while a recon pair moves out on a series of routes, like the ribs of a fan, to observation points (OPs) that see different faces of the objective. They return to the ORP between legs to report and update the plan. Fan method area recon Fan One pair, several legs from the ORP. Slow, but security stays concentrated. Good when the team is small. Converging routes Separate pairs move on different routes and link up at a point past the objective. Faster, but control and fratricide risk go up. Box Pairs move around the objective's perimeter and link up at a far point. Covers the whole outer edge. Needs more people. Observation posts and hide sites Pick a spot that sees the objective but isn't the obvious place to look from. Avoid isolated landmarks, rooftops with nothing else on them, and the only window that faces the target. In buildings, observe from back in the room, away from the window, so your silhouette and any light stay inside. A gap in a curtain or a broken pane is better than an open window. Two people minimum: one observes, one records and provides security. Rotate every 20–30 minutes to keep attention sharp. Keep a running log with times, then build the range card and building sketch from it."},{"id":"terrain","h":"Urban terrain","x":"03 Urban terrain Cities are three-dimensional. Threats and observation can come from below the street, at street level, from inside upper floors, and from rooftops. Plan sectors for every level, not just the one you're standing on. OAKOC, applied to a city block Factor Urban questions to answer O Observation and fields of fire Which windows and roofs see your route? How far can you see down each street? Where are the long, straight lanes a threat could cover? A Avenues of approach Streets, alleys, back yards, through-wall routes, rooftops, and tunnels. Which one keeps you out of the open longest? K Key terrain Tall buildings that overlook the area, intersections, bridges, the building next to the objective. O Obstacles Fences, walls, locked gates, vehicles, rubble, dogs, lighting that exposes you. C Cover and concealment What stops rounds versus what only hides you. Engine blocks and masonry are cover. Car doors, hedges, and drywall are concealment. Construction and protection Type Common examples What it means for you Wood frame Most U.S. houses, apartments up to ~4 floors Interior walls are concealment only. Rounds pass through several rooms. Watch your backstop and know where teammates are. Brick / block masonry Older downtowns, small commercial Exterior walls stop a few hits from small arms but break up under repeated fire. Interior walls vary. Reinforced concrete Parking garages, schools, offices Best protection. Windows and doors become the main openings. Radio signals degrade inside. Steel frame / curtain wall High-rise, big-box retail Glass skin gives no protection. Large open floors with long sightlines and few hard corners."},{"id":"bldg","h":"Building recon","x":"04 Building recon Observe the target building from stand-off before anyone approaches it. Build a sketch from at least two angles, ideally all four sides. What you note here drives the entry point, the route in, and the clearing order. Structure Construction type (see section 03) Number of floors, basement windows, roof access, fire escapes Door swing direction, hinge side, locks, bars, screen doors Likely floor plan from window spacing, chimneys, plumbing vents, meters Where stairs are likely, from stairwell windows offset between floors Surroundings Covered and concealed approaches; dead space you can't see into Windows and rooftops in adjacent buildings that overlook your route Danger areas: streets, alleys, parking lots, open yards Fences, gates, dogs, motion lights, cameras Pattern of life: who comes and goes, when lights change, vehicles present Choosing the entry point Top down when possible. Entering at the roof or upper floor and clearing down puts gravity and stairs on your side. Threats pushed down tend to leave the building, where an outer cordon can deal with them. Avoid the obvious door when another option exists. Front doors are the opening most likely to be watched or blocked. Shortest covered approach wins at ground level, with an overwatch position that can see the entry point and the windows above it. Have an alternate. If the primary is locked, blocked, or compromised, the team moves to the alternate on a known signal without stopping in the open to discuss it."},{"id":"move","h":"Building to building","x":"05 Building to building Open ground between buildings is where a team is most exposed. Split into two buddy pairs: one pair covers while the other moves. Every bound ends in cover with a new overwatch set before the other pair leaves. Plan the routes so the moving pair never passes through the covering pair's sectors. Bounding overwatch across a danger area Moving along buildings Stay 12–18 inches off the wall. Rounds that strike a wall tend to travel along it. Use the shadow side of the street. Avoid silhouetting against lit walls. Keep a low profile at corners and openings; let the lead set the pace. Assign one person high (windows, roofs) and one low (basements, vehicles). Danger areas Linear : streets, alleys, rail lines. Cross at the narrowest point, ideally not at an intersection. Open areas : lots, parks, yards. Bound across, or go around if time allows. Intersections : exposed from four directions. Avoid if possible. Check the far side visually before anyone crosses; cross fast, then set security on the far side. Corners Round a corner the same way you slice a doorway (section 07): back off the corner, then step out in small increments so you see a thin wedge at a time. When two people take a corner together, one can work low (kneeling) and one high (standing) so both can see without one masking the other. Agree on who goes low before you get there."},{"id":"entry","h":"Entry and the stack","x":"06 Entry and the stack The team lines up against the wall beside the entry, on the same side, in the order they'll go in. #1 owns the door. The doorway itself is the fatal funnel : anyone inside watching that opening has it covered, so nobody stops in it, stands in front of it, or waits in line inside it. Stops in the door Flows through Red wedges are what the two corners inside can see through the doorway. Where they overlap is the fatal funnel. The timer counts how long #1 spends in it. Center-fed door Door near the middle of the wall. The room opens to both sides on entry, so #1 and #2 split left and right. Corner-fed door Door within a few feet of a corner. One side is a short wall and a hard corner; the other is the full length of the room. #1 takes the long side, #2 the corner. Doors Hinge side and swing decide where you stack. Stack on the knob side when you can, so the door opens away from the stack and doesn't block #1. Inward-opening doors are pushed open and followed through. Outward-opening doors need someone to pull and hold them, which slows the entry and leaves that person in a bad spot. Try the handle quietly before anything else. An unlocked door is the fastest and quietest option. Locked doors call for a breach. Breaching methods are grouped as mechanical (tools, rams), ballistic , thermal , and explosive ; the last three are specialist tasks that need specific training and authorization. Entry techniques Technique When How it differs Dynamic High threat, speed and surprise available, little need to discriminate Team flows in without pausing at the door. Each member takes a point of domination. Relies on speed, surprise, and violence of action. Deliberate / limited penetration Threat likely, but noncombatants may be present or the room is unknown Clear as much as possible from outside by slicing the pie, then enter only to clear what can't be seen. Slower, more control. Stealth / methodical Surprise is still intact and worth keeping Quiet, slow movement with no talking. Clear by observation, move only when necessary. Speed comes only after compromise. Signals in the stack. Keep them silent and physical: a squeeze on the shoulder or thigh passed forward means \"ready.\" #1 moves when #2 signals ready, or #1 can initiate. Agree on the system before the first rehearsal and use it every time."},{"id":"pie","h":"Slicing the pie","x":"07 Slicing the pie When a single operator or a deliberate team needs to see into a room before entering, they clear it from outside in thin slices. Stand back from the opening and step laterally in a curve. The door frame limits what you see, and it also limits who can see you. Slicing a doorway from the hall Distance from the opening gives you time and narrows your exposure. Stay 6–10 feet off it when the space allows. Eyes, muzzle, and feet move together. Small lateral steps, not leaning. Corners nearest the door wall show up last, if at all. Those are the spots you can't clear from outside, and the reason a team still enters. If you see a threat, you've also been exposed to it. Decide in advance whether you engage, step back, or call it."},{"id":"two","h":"Two-person entry","x":"08 Two-person entry A pair can clear a small room when the rest of the team is holding security elsewhere, or when that's all there is. The pattern is the same as the first half of a four-person entry: #1 goes one way, #2 goes the other, and both work along the walls to points of domination. Two-person entry, center-fed Strengths Fast, simple, leaves two people for security Good for small rooms, closets, bathrooms Limits No one follows to cover the center of a large room A casualty leaves one person alone in the room Rooms over about 15 by 15 feet favor a four-person entry"},{"id":"four","h":"Four-person entry","x":"09 Four-person entry A dynamic entry relies on speed, surprise, and controlled violence of action. Each member flows through the door and takes the direction opposite the person ahead of them, moving along the wall to a point of domination . Sectors overlap and nobody crosses in front of a teammate's muzzle. Center-fed Corner-fed Position Moves Immediate threat area Ends at #1 Through the door, direction of least resistance or as rehearsed The corner on their side of the entry wall Deep along the wall, about 3 feet short of the far corner #2 Opposite of #1 The near corner on their side Along the wall on the opposite side from #1 #3 Opposite of #2 (same side as #1) Center of the room, then their sector Short of #1, about 3 feet in from the entry wall #4 Opposite of #3 (same side as #2) Center of the room, then their sector Short of #2, mirroring #3 Rules that hold every time Don't stop in the doorway. Clear it in one motion. Clear your immediate threat area first, then move along the wall. #3 and #4 stop short so they don't mask #1 and #2. If #1 has to go the \"wrong\" way, #2 adapts and goes opposite. If the room is too small for everyone (\"short room\"), the last members hold outside. Sectors Each member's sector runs from their own wall across the room, ending about one arm's length short of the next teammate. Sectors overlap in the room's depth, but no sector ever points at a teammate. #3 and #4 cover the center. Weapons drop or lift off teammates as they enter a sector, and come back once they're past."},{"id":"hall","h":"Hallways and intersections","x":"10 Hallways and intersections Hallways are long, narrow danger areas with doors along both sides. Keep spacing, keep every opening covered, and move steadily rather than fast. Hallway: post an open door, then clear it Hallway formations Serpentine (single file, staggered): narrow halls. #1 covers forward, #2 high and forward, #3 and #4 alternate sides, last man covers rear. Two-by-two : wide halls. Lead pair on opposite walls, staggered, both covering forward. #3 follows with muzzle off the lead; #4 covers the rear. Nobody covers across a teammate's lane. Doors in the hall The member on the same wall as an open door posts at the door edge and covers into the room. That sector points into the room, so teammates can pass in the hall without crossing it. Don't cover a door from across the hall. Anyone who walks past ends up in that sector. Clear the room or leave a member posted on it. Closed doors you aren't clearing get marked and reported. Don't leave uncleared, uncovered doors behind the team. T-intersection T-intersections #1 and #2 each take the branch on their own side and end back to back, so their sectors point away from each other. Don't cross over: crossing puts each lead's sector through the other's path. #3 moves up behind with muzzle down, #4 stays on rear security. The leader picks the direction; the other branch stays covered until the team has passed. L-turns Slice the corner from the outside wall. The lead uses the corner as cover and the team follows on the same side. Avoid bunching up at the corner while the lead works it."},{"id":"stairs","h":"Stairs and multi-floor","x":"11 Stairs and multi-floor Move up in a file with the lead covering the next landing and the member behind covering higher, across the open side of the stairwell. Treat each landing as a corner: slice it before committing. Clear stairs going up whenever possible. Coming down, your feet are exposed before your eyes can see the floor below. Secure the top of the stairs as a foothold before the team spreads out on the new floor, and leave someone holding the stairwell. On each floor, clear outward from the foothold room by room and report the floor clear before moving up."},{"id":"consol","h":"Consolidation and commands","x":"12 Consolidation and commands After each room Each member calls their sector; #1 or the leader calls \"room clear.\" Search for hidden spaces, closets, other doors, and connecting openings. Mark the room per SOP: chalk, tape, chem light, or marker panel. Mark the door, not the inside wall. Report to the leader: room cleared, casualties, anything found. After the building Establish security on likely approaches and overlooking positions. Redistribute ammunition and equipment; account for every member. Treat casualties and request evacuation. Report building clear and mark it so other elements can see it from the street. Prepare to continue to the next building or hold. Verbal commands Short, loud, and identical every time. Repeat a command back so the sender knows it was heard. These are common examples; your SOP sets the exact words. Command Meaning \"Up\" I'm in position and ready to move \"Clear\" My sector is clear \"Room clear\" All sectors in this room are clear \"Short room\" Room is too small for the full team; remaining members hold outside \"Door left / right\" An uncleared opening in my sector \"Coming out\" I'm about to exit through a covered door. Wait for the reply \"Come out\" Exit is clear, you're recognized \"Coming in\" I'm entering a room friendlies hold \"Man down\" Casualty; the nearest member takes over that sector \"Reloading\" / \"Cover\" My weapon is out of action; someone take my sector \"Last man\" Called by the trail member so the team knows the rear is with them"},{"id":"vassess","h":"Vehicle route assessment","x":"13 Vehicle route assessment A route assessment breaks the drive into segments and rates each one for how much it restricts you. You're looking for the places where you'll be forced to slow down, stop, or follow a single predictable path, since those are where trouble is easiest to set up. What to look for Factor What to note Why it matters Choke points Bridges, tunnels, narrow streets, construction, rail crossings, gated entrances Single path, no way around, often forced to slow Forced stops Signal lights, stop signs, toll booths, checkpoints, school zones Predictable location where a vehicle sits still Overlooking terrain Overpasses, high ground, buildings with a view of the road, wood lines close to the shoulder Places someone can observe or act from with an advantage Canalizing terrain Road cuts, embankments, walls, guard rails, medians Can't leave the road to turn around or go around Limited visibility Blind curves, hill crests, heavy vegetation, poor lighting Less warning; shorter decision time Roadside clutter Debris, parked vehicles, culverts, trash piles, new objects Hides things and people; changes are an indicator Turnaround points Wide shoulders, parking lots, side streets, median crossings Where you can get out if the road ahead is blocked Safe havens Police and fire stations, hospitals, military facilities, busy guarded lots Where you go if you're followed or hurt Communications Cell and radio dead spots Where you can't report or call for help Traffic by time Rush hour, school release, events, market days Gridlock removes your options Running the assessment Drive the route at the same time of day and day of week you'll use it, in a vehicle that blends in. Record mileage and drive time for each segment, and mark each hazard by mile marker or landmark. Photograph or video choke points and turnaround options. Rate each segment LOW MODERATE HIGH and plan an alternate around every high-risk segment. Establish the baseline Know what normal looks like on this route so you notice changes. Absence of the normal : an empty street that's usually busy, closed shops at midday, no kids at a school bus stop. Presence of the abnormal : new debris or a parked vehicle where there never is one, people watching traffic instead of going about their business, signals between people."},{"id":"vplan","h":"Vehicle route planning","x":"14 Vehicle route planning The plan turns the assessment into a route set, timings, reporting points, and actions everyone has rehearsed before the wheels turn. PACE routes Route Use P Primary Best balance of time and risk under normal conditions A Alternate Bypasses the primary's high-risk segments; used if the primary is blocked or compromised C Contingency Different direction of approach entirely; used if both primary and alternate are unusable E Emergency Shortest path to the nearest safe haven or medical facility from any point on the route Control measures Start point / release point where the move begins and ends Checkpoints at known landmarks for position reports Rally points along the route where separated vehicles regroup Decision points before each high-risk segment, where you choose primary or alternate Safe havens marked with the route to each Timing and pattern Vary departure times and routes. Predictability is the biggest vulnerability on a recurring move. Plan to arrive with daylight to spare when possible. Avoid peak traffic through high-risk segments. Set a \"no later than\" time at each checkpoint. A missed report triggers the lost-contact plan. Interval and order of march In towns , keep vehicles close enough that outside traffic can't cut between them, typically a few car lengths. On open road , open the interval so one ambush or one blast can't catch two vehicles at once, while staying in sight and radio range. Put the vehicle with the best observation and communications in the lead. The leader rides where they can see and control, usually not in the lead vehicle. The trail vehicle watches the rear and reports anything that follows. Movement brief checklist Route Strip map, PACE routes, checkpoints, rally points, safe havens, decision points, timings. Actions Halts, breakdowns, disabled vehicle, separation, contact front/left/right/rear, blocked route, bail-out. Comms Frequencies or channels, call signs, check-in times, lost-comms plan, code words. Medical Who carries the kit, casualty collection point, nearest hospitals on each route. Vehicles Order of march, seat assignments, sectors, load plan, fuel, spares, recovery gear. Contingency Who takes over if the leader is down, what triggers a route change, where to go if followed. Vehicle prep Fuel tank full at the start; refuel before it gets to half. Tires, spare, jack, recovery strap, and jumper cables checked. Medical kit within reach of the seats, not buried in cargo. Paper map of the route as a backup to phones and GPS. Loose gear secured so it doesn't become a projectile in a crash or hard turn."},{"id":"vcrew","h":"In-vehicle movement and team placement","x":"15 In-vehicle movement and team placement Every seat has a job and a sector. Sectors divide the space around the vehicle so every direction is watched and no one's sector runs across another occupant. Seat Primary job Sector On contact Driver Drive. Nothing else. Front, front-left, mirrors Keeps the vehicle moving and out of the kill zone; follows the VC's direction Vehicle cmdr Navigation, comms, decisions Front, front-right Calls direction, distance, and action (\"contact left, push through\" or \"bail right\") Rear left Observation Left side to left rear Reports, then exits away from contact on the bail-out call Rear right Observation, often medic or comms backup Right side to right rear Same as rear left; first out on a right-side bail Riding Seatbelts on. A crash is the most likely way to get hurt in a vehicle. Weapons, if carried, stay muzzle down toward the floor or outboard, never toward another seat. Doors locked. Bags, kits, and radios secured so they don't fly. Talk is short and about the route. Call what you see by clock direction: \"Vehicle stopped, two o'clock, 200 feet.\" In traffic Tires and pavement. At a stop, leave enough space that you can see the rear tires of the vehicle ahead touching the road. That leaves room to pull out around it. Take the lane with the most escape options, usually not the curb lane in town. At lights, time your approach to keep rolling when you can. In a multi-vehicle move, the trail vehicle can take the lane beside the lead at a stop to prevent a box-in. Bail-out drill The vehicle commander calls direction and exit side: \"Contact left, bail right.\" Unbuckle, open the door away from contact, exit low. Occupants on the contact side cross the cab and exit with the others. Nobody steps out into the fire. Go to the engine block and wheels, not the doors (section 17). Sound off by seat or number so the commander knows everyone is out."},{"id":"vavoid","h":"Avoidance","x":"16 Avoidance Most bad outcomes on a route are avoidable by not being predictable, noticing early, keeping space, and acting decisively. Turning around early is almost always cheaper than reacting late. Recognizing a blocked route and turning away Indicators What you see Possible meaning Road blocked with no one working the scene The block is the point, not an accident People standing off the road watching traffic, with no reason to be there Observers or a trigger team Normal activity suddenly stops or the area empties Locals know something is about to happen The same vehicle behind you across several turns Surveillance A vehicle slows in front of you while another closes behind Box-in New objects on the shoulder or in a culvert on a road you drive regularly Something placed for your route At choke points Scan the choke point and the far side before you enter it. Slow early rather than stop late. A rolling vehicle has more options than a stopped one. Leave an escape gap ahead and to one side. In a multi-vehicle move, the trail vehicle holds short until the lead is through. Know your turnaround point before you commit to the segment. If you think you're followed Don't go home or to your destination. Confirm with a few deliberate, normal-looking turns. A vehicle that stays with you through all of them is a strong indicator. Report it, then drive to a safe haven: a police or fire station, or a busy staffed location. Note plate, make, model, color, occupants, and time. Don't confront. Turning away Pick your decision points and turnaround options during planning, so the choice on the road is just which one to use. In a two-vehicle move, the trail vehicle turns first and becomes the lead. Report what you saw and where as soon as you're clear."},{"id":"vengage","h":"Vehicle route engagements","x":"17 Vehicle route engagements If contact can't be avoided, the vehicle is your best tool for getting out of it. The core principle is to get off the X: leave the place where you were attacked as fast as the vehicle allows. Drive through the kill zone Situation Action Contact while mobile, vehicle can move Drive through. Don't stop in the kill zone. Rally past it. Vehicles short of the kill zone Don't drive in. Stop, report, and take the alternate to the rally point. Support only if you can do it without entering. Route blocked ahead in contact Reverse out or turn around on the VC's call. Go back the way you came to the last known safe point. Vehicle disabled, others mobile Another vehicle pushes or recovers it if possible, or the crew bails and cross-loads into a working vehicle. Vehicle disabled, no help Bail out away from contact, get to cover, then move to hard cover. Suspicious object on the route Don't approach it. Stop well short, back off, report the location, and take the alternate. Cover at the vehicle Disabled vehicle: bail out, cover, bound to hard cover Get behind the engine block first. Behind the wheels is second best. Keep your feet behind the wheels too; rounds skip under vehicles. Stay a step or two back from the vehicle rather than pressed against it. It gives you room to move and keeps you out of rounds that skim along the body. The vehicle is temporary cover. Fuel, glass, and a stationary target make it a poor place to stay. Move to hard cover as soon as you can. Pairs bound in fire-and-movement. Movers' routes stay out of the covering pair's sectors. Security at halts Herringbone halt Herringbone Used on a road when the halt is short or the route must stay open. Vehicles angle off alternately left and right, crews facing outward. A vehicle can still pass down the center. Coil Used for longer halts off the road. Vehicles form a circle with fronts or weapons outward and soft vehicles and people in the center. Takes more space and time to set. 5 and 25 checks Before anyone gets out at a halt, the crew searches about 15 feet around the vehicle from inside, looking for anything disturbed or out of place. Then, on foot, they check out to about 80 feet. Only after the area is checked does the rest of the team dismount. The names come from the doctrinal 5- and 25-meter distances. After contact Rally past the kill zone or at the planned rally point. Set security with sectors outward. Account for every person and vehicle. Treat casualties and request evacuation. Report: location, what happened, casualties, vehicle status. Check vehicles for damage: tires, fluids, glass, steering. Decide: continue, alternate route, or return."},{"id":"errors","h":"Common errors","x":"18 Common errors Error Why it matters Fix Stopping in the doorway The doorway is the most covered spot in the room and blocks everyone behind Rehearse the first two steps past the threshold until it's automatic Following the person ahead Two people on one side leaves a whole side uncovered Always go opposite the person ahead of you Going too deep with #3 and #4 They mask #1 and #2 and cross muzzles Stop short; mark points of domination on the floor in training Hugging walls Skipping rounds, snagging gear, no room to pivot Keep 12–18 inches off Looking past the immediate threat area The close corner is where the fastest threat is Corner first, then deep Leaving doors behind Threats can move in behind the team Cover, clear, or mark every opening you pass Slow recon reports Information goes stale; the leader decides on old data Report as you observe; send a full SALUTE once you're secure Crossing fields of fire A sector that runs across a teammate puts them in the line of fire Sectors point outward and away from each other; muzzle comes off anyone who moves into yours Covering a door from across the hall Everyone who passes walks through that sector Post on the door edge on the same wall, sector into the room Stopping in a vehicle kill zone A stationary vehicle is the easiest target there is Drive through if the vehicle moves; rally past the kill zone Getting boxed in at a stop No way out if something happens Leave room to see the rear tires of the vehicle ahead; pick the lane with options Predictable routes and times Makes it easy to plan against you Rotate PACE routes and vary departure times Hiding behind car doors Doors and glass stop little Engine block first, wheels second, then move to hard cover Ignoring overpenetration Rounds go through interior walls toward teammates or bystanders Know the construction type and teammates' positions before you engage"},{"id":"train","h":"Training progression","x":"19 Training progression Crawl Walk-through Tape a floor plan on the ground or use an empty building. Walk each position slowly, call each step out loud, and mark points of domination. No weapons or training weapons only. Walk Dry rehearsal Full speed with training weapons or inert replicas. Add doors, furniture, a second room, and role players. Add low light once daylight is clean. Run Force on force or live Only in an approved facility, with qualified range staff, a written safety plan, and a team that has passed the earlier stages. Marking cartridges before live fire. Run each drill with every member in every position. Anyone may end up #1. Change the floor plan often. A team that knows the shoot house is rehearsing the house, not the drill. After-action review after every iteration: what was supposed to happen, what happened, why, what to sustain, what to fix. Recon training pairs well with the land navigation and observation drills you can run anywhere: KIM games, range estimation, and sketching a building from across the street."}]},{"u":"defensive-bolt-rifle.html","t":"Defensive Bolt Rifle","s":[{"id":"","h":"Defensive Bolt Rifle","x":"Firearm training · Defensive Updated Nov 27, 2026 Defensive Bolt Rifle Many households own one long gun, and it's a deer rifle. A bolt action is accurate, powerful, reliable, and legal almost everywhere, and on a rural property it may be the only rifle that can reach far enough. It's also slow to reload, loud indoors, and usually wears a scope set up for 200 yards. This page covers how to make a bolt gun work as a defensive tool, and where it doesn't. Builds on the Bolt Rifle page for the action, scope, and fundamentals, and on the Defensive Handgun page for the legal framework. This is training information, not legal advice. Right-handed shooter assumed."},{"id":"law","h":"Legal and ethical limits","x":"01 Legal and ethical limits Deadly force is justified only when you reasonably believe it's necessary to prevent imminent death or serious bodily harm to yourself or another innocent person. The type of gun doesn't change the standard. Distance does change how a shooting is judged: an attacker 200 yards away has to have the ability, the opportunity, and the intent to harm someone at that moment , and that is a much harder case to make than one at 7 yards. Ability The attacker has the means to kill or cause serious injury. Opportunity They can use it now, from where they are. Jeopardy Their actions show intent. A reasonable person would believe the threat is real. Full-power hunting cartridges such as the .308 and .30-06 pass through interior walls, exterior walls, and people, and travel miles. Indoors they are far more likely to over-penetrate than a 5.56 carbine with defensive ammunition. Protecting property or livestock with deadly force is governed by separate and narrower laws; learn your state's rules before you need them."},{"id":"role","h":"Where a bolt gun fits","x":"02 Where a bolt gun fits Strengths Accuracy and reach: a hit on a threat at 150–300 yd across open ground. Power: effective against large predators and dangerous animals. Simplicity and reliability; easy to own legally almost anywhere. Often already in the house, with a scope already zeroed. Limits Slow follow-up shots and slow reloads: 3 to 5 rounds before reloading one at a time. A magnified scope is slow and blurry at room distances. Muzzle blast indoors is severe. Without hearing protection, expect hearing damage. Long and heavy in hallways and vehicles; over-penetration risk inside. The bolt gun is the right tool on open ground: farms, ranches, rural homes with long driveways, and protecting livestock. Inside a house, a handgun, shotgun, or carbine is usually a better choice if you have one. If the bolt gun is what you have, the rest of this page is about getting the most out of it."},{"id":"setup","h":"Setup","x":"03 Setup Item Recommendation Why Optic Low-power variable (1–6×, 2–7×, or 3–9× kept at 3×) or iron sights Wide field of view up close, magnification when you need it. Leave it set at the lowest power. Sling Two-point adjustable sling Carries the rifle hands-free and doubles as a shooting aid. Light Weapon light on a forend rail or clamp mount You must identify what you're shooting at. A scope gathers light but doesn't create it. Magazine Detachable box if your rifle accepts one, plus a spare Much faster to reload than an internal magazine. Ammunition Bonded or controlled-expansion hunting bullets Expanding bullets transfer energy and are less likely to exit than full metal jacket. Ammo on the gun Buttstock cartridge cuff or sidesaddle-style carrier Spare rounds that go wherever the rifle goes. Suppressor Where legal; see Modifications Reduces indoor blast and makes the rifle easier to shoot well."},{"id":"ready","h":"Storage and readiness","x":"04 Storage and readiness A common ready condition for a bolt rifle is magazine loaded, chamber empty, bolt closed on the empty chamber, safety on . Bringing it into action is one cycle of the bolt. Many states have child-access and safe-storage laws; a quick-access safe or rack lock meets most of them without making the rifle slow to reach. Check the condition every time you pick up the rifle, and every time you put it away. Don't store it with the firing pin cocked and the safety as the only barrier for long periods. Keep a light and hearing protection with it."},{"id":"close","h":"Close range with a scope","x":"05 Close range with a scope Lowest magnification, both eyes open At 3× and closer than 25 yards, keep both eyes open: the dominant eye sees the reticle, the other sees the room. At high magnification, a target at 10 yards fills the scope with a blur and you lose everything around it. Mechanical offset A scope sits 1.5–2 inches above the bore. Inside about 25 yards the bullet strikes that far below the reticle. For a precise shot up close, hold high by the scope height. On a low-power variable, the top of the reticle post or the center dot held slightly high works. Find your offset on paper: shoot at 3, 7, 15, and 25 yards and measure each impact's distance below the aim point. The Defensive Rifle page has an animation of the same effect on a carbine."},{"id":"cycle","h":"Running the bolt under stress","x":"06 Running the bolt under stress Fire, keep the rifle in the shoulder, and cycle immediately. Make it automatic: shot, bolt, reticle. A defensive shooter who waits to see whether a shot worked before cycling is slow when it didn't. Run the bolt hard and fully every time. Stress shortens strokes, and short strokes cause most bolt-gun stoppages. Assess after the follow-up is ready, not before. The rifle is loaded while you decide whether to fire again. Count your shots. A 4-round magazine runs dry fast. Practice the cycle dry at home with snap caps until the motion is identical every time. Speed comes from a smooth path, not from rushing."},{"id":"feed","h":"Keeping it fed","x":"07 Keeping it fed Top off between shots With an internal magazine, the fastest way to keep a round ready is to feed one at a time: open the bolt, drop a round into the open action or press it into the magazine, close the bolt. \"Shoot one, load one\" keeps the rifle from ever running empty. Detachable magazine Swap magazines when there's a pause, not only when empty. Keep the spare in the same place every time, and practice the swap without looking."},{"id":"mal","h":"Malfunctions","x":"08 Malfunctions Symptom Likely cause Fix Click, no bang Empty chamber, or a dud Cycle the bolt and fire again. On a dud, wait a few seconds with the muzzle downrange in training; in a fight, cycle and continue. Bolt won't close Short-stroke double feed (push-feed actions) or a damaged round Open fully, clear the action and magazine top, feed a fresh round. Fired case stays in the chamber Short stroke or failed extraction Run the bolt again, hard. If it still won't extract, transition to another weapon. Magazine won't seat Wrong magazine, or rounds loaded backward Single-feed through the action."},{"id":"sidearm","h":"The sidearm with a bolt gun","x":"09 The sidearm with a bolt gun A bolt gun holds three to five rounds and reloads slowly, so a handgun on your hip matters even more than with a semi-auto. Its two jobs are the same, but the second depends on distance. 1 · Fighting back to the long gun On a property, the rifle is usually in the house or the truck when something happens. The handgun is what you carry while you work. Use it to get to the rifle, then switch. 2 · Covering a malfunction or empty gun Inside handgun range, switching to the sidearm is faster than reloading or clearing the bolt gun under pressure. Past about 25 yards the pistol isn't the answer: get behind cover and reload the rifle instead. Problem Time to fix it Time to switch to the sidearm Click (dud or empty chamber) About 1 s to cycle the bolt 1.5–2.5 s to the first shot Empty, internal magazine 2–3 s per round loaded 1.5–2.5 s Empty, detachable magazine 3–4 s 1.5–2.5 s Short-stroke double feed 3–6 s 1.5–2.5 s Stuck case Can't be fixed during the fight 1.5–2.5 s Typical times for a trained shooter. Stress slows every column, and it slows the fine-motor fixes the most. When to switch The threat is inside handgun range and the rifle is empty or stopped. You're in the open with no time to reload. When to fix it instead The threat is at a distance: get behind cover and reload the rifle. A single round will do: drop it into the action and close the bolt. Once the threat is stopped or you reach cover: reload the rifle and go back to it. Making the switch A sling lets the rifle hang instead of being dropped. Carry the handgun where you can reach it with the rifle slung. Practice dry until an empty rifle at close range triggers the draw without thinking. At distance the bolt gun is the answer; up close the sidearm keeps you in the fight until you can reload it."},{"id":"pos","h":"Positions, cover, and concealment","x":"10 Positions, cover, and concealment Defensive shooting with a bolt gun outdoors is usually done from whatever support is nearest: kneeling or sitting with a sling, over a vehicle hood, a fence post, a porch rail, or a doorframe. Learn to get into kneeling and sitting in under 3 seconds. Cover has to stop rifle fire: an engine block, a large tree, earth, or masonry. Car doors, interior walls, and fences generally don't. Cover Something solid enough to stop incoming fire: an engine block, a large tree, earth, thick masonry. Fight, reload, and fix malfunctions from behind it. It's temporary: repeated hits wear it down, and the threat can move to a new angle. Concealment Something that hides you but won't stop a bullet: drywall, doors, car doors, hedges, furniture, darkness, smoke. You can't be aimed at if you can't be seen, so use it to move, reposition, or break contact. Expect fire through it at where you were last seen. Material Handgun rounds Rifle rounds Interior walls (drywall) Concealment Rounds pass through several Concealment Wood doors, fences, furniture Concealment Concealment Car doors, body panels, glass Concealment Some rounds deflect; don't count on it Concealment Engine block, wheel and axle Cover Cover for most rounds Brick wall Cover for several hits Short-term Breaks down under repeated hits Hollow concrete block Cover for several hits Short-term Poured concrete, solid masonry Cover Cover for a while Large tree trunk Cover Depends on diameter and wood Utility pole Cover for a narrow target Partial Earth: berms, ditches, ground Cover Cover with 1–2 ft of dirt General guidance, not test data. Construction, angle, and the specific round all change the result. When in doubt, treat it as concealment. Cover depends on what's being fired at you, not on what you're carrying. Cover on open ground Earth is the best cover outdoors: a ditch, a berm, a dip in the ground. Prone behind a fold of dirt beats standing behind a fence. Most farm structures are concealment: sheet-metal barns, wood sheds, fences, and hay all pass rifle rounds. Hay bales stacked deep slow handgun rounds but not reliably rifle rounds. Vehicles: engine block and wheels only. Keep your feet behind a wheel. Don't hug what you hide behind. Rounds skip along hard surfaces a few inches off them."},{"id":"id","h":"Distance and identification","x":"11 Distance and identification Identify before you shoot: who it is, what they're holding, and what's behind them. At distance that takes optics. Carry binoculars or a monocular for observation. Watching people through a rifle scope means pointing a loaded rifle at them. Know your distances in advance: pace or laser-range your fence lines, outbuildings, and the road so you know your holds before you need them. A distant person who is armed is not automatically a threat to you. Distance often makes retreating, calling for help, or staying behind cover the better option."},{"id":"light","h":"Low light","x":"12 Low light A good scope at dawn or dusk shows more than your eyes do, but you still have to identify the target. A weapon light lets you do that; an illuminated reticle helps you see the crosshair against a dark target. Thermal and night vision devices identify heat or light, not intent: they show that something is there, not whether it's a threat."},{"id":"drills","h":"Drills","x":"13 Drills Drill Setup Standard Ready to shot Rifle in condition, chamber empty. Cycle and fire one shot on an 8-inch circle at 25 yd 3.0 s Offset check One shot each on 1-inch dots at 3, 7, 15, and 25 yd Know where each one lands Cycle cadence Three shots on an 8-inch circle at 50 yd from the shoulder 6.0 s, all hits Shoot one, load one Fire, load one round, fire, five times Under 30 s; rifle never empty Field position From standing, get into kneeling or sitting and hit a 10-inch plate at 150 yd 8.0 s Mad minute The pre-1914 British Army standard: 15 aimed hits on a 12-inch round target at 300 yd in 60 seconds, with a 10-round Lee-Enfield and stripper clips. A practical modern version uses the same target at 100 yd 10 hits in 60 s with a 4–5 round magazine is a strong result"},{"id":"after","h":"After an incident","x":"14 After an incident Make the rifle safe when the threat has ended, call 911, report that you were involved in a shooting and need police and medical help, and describe yourself so arriving officers know who you are. Put the rifle down before police arrive. The Defensive Handgun page covers the aftermath in more detail."},{"id":"errors","h":"Common errors","x":"15 Common errors Leaving the scope on high magnification. Forgetting mechanical offset up close. Short-stroking the bolt under stress. Using full metal jacket ammunition, which penetrates further and stops less. Using the scope to watch people instead of binoculars. Firing at a distance where the threat is no longer imminent. Firing indoors without accounting for over-penetration and hearing loss."}]},{"u":"defensive-handgun.html","t":"Defensive Handgun Shooting","s":[{"id":"","h":"Defensive Handgun Shooting","x":"Firearm training · Defensive Updated Nov 27, 2026 Defensive Handgun Shooting Carrying and using a handgun for self-defense: the legal framework, awareness, carry gear, the reactionary gap, drawing from concealment, moving off the line, close-contact shooting, multiple threats, low light, and what to do afterward. Built on common defensive handgun curricula, the Tueller drill, and general U.S. self-defense law, with Missouri's statute linked as an example. This is training information, not legal advice. Laws differ by state; learn yours from a qualified attorney or instructor. Right-handed shooter assumed."},{"id":"law","h":"Legal framework","x":"01 Legal framework In most U.S. jurisdictions, deadly force is justified only when you reasonably believe it's immediately necessary to prevent death or serious bodily harm to yourself or another innocent person. \"Reasonably\" is judged by what an ordinary, prudent person would believe in your position, knowing what you knew. Ability The attacker has the means to kill or seriously injure: a weapon, or a large disparity of force (size, numbers, skill). Opportunity They can use that ability now: within range of the weapon or close enough to reach you. Jeopardy Their actions show intent: words plus actions that would make a reasonable person believe the attack is coming. Preclusion / avoidance Some states require retreat if you can do so in complete safety; many don't (\"stand your ground\"). Even where not required, avoiding the fight is always the best outcome. Example: Missouri Missouri's § 563.031 allows deadly force when you reasonably believe it's necessary to protect against death, serious physical injury, or a forcible felony. It removes the duty to retreat from your dwelling, residence, vehicle, private property you own or lease, and any place you have a right to be. It also covers someone who unlawfully enters, or tries to enter, your dwelling, residence, or vehicle. Read the statute itself and get advice from a Missouri attorney; the details and exceptions matter. Force must stop when the threat stops. Shots fired after the threat has ended are judged separately. Being the initial aggressor generally removes the self-defense justification. Brandishing or displaying a firearm when deadly force isn't justified can itself be a crime."},{"id":"aware","h":"Awareness and decision","x":"02 Awareness and decision Pre-attack indicators Someone moving to intercept your path or closing distance for no reason Hands hidden, or repeatedly touching the waistband Looking around for witnesses, or coordinating glances with others \"Target glancing\": looking at you, then away, then back Sudden change in behavior, or a pretext question to get close (\"got the time?\") Options before the gun Avoid : cross the street, change direction, don't enter. Escape : leave as soon as something feels wrong. Verbal : \"Stop! Stay back!\" with a hand up. It establishes you as the one who tried to avoid the fight. Distance and barriers : put a car or counter between you."},{"id":"gear","h":"Carry gear","x":"03 Carry gear Position Strengths Drawbacks Appendix (AIWB) Fastest from concealment; easy to protect the gun; works seated and in a car Muzzle points toward the body while holstering; requires strict holstering discipline Strong side (3–4 o'clock) Natural draw; muzzle never crosses the body Harder to conceal; grip can print when bending Behind the hip (4–5) Conceals well Slower; harder to protect from a grab; awkward seated Cross-draw Accessible seated or driving Muzzle sweeps people to your side on the draw Off-body (bag) Works with any clothing Slow; bag can be taken or left behind. Use a dedicated compartment. Holster requirements Fully covers the trigger guard Rigid (Kydex or reinforced leather) so the mouth stays open for one-handed holstering Holds the gun securely through running, jumping, and turning upside down Attaches firmly so it comes out with the belt, not with the gun Everything else A stiff gun belt; regular belts sag and shift. Spare magazine: more for fixing malfunctions than for capacity. A small handheld light. A tourniquet and basic trauma kit. You're more likely to need it for someone else than the gun."},{"id":"gap","h":"The reactionary gap","x":"04 The reactionary gap In 1983 Sgt. Dennis Tueller found that an average adult could cover 21 feet in about 1.5 seconds, roughly the time it took officers to draw and fire from a duty holster. The lesson isn't that 21 feet is a magic number. It's that a person with a contact weapon can be on you before you can shoot, unless you already have distance, a head start, or are moving. 21 feet, two ways Reaction time alone (seeing, deciding, starting to move) is about 0.25–0.5 s for a prepared person, longer if surprised. A draw from concealment to a first hit is typically 1.5–2.0 s for a trained shooter. Moving off the attacker's line while drawing makes them change direction, which buys time and can avoid the first contact."},{"id":"draw","h":"Drawing from concealment","x":"05 Drawing from concealment Open-front garment: sweep, grip, draw Garment Clearing technique Open-front (jacket, open shirt) Strong hand sweeps the garment back, past the holster, and drives down onto the grip in one motion. Weight in the pocket helps it stay back. Closed-front (T-shirt, sweater), strong side Support hand grabs the shirt and pulls it up high toward the chest; strong hand gets the grip. Closed-front, appendix One or both hands pull the shirt up hard; strong hand takes the grip. A support-hand \"pec pull\" lifts the shirt to the collarbone. Practice the draw with the clothes you actually wear, in dry practice first. Re-holster slowly, sweeping the garment out of the way and looking the gun into the holster."},{"id":"contact","h":"Close-contact shooting","x":"06 Close-contact shooting When an attacker is at arm's length, extending the gun puts it within their reach. Shooting from a retention position, gun close to the body, keeps it away from their hands. Use the support hand to strike, push, or shield, then shoot from retention. Step back or to the side to create space as you shoot, then extend to a full grip. Pressing the muzzle against an attacker can push some semi-autos out of battery; keep a little space."},{"id":"move","h":"Moving off the line","x":"07 Moving off the line Why move An attacker aims (or charges) at where you are. Moving laterally or diagonally makes them adjust, which costs them time. Moving toward cover also improves what happens next. How First step is big and explosive, to the side and slightly forward or back. Draw while moving; shoot when the gun is up and the shot is available. Stopping to shoot is often more accurate than shooting on the move. Moving straight backward is slow and risks tripping. Go diagonal."},{"id":"cover","h":"Cover and concealment","x":"08 Cover and concealment Cover stops the rounds being fired at you. Concealment only hides you. Both are useful, but they aren't interchangeable. Whether something is cover also depends on the weapon: a wall that stops a 9mm may not stop a rifle. Cover Something solid enough to stop incoming fire: an engine block, a large tree, earth, thick masonry. Use it to fight from, reload, fix a malfunction, or wait for help. It's temporary: repeated hits wear it down, and an attacker can move to a new angle. Concealment Something that keeps you from being seen but won't stop a bullet: drywall, doors, car doors, hedges, furniture, darkness, smoke. It still helps, because you can't be aimed at if you can't be seen. Use it to move, reposition, or break contact. Expect someone to fire through it at where they last saw you. Material Handgun rounds Rifle rounds Interior walls (drywall) Concealment Rounds pass through several Concealment Wood doors, fences, furniture Concealment Concealment Car doors, body panels, glass Concealment Some rounds deflect; don't count on it Concealment Engine block, wheel and axle Cover Cover for most rounds Brick wall Cover for several hits Short-term Breaks down under repeated hits Hollow concrete block Cover for several hits Short-term Poured concrete, solid masonry Cover Cover for a while Large tree trunk Cover Depends on diameter and wood Utility pole Cover for a narrow target Partial Earth: berms, ditches, ground Cover Cover with 1–2 ft of dirt General guidance, not test data. Construction, angle, and the specific round all change the result. When in doubt, treat it as concealment. Using cover Stand back from it. A few feet back lets you see more around the edge with less of your body showing, and keeps your muzzle from sticking out past the edge. Don't hug walls or cars. A bullet that hits a hard surface at a shallow angle tends to skip along it a few inches off the surface, straight into someone pressed against it. Behind a vehicle, get behind the engine or a wheel. The rest of the car is concealment. Rounds fired under the car can hit your feet, so keep them behind a wheel. Show as little as possible. Shoot around the side rather than over the top, and from a low position when the cover is low. Know where you'll go next before you leave. Move in short rushes between cover, and use concealment to move without being seen."},{"id":"sidearm","h":"The sidearm when you have a long gun","x":"09 The sidearm when you have a long gun If a rifle or shotgun is available, it's the primary weapon: easier to hit with, more powerful, and holds more rounds. In a longer fight, the handgun has two jobs. 1 · Fighting back to the long gun Trouble rarely starts with the rifle in your hands. The handgun is what you have when you're caught away from it: in the yard, at the car, in another room. Use it to survive long enough to get to the rifle or shotgun, then switch. 2 · Covering a malfunction or empty long gun When the long gun stops working at close range, switching to the sidearm is faster than fixing the long gun under pressure. Clearing a double feed, reloading a shotgun tube, or dealing with a failure to fire takes fine motor skills that stress takes away first. A draw stays a gross-motor movement you've repeated thousands of times. Problem with the long gun Time to fix it Time to switch to the sidearm Failure to fire (click) 1.5–2.5 s tap-rack, if it works 1.5–2.5 s to the first shot Empty rifle 2.5–4 s magazine change 1.5–2.5 s Double feed 6–10 s, often more 1.5–2.5 s Empty shotgun 2–3 s to port-load one shell; about 1.5 s per shell into the tube 1.5–2.5 s Stuck case or broken part Can't be fixed during the fight 1.5–2.5 s Typical times for a trained shooter. Stress slows every column, and it slows the fine-motor fixes the most. When to switch The threat is close, inside handgun range, and you need rounds now. You're in the open with no time to fix the long gun. The problem is anything worse than a tap-rack. When to fix the long gun instead You're behind cover, where you have time. That's what cover is for. The threat is far enough away that handgun hits are uncertain. After the switch, once the threat is stopped or you reach cover: fix the long gun and go back to it. Making the switch A sling makes it possible: the rifle hangs out of the way instead of being dropped. Without one, set the long gun down in a direction you can keep track of. Support hand pushes the long gun down and aside; firing hand draws as normal. Don't look at the long gun. Wear the holster where you can reach it with the long gun slung and body armor on. Practice it dry with an unloaded rifle on a sling until the click triggers the draw without thinking. See the Defensive Rifle page for an animation of the timing and the Rifle to pistol transition drill. The sidearm doesn't usually win a fight a long gun can win. It keeps you in it long enough to get back to the long gun."},{"id":"assess","h":"Assess and scan","x":"10 Assess and scan After shooting, the danger isn't automatically over. The attacker may still be a threat, have a partner, or there may be bystanders in your line of fire. Assessment is deliberate. After the shots Assess the threat over the sights, then just below them so you can see hands and weapon. Is the threat still able and trying to hurt you? Breathe and break tunnel vision. Stress narrows your field of view. Scan left, right, and behind, turning your head and body. Look for other threats, a partner, and bystanders. Move to cover or away from the scene if it's safe to do so. Top off or reload if you can, then consider holstering only when the situation is truly over."},{"id":"multi","h":"Multiple threats","x":"11 Multiple threats Engage the most dangerous threat first, which is usually the nearest one, or the one with a gun pointed at you. Eyes move to the next target first, and the gun follows the eyes. Eyes lead, gun follows Move so the threats line up or so one blocks the others. Don't overswing: stop the gun on the next target, settle, then press. After the last threat, come back across all of them to assess. The first one may still be active."},{"id":"onehand","h":"One-handed shooting","x":"12 One-handed shooting Strong hand only Grip as high and tight as possible; lock the wrist. Rotate the gun slightly inward (about 15–30°) to align with the natural line of the arm. Lean forward; the arm recovers from recoil slower without the support hand. Support hand only Same grip principles with the other hand. Close the strong-side eye if you can't use the support-side eye with the sights. Practice support-hand draws (reaching across) and transfers from the strong hand in dry practice. Practice one-handed malfunction clearances: rack the slide on a belt, holster edge, or heel."},{"id":"light","h":"Low light","x":"13 Low light Technique How Notes Weapon-mounted light Light on the pistol's rail, switch under the trigger or support finger Two hands on the gun; never search with the muzzle at things you don't intend to shoot. Use the spill. Neck index Light held at the jaw line, beam forward Lights what you see; easy to maintain; works with one-handed shooting Harries Light hand under the gun hand, backs of hands pressed together Stable, aligned beam; tiring over time FBI (high) Light held high and away from the body Moves the light away from your center; harder to aim the beam Light, identify, act or turn it off, move. In a dark house, a burst of light can disorient an intruder and lets you see hands. Always identify; the person in your hallway at night may be a family member."},{"id":"after","h":"After an incident","x":"14 After an incident Immediately Stay alert; scan; get to a safe position. Holster when the threat is over, or keep the gun at low ready if not. Call 911. Holster or put the gun down before police arrive. Empty hands visible. Follow every command, even if it's uncomfortable. Render aid if it's safe and you're trained. What to say to 911 \"My name is ___. I'm at ___. I was attacked and had to defend myself. Someone is injured; send police and an ambulance. I'm wearing ___. I'm armed; my gun is ___ (holstered / on the ground).\" When police arrive Identify yourself as the person who called and was attacked. Point out evidence (the attacker's weapon, shell casings) and witnesses before they leave. Say you'll sign a complaint and will cooperate fully after speaking with your attorney. Don't give a detailed statement while adrenaline is high. Memory, distance, and time estimates are distorted after a violent encounter. Get medical attention; adrenaline masks injuries."},{"id":"drills","h":"Training and drills","x":"15 Training and drills Drill Setup Standard Concealed draw From concealment, one shot in an 8-inch circle at 7 yd 2.0 s / 1.5 s Draw and move Step off the line while drawing, 2 shots in the 8-inch circle at 7 yd 2.5 s Three seconds or less Short strings of 2–4 shots from 3–7 yd, each under 3 seconds All hits Retention 2 shots from retention at 2 yd, step back, 2 shots extended (on an approved range) Clean hits; support arm clear of muzzle Failure to stop 2 shots to the chest, 1 to the head box at 5 yd 3.0 s Assess and scan After every string: assess over and under the sights, scan 360° Every time, until it's habit Take classes beyond the range: force-on-force with marking cartridges, a legal-use-of-force class taught by an attorney, and a trauma medical course. All three cover things marksmanship doesn't."},{"id":"errors","h":"Common errors","x":"16 Common errors Error What it does Fix Standing still to draw Contact before the first shot at close range Move off the line while drawing Never practicing from concealment Fumbled garment clears under stress Dry-practice in your real clothes Holstering fast Negligent discharge, especially appendix Slow, garment clear, look it in, thumb on the hammer or slide Tunnel vision Misses other threats and bystanders Assess and scan after every string in training Extending the gun at contact distance Gun grabbed Retention position, then create space Talking too much afterward Statements made under stress are often wrong and used against you Basic facts, then attorney Carrying without knowing the law Lawful defense turns into a criminal case Take a use-of-force law class for your state"}]},{"u":"defensive-rifle.html","t":"Defensive Rifle Shooting","s":[{"id":"","h":"Defensive Rifle Shooting","x":"Firearm training · Defensive Updated Oct 26, 2026 Defensive Rifle Shooting Using a carbine to protect life: legal limits, setup, ready positions, mechanical offset at close range, shooting from cover, moving off the line of attack, malfunctions, transitions, low light, and home-defense considerations. Built on TC 3-22.9, common defensive carbine curricula, and general U.S. self-defense law. This is training information, not legal advice. Laws differ by state; learn yours from a qualified attorney or instructor. Figures assume a 5.56 mm carbine with a 50/200-yard zero and 2.6-inch sight height."},{"id":"law","h":"Legal and ethical limits","x":"01 Legal and ethical limits Most U.S. jurisdictions allow deadly force only when you reasonably believe it's necessary to prevent imminent death or serious bodily harm to yourself or another innocent person. A rifle doesn't change that standard. See the Defensive Handgun page for the full framework. Ability The attacker has the means to kill or cause serious injury. Opportunity They're in a position to use it now. Jeopardy Their actions show intent to use it. A reasonable person would believe the threat is real. A rifle round can travel through several walls and well beyond your property. You're responsible for every round, including misses and pass-throughs."},{"id":"mind","h":"Mindset and awareness","x":"02 Mindset and awareness Awareness first The best defensive outcome is one where you never need the rifle: you noticed early, avoided, left, locked the door, or called for help. The rifle is for when those options are gone. Decide in advance Know your home's layout, where family members sleep, your safe room, and the lines of fire that would cross a family member's room. Decide those things now, not at 3 a.m."},{"id":"setup","h":"Rifle setup","x":"03 Rifle setup Item Recommendation Why Optic Red dot (with backup irons) or 1–6× LPVO Fast at close range, usable to 300 yd Zero 50/200 yd Stays within about 2 inches of the point of aim from 25 to 225 yd Weapon light White light, 500+ lumens, momentary switch under the support thumb You must identify before you shoot Sling Two-point, quick-adjust Retention, hands-free use, carrying Ammunition Quality expanding or fragmenting loads, confirmed for reliability and zero Stops threats more effectively; lower risk of over-penetration than full metal jacket Magazines Tested, numbered; one in the gun and one on the person or rifle Most malfunctions start with the magazine"},{"id":"ready","h":"Ready positions","x":"04 Ready positions Position How Use Low ready Stock in the shoulder, muzzle depressed about 45°, eyes over the optic Default when a threat may be ahead; fastest to a shot; you can see hands High ready Stock low or under the arm, muzzle up Moving with people below the muzzle line; some indoor movement. Watch ceilings and upper floors. Compressed ready Rifle pulled close to the chest, muzzle down and forward Hallways, doorways, crowds; harder for someone to grab"},{"id":"offset","h":"Mechanical offset","x":"05 Mechanical offset The bore sits below the optic. Up close, the bullet hasn't risen to the line of sight yet, so it hits below where the dot is. At room distances this is about 2–2½ inches. On a large target it doesn't matter; on a small, precise shot it can mean a miss. Point of aim vs. point of impact Distance 3 yd 7 yd 15 yd 25 yd 50 yd Impact vs. dot −2.4\" −2.2\" −1.7\" −1.2\" 0.0\" For a precise close shot, hold the dot about 2 inches high. Practice it on small targets at 3–7 yards until it's automatic."},{"id":"cover","h":"Cover, concealment, and shooting from cover","x":"06 Cover, concealment, and shooting from cover Cover stops rounds; concealment only hides you. Using cover well means staying back from it, so you see around the edge with as little of your body exposed as possible, and so your muzzle doesn't poke past the edge where it can be seen or grabbed. Cover Something solid enough to stop incoming fire: an engine block, a large tree, earth, thick masonry. Fight, reload, and fix malfunctions from behind it. It's temporary: repeated hits wear it down, and the threat can move to a new angle. Concealment Something that hides you but won't stop a bullet: drywall, doors, car doors, hedges, furniture, darkness, smoke. You can't be aimed at if you can't be seen, so use it to move, reposition, or break contact. Expect fire through it at where you were last seen. Material Handgun rounds Rifle rounds Interior walls (drywall) Concealment Rounds pass through several Concealment Wood doors, fences, furniture Concealment Concealment Car doors, body panels, glass Concealment Some rounds deflect; don't count on it Concealment Engine block, wheel and axle Cover Cover for most rounds Brick wall Cover for several hits Short-term Breaks down under repeated hits Hollow concrete block Cover for several hits Short-term Poured concrete, solid masonry Cover Cover for a while Large tree trunk Cover Depends on diameter and wood Utility pole Cover for a narrow target Partial Earth: berms, ditches, ground Cover Cover with 1–2 ft of dirt General guidance, not test data. Construction, angle, and the specific round all change the result. When in doubt, treat it as concealment. Cover depends on what's being fired at you, not on what you're carrying. Exposure at a corner Rules Stand back from cover, 3–6 feet when space allows. Work the edge like slicing the pie, in small steps. Shoot around the side, not over the top, when possible; less head exposure. Switch shoulders to match the side of cover if you've trained it. It reduces exposure on the support-side corner. Remember mechanical offset when shooting just over or past a low edge: the dot can be clear while the bore is still behind the cover. Walls and vehicles Don't hug walls or cars. A bullet that strikes a hard surface at a shallow angle skips along it a few inches off the surface. Behind a vehicle, use the engine block or a wheel. The rest of the car is concealment, and rounds fired under it hit feet: keep yours behind a wheel. Know where you're going next before you leave. Move in short rushes between cover; use concealment to move unseen."},{"id":"move","h":"Movement and assessment","x":"07 Movement and assessment A stationary person is the easiest target there is. When a threat appears, moving off the line of attack, ideally toward cover, forces the attacker to react to you. After the threat stops, assess: is it actually over, is anyone else involved, is anyone hurt? Off the X, to cover, then assess Move laterally or diagonally away, not straight backward. Most people can't move backward as fast as an attacker moves forward. If you shoot, shoot until the threat stops, then stop. Each round must be justified. Come off the sights to see the whole threat: hands, weapon, movement. Scan left, right, and behind. Break tunnel vision by moving your head, not just your eyes."},{"id":"mal","h":"Malfunctions and transitions","x":"08 Malfunctions and transitions Malfunction Signs Clearance Failure to fire Click; magazine not seated or empty chamber Tap the magazine, rack the charging handle fully, reassess Failure to eject (stovepipe) Case visible in the port Tap, rack with the port down; strip it out if needed Double feed Bolt won't close fully; charging handle feels stuck Lock the bolt back, strip the magazine, rack 2–3 times, reload, rack Bolt override Round trapped above the bolt Lock back if possible, strip the magazine, clear the round through the magazine well; may need a tool Stuck case Bolt locked closed on an empty case Out of the fight; needs a cleaning rod. Transition. Clear the rifle or transition? If you carry a pistol and the threat is close, transitioning is usually faster than clearing anything beyond a tap-rack. At longer range or behind solid cover, clearing the rifle may be better since the pistol is harder to hit with at distance."},{"id":"sidearm","h":"The sidearm in a longer fight","x":"09 The sidearm in a longer fight With a rifle, the handgun is the secondary weapon. In a longer fight it has two jobs, and both are about getting the rifle back into action. 1 · Fighting back to the long gun Trouble rarely starts with the rifle in your hands. The handgun is what you have when you're caught away from it: in the yard, at the car, in another room. Use it to survive long enough to reach the rifle, then switch. 2 · Covering a malfunction or empty gun When the rifle stops at close range, switching to the sidearm is faster than fixing the rifle under pressure. A double feed, bolt override, or reload takes fine motor skill that stress takes away first. The draw is a movement you've repeated thousands of times. Problem Time to fix it Time to switch to the sidearm Failure to fire (click) 1.5–2.5 s tap-rack, if it works 1.5–2.5 s to the first shot Empty rifle 2.5–4 s magazine change 1.5–2.5 s Failure to eject (stovepipe) 2–3 s 1.5–2.5 s Double feed 6–10 s, often more 1.5–2.5 s Bolt override 10 s or more; may need a tool 1.5–2.5 s Stuck case or broken part Can't be fixed during the fight 1.5–2.5 s Typical times for a trained shooter. Stress slows every column, and it slows the fine-motor fixes the most. When to switch The threat is close, inside handgun range, and you need rounds now. You're in the open with no time to fix the rifle. The problem is anything worse than a tap-rack. When to fix it instead You're behind cover, where you have time. That's what cover is for. The threat is far enough away that handgun hits are uncertain. After the switch, once the threat is stopped or you reach cover: fix the rifle and go back to it. Making the switch A two-point sling makes it possible: the rifle hangs out of the way instead of being dropped. Support hand pushes the rifle down and aside; firing hand draws. Don't look at the rifle. Wear the holster where you can reach it with the rifle slung and armor on. Practice dry with an unloaded rifle until a click triggers the draw without thinking. The animation above shows the timing; the Rifle to pistol transition drill trains it. The sidearm doesn't usually win a fight a rifle can win. It keeps you in it long enough to get the rifle running again."},{"id":"reload","h":"Reloads","x":"10 Reloads Emergency reload Bolt locked back, empty magazine. Press the magazine release as the support hand gets a fresh magazine. Insert, tug to confirm it's seated. Hit the bolt release or slingshot the charging handle. Back on target; reassess. Tactical reload During a lull, from behind cover: take a fresh magazine in the support hand, remove the partial, insert the fresh one, stow the partial. Only do this when you have the time; a gun with rounds in it is better than a gun in the middle of a reload."},{"id":"light","h":"Low light","x":"11 Low light Identify before you shoot. Low light is where misidentification happens. The light's main job is to see what's in someone's hands. Momentary use. Light on to search or identify, off, then move. Constant-on tells everyone where you are. Use the edge of the beam (the spill) to search without pointing the muzzle at what you're looking at. Know your switch by feel. Practice activation from each ready position."},{"id":"home","h":"Home defense","x":"12 Home defense Plan Default plan: gather family to a safe room, call 911, hold a defensible position covering the entry. Don't search the house unless someone needs you. Know where every family member's room is relative to your likely lines of fire. Store the rifle so you can get to it quickly and unauthorized people (including children) can't. A quick-access safe or rack with a lock. Over-penetration Typical interior walls are two layers of drywall and offer almost no resistance. Testing by independent groups commonly shows lightweight 5.56 mm bullets fragmenting in wallboard and passing through fewer walls than many 9 mm or buckshot loads, but they still pass through several. No common defensive round is wall-safe. Plan lines of fire, not cartridges. Short barrels and hearing Indoors, a 16-inch or shorter carbine is loud enough to cause permanent hearing damage in one shot. A suppressor, where legal, or electronic hearing protection kept with the rifle reduces it. Plan for disorientation if neither is available."},{"id":"drills","h":"Drills","x":"13 Drills Drill Setup Standard Ready-up Low ready, one shot on a 3×5 card at 7 yd 1.0 s; all hits Offset drill Five 1-inch dots at 5 yd, one shot each, holding for offset 5/5 Pairs Low ready, 2 shots on an 8-inch circle at 15 yd 1.5 s Cover drill From behind a barricade, 2 shots on each side and 2 low, alternating shoulders Clean hits; no muzzle past the edge Malfunction to transition Dummy round induces a failure; transition to pistol, 2 shots at 7 yd Under 3.0 s from click Light drill Low light, identify a target (hands visible, armed or not), shoot only armed targets No shots on unarmed targets"},{"id":"after","h":"After an incident","x":"14 After an incident Stay behind cover and keep watching until you're sure it's over. Call 911: your location, that you were attacked, that someone is hurt and needs medical help, your description, and that you're armed. Put the rifle down or sling it before police arrive. Have empty hands visible. Follow every command. Give officers the basic facts: you were attacked, point out evidence and witnesses. Then say you'll cooperate fully after speaking with your attorney. Get medical attention for yourself. Adrenaline hides injuries."},{"id":"errors","h":"Common errors","x":"15 Common errors Error What it does Fix Ignoring offset up close Misses on small targets or hits on cover Hold high on precise close shots; train it Crowding cover More exposure; muzzle visible and grabbable Stand off cover; slice the edge Standing still Easy target Move off the line, to cover Tunnel vision after shots Misses additional threats Come off the sights, scan with the head Light on, staying put Gives away your position Light, identify, off, move Searching the house alone Walking into an ambush Safe room, call, hold Untested ammunition or magazines Malfunctions when it matters Fire several hundred rounds of the carry load through the rifle and magazines"}]},{"u":"defensive-shotgun.html","t":"Defensive Shotgun Shooting","s":[{"id":"","h":"Defensive Shotgun Shooting","x":"Firearm training · Defensive Updated Oct 26, 2026 Defensive Shotgun Shooting The shotgun for home and property defense: legal limits, setup, ammunition, how buckshot actually patterns at room distances, the manual of arms, keeping the gun fed, recoil control, moving indoors with a long gun, and low light. Built on common defensive shotgun curricula, manufacturer pattern data, and general U.S. self-defense law. This is training information, not legal advice. Pattern figures are typical for a cylinder-bore 18.5-inch barrel; pattern your own gun with your own load."},{"id":"law","h":"Legal and ethical limits","x":"01 Legal and ethical limits Deadly force is justified only when you reasonably believe it's necessary to prevent imminent death or serious bodily harm to yourself or another innocent person. Every pellet you fire is your responsibility, including the ones that miss. See the Defensive Handgun page for the full framework. Ability The attacker has the means to kill or cause serious injury. Opportunity They're in a position to use it now. Jeopardy Their actions show intent to use it."},{"id":"role","h":"Role of the shotgun","x":"02 Role of the shotgun Strengths Each 00 buckshot shell fires about nine .33-caliber pellets at once. Very effective at stopping a threat at short range. Flexible: buckshot for close range, slugs for precision out to 50–100 yd. Widely legal and familiar; pumps are inexpensive and reliable. Limits Limited capacity (4–8 rounds) and slow to reload. Heavy recoil; harder for smaller shooters without good technique. Long and harder to maneuver indoors than a handgun or short carbine. It still has to be aimed. At room distance the pattern is only a few inches wide."},{"id":"setup","h":"Setup","x":"03 Setup Item Recommendation Why Sights Ghost ring, rifle sights, or a red dot rather than a bead Slugs and precise buckshot shots need real sights Length of pull Short, 12.5–13.5\" Squarer stance, better recoil control, easier with armor or winter clothes Light Forend-mounted with a switch you hit without changing grip Identify before you shoot Sling Two-point Carry, retention, hands free to open doors or help family Sidesaddle 4–6 shells on the receiver; consider one slug row Reload ammunition always on the gun Barrel 18–20\", cylinder or improved cylinder Maneuverable; patterns open enough for room distances"},{"id":"ammo","h":"Ammunition","x":"04 Ammunition Load Projectiles Defensive suitability Birdshot (#7½–#9) Hundreds of small pellets POOR Shallow penetration; may not stop a determined attacker. Not recommended. #4 buck ≈27 pellets of .24\" FAIR Often falls short of FBI penetration guidelines. 00 buck 8–9 pellets of .33\" GOOD The standard defensive load. Reduced-recoil versions are easier to control. #1 buck ≈15–16 pellets of .30\" GOOD More pellets, similar penetration to 00. Tight-patterning 00 buck 8 pellets in a wad that holds them together longer BEST Patterns about half as wide as standard buckshot. Fewer stray pellets. Slug One 1-oz projectile SPECIAL Precision and distance; greater over-penetration risk indoors."},{"id":"pattern","h":"Patterns at room distance","x":"05 Patterns at room distance The most common misunderstanding about shotguns is that you don't have to aim. Across a bedroom the pattern is about the size of a fist. It grows about an inch per yard with standard buckshot from a cylinder bore, about half that with tight-patterning loads. Pattern on a torso target as distance grows Distance Standard 00 Tight-patterning 00 3 yd (across a room) ≈3\" ≈1.5\" 7 yd (long room or hallway) ≈7\" ≈3.5\" 15 yd (yard) ≈15\" ≈7\" 25 yd ≈25\" (pellets off a torso) ≈12\" Find your gun's maximum buckshot distance: the farthest range where every pellet still lands inside an 8–10 inch circle. Beyond that, use a slug or get closer."},{"id":"arms","h":"Manual of arms","x":"06 Manual of arms Most defensive shotguns are pumps. A pump only works if you run it: all the way back, all the way forward, hard. A short stroke under stress is the pump's most common failure. Inside the action: full stroke vs. short stroke Pump Run the action as soon as you fire; don't wait to see the result. Support hand stays on the forend; it never has to search for it. To unload: on many pumps, cycle shells out one at a time through the action, finger off the trigger, or depress the shell latch per the manual. Semi-automatic Softer felt recoil (gas) and no pump to short-stroke. Test with your defensive load; some guns are picky with reduced-recoil loads. Ride the bolt forward fully when chambering; easing it can leave it out of battery. Storage condition A common home setup is \"cruiser ready\" : tube full, chamber empty, action closed on an empty chamber, safety off (on guns where that's safe), so one full pump chambers a round. It's drop-safer and lets you decide when to chamber. Follow your gun's manual and keep it locked from unauthorized people."},{"id":"feed","h":"Keeping the gun fed","x":"07 Keeping the gun fed With 4–8 rounds and single-shell loading, the shotgun rewards topping off at every pause rather than waiting to run dry. Rounds available over time Technique When How Shoot one, load one Any pause After each shot, load one shell into the tube from the sidesaddle or belt Load two Longer pause Two shells in the support hand, push both into the tube Emergency (port) load Gun is empty Drop a shell through the ejection port onto the carrier, close the action, fire. Then load the tube. Slug select Need precision now With a buckshot shell chambered: open the action partway, drop a slug in the port, close, fire. The ejected buckshot shell is lost."},{"id":"sidearm","h":"The sidearm when the shotgun runs dry","x":"08 The sidearm when the shotgun runs dry With a shotgun, the handgun is the secondary weapon. A shotgun holds few rounds and reloads one shell at a time, so in a longer fight the sidearm has two jobs. 1 · Fighting back to the long gun Trouble rarely starts with the shotgun in your hands. The handgun is what you have when you're caught away from it. Use it to survive long enough to reach the shotgun, then switch. 2 · Covering a malfunction or empty gun When the shotgun runs dry or stops at close range, switching to the sidearm is faster than feeding or clearing the shotgun under pressure. Loading a tube shell by shell is the slowest reload of any common defensive gun, and stress makes it slower. Problem Time to fix it Time to switch to the sidearm Click (empty chamber) 1–1.5 s to rack, if a shell is in the tube 1.5–2.5 s to the first shot Gun empty 2–3 s to port-load one shell; about 1.5 s per shell into the tube 1.5–2.5 s Short-stroke or failure to eject 2–4 s 1.5–2.5 s Shell jammed or swollen hull Can't be fixed quickly 1.5–2.5 s Typical times for a trained shooter. Stress slows every column, and it slows the fine-motor fixes the most. When to switch The threat is close and the shotgun is empty or stopped. You're in the open with no time to feed it. More than one shell is needed right now. When to fix it instead You're behind cover with time to feed the tube. Only one shell is needed: port-load it. Once the threat is stopped or you reach cover: load the shotgun and go back to it. Making the switch A sling lets the shotgun hang instead of being dropped. Without one, set it down where you can keep track of it. Support hand pushes the shotgun aside; firing hand draws. Don't look at the shotgun. Practice dry with an unloaded shotgun until running empty triggers the draw without thinking. The sidearm doesn't usually win a fight a shotgun can win. It keeps you in it long enough to get the shotgun loaded again."},{"id":"recoil","h":"Stance and recoil","x":"09 Stance and recoil Stock high in the pocket of the shoulder, inside the shoulder joint, not on the arm. Bring the stock to the cheek; keep the head upright. Run the pump during recoil; the rearward motion of the gun helps you cycle it."},{"id":"indoors","h":"Moving indoors with a long gun","x":"10 Moving indoors with a long gun A shotgun is 36–40 inches long. Leading with the muzzle at corners and doorways lets someone around the corner see it, grab it, or push it aside before you see them. Stay back from corners and keep the gun compressed until you need to shoot. Approaching a corner with a long gun Retention Pull the stock under the arm or to the chest in tight spaces (compressed ready). If someone grabs the barrel, pull it toward you while stepping back and rotating the muzzle toward them, or drive the muzzle into them. Keep the sling on. It makes the gun hard to take. Corners and doors Slice the corner from several feet back, like the CQB Primer shows. Don't search alone if you can avoid it. Hold a position and let police clear. Know your lines of fire relative to family members' rooms."},{"id":"cover","h":"Cover and concealment","x":"11 Cover and concealment Cover stops the rounds being fired at you. Concealment only hides you. Both matter, but they aren't interchangeable, and inside a house almost everything is concealment. Cover Something solid enough to stop incoming fire: an engine block, a large tree, earth, thick masonry. Fight, reload, and fix malfunctions from behind it. It's temporary: repeated hits wear it down, and the threat can move to a new angle. Concealment Something that hides you but won't stop a bullet: drywall, doors, car doors, hedges, furniture, darkness, smoke. You can't be aimed at if you can't be seen, so use it to move, reposition, or break contact. Expect fire through it at where you were last seen. Material Handgun rounds Rifle rounds Interior walls (drywall) Concealment Rounds pass through several Concealment Wood doors, fences, furniture Concealment Concealment Car doors, body panels, glass Concealment Some rounds deflect; don't count on it Concealment Engine block, wheel and axle Cover Cover for most rounds Brick wall Cover for several hits Short-term Breaks down under repeated hits Hollow concrete block Cover for several hits Short-term Poured concrete, solid masonry Cover Cover for a while Large tree trunk Cover Depends on diameter and wood Utility pole Cover for a narrow target Partial Earth: berms, ditches, ground Cover Cover with 1–2 ft of dirt General guidance, not test data. Construction, angle, and the specific round all change the result. When in doubt, treat it as concealment. Cover depends on what's being fired at you, not on what you're carrying. Using cover with a shotgun Stand back from it. The barrel is long; crowding a doorframe or corner puts the muzzle past the edge where it can be seen or grabbed. Don't hug walls or cars. Rounds that hit a hard surface at a shallow angle skip along it a few inches off the surface. Indoors, a position matters more than a wall. Interior walls stop nothing; choose a spot where the threat has to come to you through a doorway, with something solid behind them. Reload behind cover, not in the open. Feeding a tube takes time, and cover is what buys it."},{"id":"light","h":"Low light","x":"12 Low light A forend light is the best option. Your support hand both runs the pump and controls the light. Identify before you shoot: hands and what's in them. Light on to look, off, move. In a hallway the splash lights you as well. Flash from a short barrel is bright at night. Practice in low light so it isn't a surprise."},{"id":"drills","h":"Drills","x":"13 Drills Drill Setup Standard Pattern check One shell each at 3, 7, 15, 25 yd on paper Know where every pellet goes Ready to one shot Low ready, one buckshot round in an 8-inch circle at 7 yd 1.2 s Pairs with pump Two rounds at 7 yd, running the pump between 2.0 s Shoot one, load one Fire one, load one from sidesaddle, fire one 4.0 s Emergency load Gun empty, port-load, one shot at 7 yd 3.0 s Slug select Buckshot chambered; select slug, one shot on a 6-inch plate at 25 yd 5.0 s"},{"id":"after","h":"After an incident","x":"14 After an incident Stay in a defensible position and keep watching until you're sure it's over. Call 911: location, that you were attacked, that someone needs medical help, your description, and that you're armed. Set the shotgun down before police arrive. Empty hands visible; follow every command. Give the basic facts, point out evidence and witnesses, then say you'll cooperate fully after speaking with your attorney."},{"id":"errors","h":"Common errors","x":"15 Common errors Error What it does Fix \"No need to aim\" Misses; stray pellets Aim every shot; know your pattern Short-stroking the pump Click instead of bang Run it hard both directions, every time Birdshot for defense Inadequate penetration 00 or #1 buckshot Standing upright, leaning back Recoil pushes you off balance; slow follow-ups Weight forward, push-pull Running it dry Empty gun at the worst time Top off at every pause Muzzle past the corner Gun seen or grabbed Stay back; compress the gun"}]},{"u":"drills.html","t":"Common Practice Drills","s":[{"id":"","h":"Common Practice Drills","x":"Firearm training · Drills Updated Nov 27, 2026 Common Practice Drills Named drills give you a fixed task and a standard, so you can measure progress across months instead of guessing. This page collects the well-known ones for the handgun, rifle, and shotgun, with the setup, round count, standard, and what each one builds. There's a par timer at the top you can run on your phone at the range. Run every drill on a range that allows it: drawing from a holster, moving, and shooting multiple targets aren't allowed everywhere. Standards are common published figures; where sources differ, the most widely used version is given. Start slow and clean, then speed up. A fast miss isn't progress."},{"id":"timer","h":"Par timer","x":"00 Par timer 0.00 Par (s) Delay min (s) Delay max (s) Start Stop Press Start, then get ready. A beep after a random delay starts the string; a second beep marks the par time. The timer uses your phone's speaker. Turn the volume up and wear electronic hearing protection so you can hear it. It doesn't detect shots; it gives a start signal and a par signal."},{"id":"how","h":"How to use drills","x":"01 How to use drills Shoot it cold. The first run of the day, without warm-up, is the honest measure of what you can do on demand. Write it down. Date, gun, ammunition, time, hits, and what went wrong. Progress shows up in the log, not in memory. Penalize misses. Most standards add time for each miss. Don't let a fast time hide poor hits. Change one thing at a time. If you're working on the draw, keep the distance and target the same."},{"id":"hg","h":"Handgun drills","x":"02 Handgun drills Mozambique (failure drill) Distance 3–7 yd Rounds 3 Target 8\" chest, 3×5\" head box Standard 3.0 s from holster at 7 yd; 2.5 s advanced Two shots to the chest, then one precise shot to the head. Named for an incident in the Mozambique war of independence that firearms instructor Jeff Cooper later taught as a drill. It trains changing from a fast pair to a precise shot, which means slowing down and refining the sight picture for the third round. Most modern classes teach assessing after the pair instead of firing the head shot automatically; run it both ways. Dicken drill Distance 40 yd Rounds 10 Target USPSA or IDPA torso Standard From concealment, 8 of 10 hits in 15 s Named after Elisjsha Dicken, who stopped an active shooter at the Greenwood Park Mall in Indiana in July 2022 with a pistol, firing from about 40 yards. It tests whether you can make hits at a distance most carry practice ignores. Some versions score it as a USPSA hit factor or allow a supported position with a time penalty. Shoot it slowly and accurately first; at 40 yards, the trigger press matters more than speed. Bill drill Distance 7 yd Rounds 6 Target A-zone or 8\" circle Standard 3.0 s good, 2.0 s advanced Draw and fire six shots as fast as you can keep them all in the A-zone. Named after Bill Wilson. It trains recoil control and seeing the sights lift and return between shots. FAST (Fundamentals, Accuracy and Speed Test) Distance 7 yd Rounds 6, with 2 in the gun Target 3×5\" card above an 8\" circle Standard Under 10 s intermediate, under 7 s advanced, under 5 s twice for the coin From concealment: two shots on the card, slide-lock reload, four shots on the circle. Add 2 seconds for each miss on the card and 1 for each miss on the circle. Designed by Todd Green of pistol-training.com to test the draw, a precise shot, a reload, and fast accurate shots in one short run. El Presidente Distance 10 yd Rounds 12 Target 3 targets, 1 yd apart Standard 10 s good, 6 s advanced, all A's Start facing away with hands above the shoulders. On the signal, turn, draw, fire two on each target, reload, and fire two on each again. Created by Jeff Cooper. It covers the turn, the draw, transitions between targets, and a reload under time. Casino drill Distance 5 yd Rounds 21, 7 per magazine Target Six numbered shapes Standard 21 s, +1 s per miss or wrong target One round on shape 1, two on shape 2, up to six on shape 6, with two forced reloads along the way. Adds thinking to the shooting: you have to count and pick the right target while running the gun. Dot Torture and 10-10-10 Distance 3 yd / 10 yd Rounds 50 / 10 Target 2\" dots / B-8 bullseye Standard 50 of 50 / all in the black in 10 s Dot Torture covers draws, pairs, one-handed shooting, and transitions on ten 2-inch dots at slow speed; it's untimed and shows trigger problems clearly. The 10-10-10 is ten shots at ten yards in ten seconds from the holster on a B-8 bullseye. Both reward a clean trigger press over speed. The Handgun and Defensive Handgun pages have more, including the 5×5, doubles, the Blake drill, and the FBI qualification."},{"id":"rf","h":"Rifle drills","x":"03 Rifle drills Rifle Mozambique Distance 7–25 yd Rounds 3 Target 8\" chest, 3×5\" head Standard 2.0 s from low ready at 7 yd; 3.0 s at 25 yd The same two-and-one as the pistol drill, from low ready. With an optic and an AR, the head shot is where mechanical offset shows up: aim slightly high at close distances. Ready-up Distance 25–50 yd Rounds 1 Target 3×5\" card Standard 1.5 s at 25 yd, 2.0 s at 50 yd From low ready, mount the rifle and fire one precise shot. The mount, cheek weld, and first sight picture decide almost every rifle engagement, and this drill isolates them. Rifle Bill drill Distance 10–25 yd Rounds 6 Target 8\" circle Standard 2.5 s at 25 yd Six shots from low ready, as fast as you can keep them in the circle. Teaches you to drive the rifle with the support hand and keep the dot in the circle through recoil. Rifle to pistol transition Distance 7 yd Rounds 1 + 2 Target 8\" circle Standard Under 3.0 s from the click Load the rifle with one round. Fire it, get a click on the next trigger press, let the rifle hang on the sling, draw, and fire two pistol rounds. It only makes sense inside handgun distance; farther out, fixing the rifle is usually faster. 9-hole barricade Distance 50–100 yd Rounds 9–18 Target 8–10\" steel Standard One hit per hole, clean, then add time Kyle Lamb's VTAC barricade has nine openings at different heights and angles. Fire one or two rounds through each, from the high standing hole down to prone. It builds stable improvised positions and teaches mechanical offset, because the bullet leaves below the optic and can hit the barricade edge. Positional and KD Distance 100–500 yd Rounds 8–20 Target 8–12\" plates Standard First-round hits from your data Two rounds each from prone, sitting, kneeling, and standing at 100 yd; then plates at known distances using your data book. See the Rifle and Bolt Rifle pages for full standards."},{"id":"sg","h":"Shotgun drills","x":"04 Shotgun drills Pattern check Distance 3, 7, 15, 25 yd Rounds 4+ Target Large paper Standard Know every pellet's landing One round of your defensive load at each distance. Measure the spread. Every other shotgun drill depends on knowing at what distance your pattern stops fitting inside the target. Shoot one, load one Distance 7 yd Rounds 5+ Target 8\" circle Standard Under 4 s per fire-load cycle Fire one, load one shell from a sidesaddle or belt, fire again. Repeat. The gun never runs dry, and you learn to feed it in pauses rather than when it's empty. Shoot two, load two Distance 7 yd Rounds 4+ Target 8\" circle Standard Under 6 s per cycle Two rounds, running the pump hard between them, then two shells into the tube. Builds the rhythm of a pump gun: fire, pump, fire, feed. Emergency load Distance 7 yd Rounds 1 Target 8\" circle Standard 3.0 s from empty Gun empty with the action open. Drop a shell through the ejection port (port load), close the action, and fire. This is how you get a pump shotgun back into action fastest when it runs dry. Slug select Distance 25 yd Rounds 1 slug Target 6\" plate Standard 5.0 s Buckshot chambered. Switch to a slug and hit the plate. Most methods put the slug in the chamber by ejecting the chambered shell or by port loading. Used when the pattern is too wide for the distance or the target needs precision. Kick the can Distance 7 yd Rounds 4–8 Target 2+ aluminum cans Standard Two hits on each can while it's still moving Paper never moves. Hitting each can, then hitting it again as it tumbles, forces you to drive the gun onto a moving target and run the action fast. Use birdshot, and only on ranges that allow it. More on the Defensive Shotgun page."},{"id":"plan","h":"Building a session","x":"05 Building a session Block Time What to do Cold test 2 min One benchmark drill, first rounds of the day. Log it. Fundamentals 15 min Slow, precise work: Dot Torture, groups, or a ready-up at distance. Skill block 20 min One skill: draws, reloads, transitions, or movement. Short reps with a timer. Stress test 10 min A timed standard such as FAST, El Presidente, or the Casino drill. Close 5 min Repeat the cold test and compare. Write down one thing to fix next time."}]},{"u":"fieldcraft.html","t":"Fieldcraft","s":[{"id":"","h":"Fieldcraft","x":"Medical & fieldcraft · Fieldcraft Updated Nov 27, 2026 Fieldcraft How small teams move and stay unseen outside of buildings: finding your way with a map and compass, choosing a hide site, moving without leaving a trail, managing what you give off in visible light, infrared, and thermal, living under drones, watching from an observation post, getting out of a city, moving a casualty, and dividing the work across a team. Fieldcraft is learned outdoors, not on a page. Practice each skill on familiar ground, in daylight first, before you need it somewhere unfamiliar at night."},{"id":"nav","h":"Land navigation","x":"01 Land navigation GPS fails: batteries die, signals are jammed or spoofed, and a glowing screen gives you away at night. A paper map and a compass don't. The aim is to always know roughly where you are, so a check confirms your position instead of finding it from scratch. The map Learn the marginal information first: scale, contour interval, grid zone, and declination diagram. Military maps use MGRS grids; a six-digit grid locates you to 100 meters, an eight-digit grid to 10. Read grids right, then up. Keep the map in a waterproof case, folded to your area. The compass A lensatic or baseplate compass gives a magnetic azimuth. The map uses grid north, so convert with the declination from the map's diagram (the old mnemonic: \"east is least, west is best\" when converting grid to magnetic). Hold it away from steel, rifles, and radios. Terrain association Orient the map so its features line up with the ground, then match hills, saddles, draws, ridges, and streams to the contour lines. Contour lines close together mean steep ground; a V pointing uphill is a draw or stream. Distance Keep a pace count: the number of steps you take per 100 meters on flat ground, usually 60–70 for an adult. It rises on slopes, in brush, and under load. Ranger beads let you count without thinking about it. Technique What it does When to use it Dead reckoning Follow an azimuth for a measured distance Featureless ground, at night, in thick forest Terrain association Navigate by landforms and features Open or hilly country; faster and more forgiving Handrail Follow a linear feature such as a ridge or stream beside your route Moving quickly without constant compass checks Attack point Navigate to an obvious feature near the objective, then do the last short leg precisely Finding a small objective such as a rally point Deliberate offset Aim to one side of a point on a linear feature, so you know which way to turn when you hit it Reaching a bridge, trail junction, or ford Resection Shoot back-azimuths to two or three known features and plot where they cross Confirming your position when you're unsure"},{"id":"hide","h":"Leaving the area and hide sites","x":"02 Leaving the area and hide sites When a plan falls apart, the first move is to break contact and put distance and terrain between you and whatever went wrong. The first stop is usually short and hasty; the deliberate hide comes later, once you know you weren't followed. Get out first Leave by a route that puts terrain between you and the threat as quickly as possible. Don't stop at the first comfortable spot. Agree on a rally point and a time limit in advance so a separated team can find each other. Pick the unlikely spot Searchers check obvious hiding places first: the thickest cover, the only tree line, buildings. A small, unremarkable patch of low cover away from trails, with a clear view of approaches and a back door, is better than the best-looking thicket. Approach with a buttonhook Walk past the hide site, then loop back to it from downwind so anyone following your trail walks past you first. Enter the last few meters slowly and leave no sign at the entrance. Living in it Low profile, minimal movement, no cooking smell or light. One person on watch at all times. Keep gear packed so you can leave in seconds, and know your escape route and the next rally point before you settle in."},{"id":"track","h":"Moving without a trail","x":"03 Moving without a trail Trackers read sign: anything you disturb. Ground sign is footprints, scuffs, overturned stones, and broken sticks; top sign is bent or broken vegetation above the ankle, scraped bark, and disturbed dew or spider webs. You can't leave nothing, but you can leave less, leave it where it's hard to see, and make following it slow. Reduce sign Step on rock, roots, leaf litter, and hard ground; avoid mud, sand, snow, and dew-wet grass. Move around vegetation rather than through it, and don't break branches. Walk in each other's footprints in single file to hide the number of people. Pack out everything, and bury nothing that animals can dig up. Slow the tracker Cross hard surfaces, streams, and roads at angles that don't line up with your direction of travel. Change direction often, and after a few changes make a large one. Avoid trails and the easy, obvious line through terrain that anyone would take. Move at night or in rain, which hide sign and slow searchers. Movement How Use Traveling Everyone moves together at a steady pace Contact unlikely; speed matters Traveling overwatch The lead element moves ahead; the trail element follows at a distance where it can support Contact possible Bounding overwatch One element stops and covers while the other moves, then they swap Contact expected; slowest Halts Stop, look, listen, and smell for a few minutes before moving again Every time you stop, and before crossing a danger area"},{"id":"sig","h":"Signature: visible, IR, thermal","x":"04 Signature: visible, IR, thermal Anything that makes you stand out from the background gives you away: your shape, shine, shadow, silhouette, spacing, movement, sound, and electronic signals. Modern sensors add two more spectrums. Night vision sees near-infrared light, including IR lasers and illuminators, and thermal sees heat. Each needs its own countermeasure. Spectrum What gives you away What helps Visible Regular outlines (head, shoulders, rifle), shine from skin, glass, and metal, skylining on ridges, movement, spacing in a straight line Break up outlines with local vegetation, cover skin and glass, move below crests, stay in shadow, move slowly and only when needed Near-infrared (night vision) IR lasers and illuminators, some fabrics and camo patterns that glow under NV, screens, indicator LEDs Use IR only when necessary and briefly, IR-treated clothing, cover LEDs and screens, assume the other side has night vision too Thermal Body heat (especially head, face, and hands), warm engines and exhaust, recently fired barrels, disturbed ground, campfires Mass between you and the sensor (earth, rock, thick logs, vehicles), insulating layers and purpose-built thermal blankets, staying still, moving when the ground is close to body temperature Electronic Phones, radios, Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, GPS devices that transmit, smart watches Turn off or leave behind what you don't need, keep transmissions short and scheduled Thermal is not magic Thermal sensors see surface temperature, not through walls, glass, dense foliage, or solid objects. Rain, fog, and ground that has warmed to near body temperature in the afternoon reduce contrast. A thermal blanket or poncho hides a still person, but a warm patch of ground stays after you leave. Movement beats camouflage Every sensor, human eyes included, picks up movement first. A well-camouflaged person who moves is easier to spot than a poorly camouflaged one who stays still. Move when the observer is least likely to be looking, and freeze when you think you've been seen."},{"id":"drones","h":"Drones","x":"05 Drones Small drones put an observer overhead, often with a thermal camera. Some carry munitions. A drone sees what a person on a hill sees, from directly above, and can follow you. Overhead cover now matters as much as cover from the front. Detection Many small drones are heard before they're seen: a high-pitched buzz that changes as they maneuver. Assign someone to listen and look up. Commercial drones hover and observe; FPV drones fly fast and low toward a target. Hiding from above Tree canopy, building overhangs, and vehicles block the view. Stay under them, freeze when a drone is close, and don't look up with an uncovered face. In thermal, mass overhead (a thick tree trunk, a building, a vehicle) beats thin cover. Moving under drones Move in short bounds between overhead cover, spread out so one camera frame doesn't capture everyone, and change direction once out of sight, since an operator watching you will predict where you're heading. A sharp hook after breaking line of sight can lose a following drone. Engaging them Shooting a drone is hard: it's small, moving, and often closer or farther than it looks. A shotgun with birdshot is more effective than a rifle at short range. Laws on interfering with aircraft apply; know them before you act outside a war zone."},{"id":"recon","h":"Recon and observation posts","x":"06 Recon and observation posts Recon answers specific questions without being seen. The CQB Primer covers recon fundamentals, the SALUTE report, and actions at the objective; this section covers watching from a fixed position. Choosing an OP It needs a view of the objective, a covered and concealed route in and out, cover from fire, and concealment from the likely directions of observation, including from above. Avoid the most obvious vantage point, which is where the other side will look first. Running it Two people minimum: one observes and records, one provides security and rests. Rotate before the observer's attention fades, usually every 20–30 minutes. Log times, counts, descriptions, and directions exactly as seen. Optics Binoculars or a spotting scope for detail, a monocular for quick checks, and night vision or thermal after dark. Shade the lenses with a hood or mesh, and keep glass back from the edge of the hide so it doesn't reflect. Reporting Report what you saw, not what you think it means, using a set format such as SALUTE: size, activity, location, unit or uniform, time, and equipment. Agree in advance when to report and what triggers an immediate report."},{"id":"urban","h":"Urban movement","x":"07 Urban movement In a city, the threat to a small group moving through it is being noticed. The terrain is full of people, cameras, and lines of sight from above. The goal is to look like you belong and to avoid places where you'd stand out. Blend in Dress and carry yourself like the people around you. Visible kit, matching outfits, and a group moving in formation attract attention. Split a group into pairs that move separately and link up at planned points. Routes Avoid chokepoints such as bridges, tunnels, and main roads that can be closed or watched. Know several routes out, and use alleys, parks, rail lines, and drainage channels where they help. Move at the times when the streets are busy enough to hide in, or empty enough to see who's watching. Stopping Hasty hides in a city are unoccupied buildings, garages, and roofs, but each is a box with limited exits. Pick spots with two ways out and a view of the approach, and don't stay long. Urban is not CQB Most of a city fight happens across streets, from windows and rooftops, at 50–300 meters, not inside rooms. A rifle with some magnification and a team that can observe high and low matter as much as room-clearing skills."},{"id":"casevac","h":"Moving a casualty","x":"08 Moving a casualty A casualty changes everything: the team's speed, its route, and how many people are free to fight. Treat life threats first (massive bleeding, airway, breathing), then plan the move. Carrying a person is exhausting, so rotate carriers often. Method Carriers Notes Drag (by the vest or a drag strap) 1 Fast, short distances, out of the line of fire; keeps both rescuer and casualty low Fireman's carry 1 Short to medium distances; hard on the carrier and on an injured casualty Two-person carries 2 Supporting a conscious casualty or carrying between two people Soft litter 2–4 Rolls into a pack; the standard for moving any distance Improvised litter 2–4 Two poles through two buttoned jackets, or a poncho with poles Get trained. A tourniquet, a hemostatic dressing, and a litter are only useful in hands that have practiced with them. A Stop the Bleed or TCCC-based course is the starting point."},{"id":"team","h":"Team roles","x":"09 Team roles A small team works when each person has a primary job and a backup for someone else's. Pick roles from what people are already good at, then cross-train so the team survives losing anyone. Role Job Leader Plans, decides, assigns tasks, and keeps the team pointed at the goal Navigator Route planning, pace count, position; a second person keeps a backup count Medic Carries the main medical kit and directs casualty care Communicator Radios, frequencies, schedules, and reports Marksman / observer Optics, observation, and precise fire at distance Rear security Watches behind, checks for sign the team leaves, and covers the back during halts"}]},{"u":"fire-shelter.html","t":"Fire and Shelter","s":[{"id":"","h":"Fire and Shelter","x":"Preparedness · Fire and shelter Updated Nov 27, 2026 Fire and Shelter Staying warm and dry when you can't rely on a heated house: the order of survival priorities, picking and building a shelter, starting and keeping a fire, putting it out properly, and dealing with cold and wet. This is a reference, not wilderness training. Check fire restrictions and burn bans before lighting anything outdoors; a campfire that escapes can kill people and destroy homes. Never burn anything indoors except in a fireplace or stove built for it, and keep a CO alarm in any space you heat."},{"id":"priorities","h":"Priorities","x":"01 Priorities A common rule of thumb, the rule of threes , puts the order in perspective: in harsh conditions people can last roughly three hours without shelter, three days without water, and three weeks without food. The numbers vary widely, but the order is the point: in cold, wind, or rain, shelter comes before everything except stopping serious bleeding. Get out of the weather Wind and wet pull heat from the body far faster than still, dry cold. Get off the ground Cold ground draws heat out by contact. Insulation underneath matters more than a roof overhead. Then fire Fire warms, dries clothes, boils water, signals, and lifts morale. Build the shelter first, while you still have daylight and energy. Stay found If you're lost, staying put near your vehicle or a clearing usually beats wandering. Tell someone your plan before you go out."},{"id":"site","h":"Picking a site","x":"02 Picking a site Avoid low ground Valley floors and hollows collect cold air at night and flood in rain. A spot a little up a slope is often several degrees warmer. Look up Dead branches and leaning dead trees (\"widowmakers\") fall without warning, especially in wind or snow. Avoid dry creek beds and washes Flash floods arrive from rain miles away. Use the land A rock face, fallen log, or dense evergreens block wind. Face the opening away from the wind."},{"id":"shelters","h":"Shelter types","x":"03 Shelter types Shelter Needs Good for Tarp A-frame Tarp, ridgeline cord between two trees, stakes Rain from both sides; the most versatile setup Tarp lean-to Tarp, ridgeline, stakes Wind from one direction, with a fire in front to reflect heat in Emergency bivy A reflective bivy sack Light and fast; keeps a sleeping person dry and warmer. Expect condensation. Debris hut A ridge pole, sticks, and a thick pile of leaves or pine needles No gear at all. Leaves at least an arm's length deep on top and a thick bed underneath. Vehicle The car you're in Out of wind and wet. Crack a window, keep the exhaust clear of snow, and run the engine only in short spells to avoid carbon monoxide. Small beats big: a shelter just big enough for you is far easier to keep warm."},{"id":"fire","h":"Building a fire","x":"04 Building a fire Fire needs heat, fuel, and oxygen. Most failures come from too-big fuel too early, damp fuel, or smothering a young flame. Stage Size Examples Tinder Fluffy, catches a spark Cotton balls with petroleum jelly, dryer lint, birch bark, fatwood shavings, dry grass, char cloth Kindling Pencil lead to pencil thick Dead twigs snapped from standing trees (they're drier than ground wood), split sticks Fuel Thumb to wrist thick, then larger Dead, dry wood that snaps cleanly; add gradually Gather it all first Collect twice as much of each size as you think you need before you strike anything. Teepee or log cabin A teepee of kindling over tinder catches fast; a log-cabin stack burns longer and makes good coals for cooking. Wet weather Split wood to reach the dry core, shave feather sticks, and build on a platform of sticks so the fire isn't sitting on wet ground. The fire site Use an existing fire ring where there is one. Otherwise clear a 10-foot circle down to bare soil, away from overhanging branches, tents, and dry grass."},{"id":"ignition","h":"Ignition","x":"05 Ignition Lighter The easiest and most reliable. Carry two. Warm a butane lighter in a pocket in the cold; cold butane won't light well. Ferro rod Works wet and in the cold, thousands of strikes. Pull the rod back against a fixed striker so you don't knock your tinder apart. Storm-proof matches Burn in wind and even after being dunked. Keep them in a waterproof case with the striker. Practice Light a fire with each method in the backyard, in rain, with cold hands, before you need to."},{"id":"out","h":"Putting it out","x":"06 Putting it out The Forest Service method: drown, stir, feel. Pour water over every ember, stir the ashes, pour again, and feel with the back of your hand. If it's too hot to touch, it's too hot to leave. Never bury a fire with dirt alone: it can smolder for days and flare up."},{"id":"cold-wet","h":"Cold and wet","x":"07 Cold and wet Dress in layers A base layer that wicks sweat (wool or synthetic), an insulating layer (fleece or down), and a wind and rain shell. Shed layers before you sweat; sweat-soaked clothes chill you later. Cotton kills Wet cotton loses almost all its insulation and dries slowly. Avoid it in cold or wet conditions. Hypothermia signs Shivering, fumbling hands, slurred speech, confusion, and drowsiness. Get the person dry, insulated from the ground, out of wind, and give warm sweet drinks if they're alert. See Field Medical . Keep extremities warm Hat, gloves, and dry socks. Change into dry socks before sleeping."},{"id":"at-home","h":"Shelter at home","x":"08 Shelter at home In a winter outage, treat the house like a big shelter. Pick one room A small interior room with few windows, ideally on the sunny side. Close the doors to the rest of the house. Seal it Blankets or plastic over windows, towels along door gaps. A tent set up inside that room holds body heat for sleeping. Safe heat only A wood stove, a heater rated for indoor use, or the low-power options on the Off-Grid page . Never a grill, camp stove, or gas oven. Protect the pipes If the heat will be off for days in freezing weather, shut off the water main and drain the pipes to prevent burst lines."}]},{"u":"food.html","t":"Food Storage","s":[{"id":"","h":"Food Storage","x":"Preparedness · Food Updated Nov 27, 2026 Food Storage Building a food supply you'll actually use: how much to keep, a pantry that rotates itself, long-term staples that last decades, what to do with the fridge and freezer when the power goes out, and cooking without the grid. This is a reference, not food-safety certification. When in doubt, throw it out: food poisoning in an emergency, with no doctor and no clean water, is far worse than going hungry for a meal. Follow USDA guidance on home canning; untested methods can cause botulism."},{"id":"how-much","h":"How much to keep","x":"01 How much to keep Ready.gov's minimum is three days of food per person; two weeks covers most outages and disruptions, and many families build from there to one and then three months. Count calories, not cans Most adults need around 2,000 calories a day, more when working hard or in cold weather (2,500–3,000+). A pantry that looks full can hold surprisingly few calories. Build in layers Start with two weeks of what you already eat, then add long-term staples. Each layer covers a longer disruption without anything going to waste. Special needs first Infant formula, medical diets, food allergies, prescriptions taken with food, and pet food. These are the hardest to find during a shortage. Food you'll eat Stress isn't the time to discover you hate the bucket of survival food. Store what your household already eats and knows how to cook."},{"id":"pantry","h":"The rotating pantry","x":"02 The rotating pantry The simplest system: store what you eat, eat what you store. Buy extra of the shelf-stable things you use every week, and always use the oldest first. First in, first out New purchases go to the back, old ones move to the front. Write the purchase month on each item with a marker. Good pantry foods Canned meat, fish, beans, soup, fruit and vegetables; peanut butter; pasta and rice; oats; crackers; dried fruit and nuts; powdered milk; honey; coffee and tea. Needs no cooking Keep some food that's ready to eat cold: canned meat and beans, nut butters, crackers, bars. You may not always be able to cook. The forgotten items A manual can opener (two), salt, cooking oil, spices, vitamins, and comfort food. Morale matters in a long disruption."},{"id":"long-term","h":"Long-term staples","x":"03 Long-term staples Dry staples with very little moisture or fat last for decades when packed properly. They're cheap per calorie and make the backbone of a long-term supply. Staple Why Notes White rice Cheap calories, long life Brown rice goes rancid in months to a year because of its oils. Dry beans and lentils Protein, pairs with rice Old beans take much longer to cook; budget more fuel and water. Wheat berries, rolled oats Breakfast and baking Wheat needs a grain mill to make flour. Flour itself keeps far less time. Pasta Easy, familiar Cooks fast, so it saves fuel. Sugar, honey, salt Keep indefinitely Store dry. Don't pack sugar or salt with oxygen absorbers: they harden into a brick. Freeze-dried meals Lightweight, just add water Expensive, but very long shelf life and easy for a go-bag or shelter."},{"id":"shelf-life","h":"Shelf life","x":"04 Shelf life Typical figures for cool (around 70 °F or below), dry, dark storage. Heat is the biggest enemy: as a rule of thumb, every 10 °F warmer roughly halves shelf life. A hot garage ruins food storage. Food Typical shelf life High-acid canned food (tomatoes, fruit, juice) 12–18 months Low-acid canned food (meat, vegetables, soup, beans) 2–5 years Peanut butter, unopened 6–24 months Rice, pasta, oats, beans in original packaging 1–2 years White rice, dry beans, oats, wheat sealed in Mylar with oxygen absorbers Up to 25–30 years Freeze-dried meals (unopened, per maker) Up to 25–30 years Powdered milk (nonfat, sealed) Up to 20 years sealed; months once opened Dates on cans are quality dates, not safety dates. Throw out any can that is bulging, leaking, badly dented on a seam, rusted through, or that spurts when opened."},{"id":"packing","h":"Packing for the long haul","x":"05 Packing for the long haul Mylar bags + oxygen absorbers Fill the bag, drop in the right size absorber, squeeze the air out and seal it with an iron or impulse sealer. Store the bags inside food-grade buckets so rodents can't chew through. Buckets and gamma lids Food-grade 5-gallon buckets with screw-on gamma lids make bulk dry goods easy to open and close every week. Only dry food Only pack foods with low moisture (about 10% or less) and low fat in oxygen-free containers. Moist foods sealed without oxygen are where botulism grows. Off the floor, out of the heat Keep buckets on shelves or pallets, away from walls that sweat, out of the garage and attic, and away from chemicals and fuel."},{"id":"outage","h":"Fridge and freezer in an outage","x":"06 Fridge and freezer in an outage Keep the doors shut An unopened fridge keeps food safe for about 4 hours . A full freezer holds about 48 hours (a half-full one about 24) if you don't open it. The 40 °F rule Throw out perishable food (meat, dairy, eggs, leftovers) that has been above 40 °F for 2 hours or more. A cheap appliance thermometer in each tells you instead of guessing. Refreezing Food in the freezer that still has ice crystals or is at 40 °F or below can be refrozen safely. Eat in order Fridge food first, then the freezer, then the pantry. A generator or power station running the fridge in cycles (see Off-Grid Configurations ) stretches this a long way."},{"id":"cooking","h":"Cooking without power","x":"07 Cooking without power Camp stove Propane or butane. Outdoors or in a well-ventilated space only: they make carbon monoxide. Keep spare fuel canisters. Grill Gas or charcoal grills outdoors only, never in a garage, tent, or under an awning that traps fumes. Fuel-saving tricks Soak beans overnight, use a lid, and try \"retained heat\" cooking: bring rice or beans to a boil, then wrap the pot in blankets or put it in a cooler to finish cooking without fuel. Wood A wood stove, fireplace with a cooking grate, or an outdoor rocket stove. See Fire and Shelter ."}]},{"u":"gun-guide.html","t":"Gun Guide","s":[{"id":"","h":"Gun Guide","x":"What should I buy? Gun Guide A starting-point buying reference for new and entry-level owners — two solid picks for every gun type in every budget tier, each with the cartridge it feeds on and a quick take. Filter by type, caliber, or price. Price tiers Bare Minimum Entry Level Acceptable Daily Use Service Tier Type All types Handguns Revolvers Bolt Rifles Semi-Automatic Rifles Lever Rifles Shotguns Surplus Caliber All calibers .22 LR .380 ACP 5.7×28mm 9mm 9×18 Makarov .38 Special .357 Magnum .44 Magnum 5.56 NATO 7.62×39mm .30-30 Win .308 Win 6.5 Creedmoor .300 Win Mag .45-70 Govt 7.62×54R 7.5×55 Swiss 8mm Mauser .303 British .30 Carbine .30-06 12 Gauge 20 Gauge Price range All prices Bare Minimum · $200–$500 Entry Level · $500–$700 Acceptable Daily Use · $700–$1,000 Service Tier · $1,000+ Reset Read first. A plain-language starting point, not legal, safety, or investment advice. Prices are ballpark U.S. street ranges cross-checked against retailer and auction listings in late 2025 / early 2026, and they move constantly — AK and lever-gun prices in particular have climbed. Fit, intended use, and your local laws matter more than any list here. Always buy through a licensed dealer, follow all federal, state, and local law, and get hands-on training. Handguns .22 LR Entry Level $380–450 Ruger Mark IV 22/45 Target .22 with a 1911-style grip and one-button takedown; Guns & Ammo says you'd be hard pressed to find a better .22 for the money. .380 ACP Bare Minimum $350–430 Ruger LCP MAX Tiny 10+1 pocket .380 at about 10 oz — reviewers call it the smallest gun that reliably holds that many rounds. Needs a tool to field-strip. 5.7×28mm Acceptable Daily Use $600–700 Smith & Wesson M&P 5.7 Usually the cheapest of the 5.7 pistols; Guns & Ammo called it the most comfortable 5.7 to shoot. High capacity, light recoil, thin aftermarket, pricey ammo. 5.7×28mm Service Tier $990–1,100 FN Five-seveN MRD The original 5.7 pistol, now optics-ready — 20+1 of fast, flat-shooting ammo. Superb but the priciest 5.7, and the ammo is dear. 9mm Bare Minimum $250–320 Taurus G3 Full-size budget 9mm that undercuts almost everything; widely judged dependable for the money, with basic sights and trigger. 9mm Entry Level $540–620 Glock 19 Gen5 The benchmark 9mm — boringly reliable, holsters and parts everywhere. Weak factory sights are the usual gripe. (Canik's METE SFx is the value alternative near $525.) 9mm Acceptable Daily Use $650–750 Heckler & Koch VP9 HK's striker gun with class-leading ergonomics and a paddle mag release; long praised for reliability and bright three-dot sights. 9mm Service Tier $1,300–1,450 CZ Shadow 2 All-steel competition 9mm that soaks up recoil; a staple on the IPSC line. Heavy for carry, exceptional to shoot. Revolvers .22 LR Bare Minimum $150–220 Heritage Rough Rider Dirt-cheap single-action .22 plinker. Not a defensive gun, but the lowest-cost way into revolvers and a fine trainer. .38 Special Bare Minimum $340–420 Taurus 856 Six-shot .38 snub-nose, a budget alternative to the S&W J-frame; a common first carry revolver. .38 Special Entry Level $480–560 Smith & Wesson Model 642 Hammerless Airweight J-frame rated for .38 +P; the classic snag-free pocket and backup revolver. .357 Magnum Entry Level $520–620 Taurus 692 Seven-shot .357 with a swappable 9mm cylinder — a lot of versatility for the price. .357 Magnum Acceptable Daily Use $750–900 Ruger GP100 Tank-tough .357 built to digest full-power loads for life; heavier, but close to indestructible. .357 Magnum Service Tier $950–1,050 Smith & Wesson 686 The reference .357 Magnum — L-frame, seven shots, the one most shooters measure others against. .44 Magnum Acceptable Daily Use $850–950 Ruger Super Blackhawk Single-action .44 Magnum for hunting and the range; classic Ruger strength at a fair price. .44 Magnum Service Tier $1,100–1,300 Smith & Wesson 629 Stainless N-frame .44 Magnum; the serious handgun-hunting and big-bore standard. Bolt Rifles .22 LR Bare Minimum $200–280 Savage Mark II FV Accurate bolt-action .22 trainer with the AccuTrigger; the standard cheap rimfire for learning fundamentals. .308 Win Bare Minimum $500–625 Ruger American Rifle Gen II The current (Gen II) budget hunting bolt gun — threaded barrel with a muzzle brake, spiral-fluted bolt, and a trigger reviewers call outstanding for the price. Still the budget accuracy champ. .308 Win Entry Level $550–650 Winchester XPR No-frills push-feed hunter known for out-of-box accuracy and a clean trigger. 6.5 Creedmoor Entry Level $500–620 Savage Axis II Entry hunting rifle with the AccuTrigger; 6.5 Creedmoor makes it an easy long-range starter. 6.5 Creedmoor Acceptable Daily Use $825–950 Bergara B-14 Hunter Spanish barrels with a strong accuracy reputation; a common step into precision shooting. 6.5 Creedmoor Service Tier $1,100–1,250 Bergara B-14 HMR Mini-chassis stock and heavy barrel — a ready-made precision/PRS starter rifle. .300 Win Mag Acceptable Daily Use $750–875 Weatherby Vanguard Backed by a sub-MOA guarantee; a proven, affordable magnum for elk and open country. .300 Win Mag Service Tier $1,000–1,250 Browning X-Bolt Refined action, crisp trigger, excellent fit; a lifetime magnum hunter. Semi-Automatic Rifles .22 LR Bare Minimum $300–380 Ruger 10/22 The default first rifle: cheap, reliable, endless aftermarket. Nearly everyone learns on one. 9mm Entry Level $600–700 Ruger PC Carbine Takedown 9mm carbine that feeds Glock mags; soft-shooting and cheap to train with. 5.56 NATO Bare Minimum $500–600 Palmetto State Armory PA-15 The go-to budget AR-15 — the gun that defined the cheap, functional 5.56; plain but reliable, and the easiest platform to learn and build on. 5.56 NATO Entry Level $600–700 Ruger AR-556 Reliable, no-frills AR-15 from a major maker; a safe first 5.56 that simply runs. 5.56 NATO Acceptable Daily Use $850–950 Springfield SAINT Mid-tier AR with better furniture and trigger; a well-rounded do-everything 5.56. 5.56 NATO Service Tier $1,500–1,750 BCM RECCE-16 Du"}]},{"u":"handgun.html","t":"Professional Handgun Shooting","s":[{"id":"","h":"Professional Handgun Shooting","x":"Firearm training · Professional Updated Oct 27, 2026 Professional Handgun Shooting Accuracy and speed with a handgun: grip, stance, sight alignment, trigger control, recoil management, the draw, reloads, malfunctions, dry practice, and competition formats. Short sight radius and a light gun make every error bigger than with a rifle. Built on U.S. Army TC 3-23.35 Pistol , the FBI pistol qualification, and USPSA, IDPA, and NRA practice. Times are typical standards, not hard limits. Right-handed shooter assumed; mirror for left-handed."},{"id":"safety","h":"Safety","x":"01 Safety The four rules Treat every firearm as if it's loaded. Never point the muzzle at anything you aren't willing to destroy. Keep your finger off the trigger until your sights are on the target and you've decided to fire. Be sure of your target and what's beyond it. Handgun-specific A handgun's short length makes it easy to sweep yourself and others. Watch the support hand during the draw and holstering. Holster slowly. Look the gun into the holster, finger straight, clothing and straps clear of the holster mouth. Use a holster that fully covers the trigger guard and holds the gun without a hand on it. In competition, the muzzle must stay within the 180° line : never past perpendicular to the backstop. Never try to catch a dropped gun. On the range A shared range runs on commands from the range safety officer (RSO). Listen for them the whole time you're on the line. With hearing protection on, commands are easy to miss: electronic muffs make speech easier to hear, and you should watch the RSO, the range lights or flags, and the shooters on either side. If everyone around you has stopped, stop and find out why. Command What it means What you do \"The range is hot\" Live fire is allowed. Nobody is forward of the firing line. Eye and ear protection on, including behind the line. Handle firearms only at your position on the line, muzzle downrange. \"Commence fire\" You may load and shoot. Load only now, and only at the line. Wait for this even when the range has been called hot. \"Cease fire\" Stop shooting immediately. Anyone who sees a danger can call it, and everyone obeys it no matter who called it. Finger off the trigger and out of the guard, muzzle downrange, and stay where you are. Don't load, unload, or step back until the RSO tells you what to do next. \"Unload and show clear\" Make your firearm safe and let the RSO check it. Remove the ammunition, open and lock the action, and show the empty chamber to the RSO. \"Is the line clear?\" / \"The line is clear\" The RSO is confirming, then announcing, that every firearm on the line is unloaded with its action open. Your gun is benched or racked: action open, chamber flag in, magazines out. Step back behind the safety line. \"The range is cold\" Nobody touches a firearm. People may go downrange to check or change targets. Hands off guns and ammunition, including loading magazines at the bench, until the range is called hot again. Stay behind the safety line unless you're going downrange. Wording varies by range and organization; some use \"the line is safe,\" whistles, or lights. Read the posted range rules and listen to the briefing before you shoot. The principle doesn't change: firing stops on a cease fire, and no one goes forward until every gun is verified clear. Clear the gun before you step off the line Finger off the trigger, muzzle downrange. Remove the ammunition source first: magazine out, or the magazine tube emptied. Open the action and lock it open. Any round in the chamber comes out. Look and feel: check the chamber and the magazine well or tube by eye, then with a finger. Do the check every time, even when you're sure it's empty. Insert a chamber flag if the range requires one. Bench, rack, case, or holster it the way the range rules say. Never walk away from a loaded gun or carry one off the line. Handgun: drop the magazine first, then rack the slide several times and lock it back. Racking first and dropping the magazine second chambers a new round. Check the chamber and the magazine well before you holster or case it."},{"id":"ammo","h":"Ammunition","x":"02 Ammunition Bullet weight is measured in grains; there are 7,000 grains in a pound. In a given handgun cartridge, lighter bullets leave faster with a sharper recoil impulse, and heavier bullets are slower, recoil with more of a push, and usually print a little higher on the target. Changing weight or type moves your point of impact, so confirm it on paper when you change loads, and run at least 200 rounds of your carry ammunition through the gun before you trust it. Cartridge Common weights Common types Typical velocity Typical use .380 ACP 90 · 95 · 99 gr FMJ, JHP 900–1,000 ft/s (3.75\") Deep concealment pocket pistols; marginal penetration with some JHPs .38 Special 110 · 125 · 130 · 158 gr LRN, FMJ, +P JHP, wadcutter 750–950 ft/s (4\") less from a 2\" snub Revolver carry and home defense, light-recoil practice in .357 revolvers 9mm Luger (9×19) 115 · 124 · 147 gr FMJ, JHP, +P JHP, frangible 950–1,250 ft/s (4\") +100–200 from a 16\" carbine Carry, duty, training, pistol-caliber carbine .357 Magnum 125 · 158 · 180 gr JHP, JSP, hard-cast lead 1,150–1,450 ft/s (4\") up to 1,800 from an 18\" lever gun Revolver carry, woods carry, hunting, lever-action carbines; fires .38 Special too .40 S&W 155 · 165 · 180 gr FMJ, JHP 980–1,200 ft/s (4\") Duty (declining), carry 10mm Auto 155 · 180 · 200 gr FMJ, JHP, hard-cast lead 1,100–1,400 ft/s (5\") Woods carry against large animals, hunting, carry .45 ACP 185 · 200 · 230 gr 230 gr FMJ (\"ball\"), JHP, +P JHP 830–1,000 ft/s (5\") Carry, duty, competition .44 Magnum 180 · 240 · 300 gr JHP, JSP, hard-cast lead 1,200–1,600 ft/s (6\") up to 1,750 from a 20\" lever gun Woods carry against large animals, handgun and lever-action hunting; heavy recoil 5.7×28 mm 27 · 40 gr V-Max polymer tip, JHP, FMJ 1,650–1,750 ft/s (4.8\") Low-recoil, high-capacity pistols and PDWs Bullet type What it is Use FMJ (full metal jacket, \"ball\") Lead core covered by a copper jacket at the nose Training and practice; penetrates deeply and expands little JHP (jacketed hollow point) Cavity in the nose that opens on impact Carry and duty; expands to a larger diameter and is less likely to exit Bonded JHP Core bonded to the jacket so they stay together Duty loads that must go through car glass and heavy clothing Solid copper hollow point Monolithic copper, no lead core Defense where lead-free is preferred; keeps its weight through barriers LRN / LFN (lead round nose, lead flat nose) Bare or coated lead, no jacket Cheap practice, revolvers; hard-cast flat nose for deep penetration on large animals TMJ / total synthetic jacket Base fully covered, no exposed lead Indoor ranges that restrict exposed-lead ammunition Frangible Compressed copper powder that breaks apart on hard surfaces Steel targets at close range, shoot houses +P / +P+ Loaded to higher pressure for more velocity Only in guns rated for it; more recoil and wear. +P+ has no industry standard Carry ammunition Pick a JHP from a major maker that meets the FBI protocol of 12 to 18 inches of penetration in ballistic gelatin. Fire enough of it to prove it feeds, and replace carry rounds every year or after repeated chambering, which can push the bullet deeper into the case (setback) and raise pressure. Practice ammunition Match your practice FMJ to your carry load's weight (for example, 124 gr FMJ for 124 gr JHP carry) so recoil and point of impact are close. Avoid steel-cased ammunition in guns that haven't been proven with it."},{"id":"calc","h":"Ballistic calculator","x":"03 Ballistic calculator Handgun bullets are slow and drop quickly past 25 yards. Pick a load, set your zero and sight height (about 0.6\" for iron sights, 0.9–1.2\" for a slide-mounted red dot), and see the hold at any distance. Choose a carbine load to compare a 16-inch pistol-caliber carbine, or a lever gun for the magnum revolver cartridges."},{"id":"grip","h":"Grip","x":"04 Grip The grip controls where the muzzle goes during recoil and how much the trigger finger moves the gun. Get it right on the draw; there's no time to fix it after. Building the grip Web of the strong hand as high as the frame allows. Trigger finger free to move independently; the other three fingers wrap the front strap. Support palm fills the open panel on the left of the grip, heel of the hand against the frame. Support fingers wrap over the strong fingers, index finger pressed up under the trigger guard. Both thumbs forward, support thumb under the slide. Lock the support wrist slightly forward (canted down) to resist muzzle rise. Pressure Most instructors teach firm pressure from both hands, with the support hand doing more of the clamping (often cited as about 60/40 support to strong). That leaves the strong hand loose enough to let the trigger finger move without dragging the gun. Grip as hard as you can without shaking, and keep the same pressure through the whole string."},{"id":"stance","h":"Stance","x":"05 Stance Feet about shoulder-width, knees soft, weight on the balls of the feet. Lean the shoulders slightly forward of the hips so recoil doesn't push you back. Bring the gun up to your eye line; don't drop your head down to the gun. Arms extended without locking the elbows hard."},{"id":"sights","h":"Sight alignment and sight picture","x":"06 Sight alignment and sight picture Sight alignment is the front sight centered in the rear notch, tops level, equal light on each side. Sight picture is that alignment placed on the target. The eye can focus on only one distance at a time; for precision, it belongs on the front sight. Focus and alignment Front sight off by 7 yd 15 yd 25 yd 1/32\" (half a sight width) ≈1.2\" ≈2.6\" ≈4.3\" 1/16\" ≈2.4\" ≈5.2\" ≈8.7\" Assumes a 6.5-inch sight radius, typical of a full-size service pistol. Shorter guns magnify the error. Red dot on a pistol The dot rides on the slide, so there's nothing to align. Focus on the target and put the dot on it. The hard part is finding the dot on the presentation: practice a consistent grip and draw so the dot appears in the window every time. Keep iron sights tall enough to co-witness as a backup. How much precision Match the sight picture to the target. A small target at 25 yd needs a refined, crisp front sight. A large target at 5 yd needs only a flash of the front sight in the notch, or the dot somewhere on the target. Spend only the time the shot requires."},{"id":"trigger","h":"Trigger control","x":"07 Trigger control A handgun trigger has phases you can feel: slack (take-up), the wall, the break, overtravel, and the reset. Take up the slack, press smoothly through the wall, hold to the rear after the break, and let it out only to the reset. Trigger phases and the muzzle Finger placement Most shooters use the center of the pad of the index finger. Large hands on small guns may need more finger; double-action triggers often work better at the first joint. The test is whether the front sight moves as you press. Prepping the trigger At speed, shooters take up the slack while the gun is still coming onto the target, so only the final press through the wall is left when the sights arrive. Prep only once the muzzle is on target and you've decided to shoot."},{"id":"diagnose","h":"Diagnosing errors","x":"08 Diagnosing errors Shots that consistently miss in one direction usually come from a specific habit. This chart is for a right-handed shooter; mirror it left to right for a left-handed shooter. Treat it as a starting point and confirm with dry fire and shot calls."},{"id":"recoil","h":"Recoil and follow-through","x":"09 Recoil and follow-through The gun will move in recoil. What matters is that it moves the same way every time and returns to the same place. A firm grip and forward-leaning stance shorten the time the sights are off target, which sets how fast you can accurately fire the next shot. Muzzle rise, slide cycle, and sight tracking Track the sights. Watch the front sight (or dot) lift and come back down. Seeing that clearly is how you call shots at speed. Reset during recoil. Let the trigger out only to the reset while the gun is moving, so you're ready when the sights return. Don't fight it. Pushing down against recoil before the shot causes low hits. Let the grip and stance absorb it."},{"id":"draw","h":"The draw","x":"10 The draw A draw is a series of positions the gun passes through every time. Practicing it as steps first makes it consistent, then smooth, then fast. Four-step draw from an outside-the-waistband holster Step What happens Check 1 · Grip Strong hand takes a full firing grip on the gun in the holster. Support hand moves to the center of the chest. Web of the hand high; finger along the holster 2 · Clear and rotate Pull the gun straight up out of the holster, then rotate the muzzle toward the target with the wrist locked. Muzzle never points at your own body 3 · Meet Hands meet in front of the chest and the support hand completes the grip. Eyes stay on the target. Muzzle on the target line; finger still straight 4 · Extend Push the gun out along the line of sight, bringing it up to the eye. Finger enters the guard as the gun nears full extension. Sights arrive at the eye; trigger prepped Holstering reverses steps 4 to 1, slowly, finger straight, looking the gun into the holster."},{"id":"reloads","h":"Reloads","x":"11 Reloads Reload When Steps Typical time Emergency (slide-lock) Gun is empty, slide locked back Drop the empty magazine while the support hand gets a fresh one; seat firmly; release the slide with the slide stop or by racking 1.5–2.5 s Speed Gun still has a round chambered; you want a full magazine now Drop the partial magazine to the ground; insert a fresh one 1.2–2.0 s With retention / tactical Lull in the action; keep the partial magazine Get the fresh magazine first, swap, stow the partial in a pocket 3–5 s Reload technique Bring the gun back toward your face, in your field of view, muzzle up slightly. Watch the magazine well, not the magazine. Index finger along the front of the fresh magazine, pointing at the bullets. Insert the back of the magazine against the back of the well, then rock it in and seat it hard with the palm. Look for the target as soon as the magazine is seated."},{"id":"mal","h":"Malfunctions","x":"12 Malfunctions Type Signs Clearance Type 1: failure to fire Click instead of bang; magazine not seated, empty chamber, bad primer Tap the magazine hard, rack the slide fully, reassess and fire if needed Type 2: failure to eject Empty case caught in the port, often standing up (\"stovepipe\") Tap, rack with the gun rotated slightly so the case falls clear. Some teach sweeping the port; tap-rack works for both Type 1 and 2, so you don't have to diagnose. Type 3: double feed Slide out of battery; tap-rack doesn't fix it Lock the slide back, strip the magazine out, rack three times to clear, reload a fresh magazine, rack, reassess Squib Weak report, little or no recoil Stop. Don't fire again. Unload and check the bore for a lodged bullet."},{"id":"dry","h":"Dry practice","x":"13 Dry practice Dry-fire safety: no ammunition in the room. Unload, put all ammunition in another room, check the gun and magazines twice, pick a target with a backstop that would stop a round, and say aloud \"dry practice begins.\" When you finish, say \"dry practice is over\" before reloading, and don't take \"one more.\" Drill How Reps Wall drill Front sight on a blank wall from 6 inches; press without the sight moving 20 Coin or brass on the slide Balance a coin or empty case on the front sight; press without dropping it 20 Draw to first shot Full draw to a single press on a small target; use a timer with a par time you can just make 25 Reloads Slide-lock and speed reloads with dummy rounds or empty magazines 15 each Target transitions Three small targets on the wall; one press on each, eyes move first 15 Ten to fifteen minutes, four or five days a week, builds more skill than an occasional long range session. Use the range to confirm what dry practice built."},{"id":"comp","h":"Competition formats","x":"14 Competition formats Format What it tests Scoring Distances Bullseye (NRA Precision Pistol) One-handed precision; slow, timed, and rapid fire Points by ring 25–50 yd USPSA Speed, accuracy, and power on freestyle stages with movement Hit factor: points ÷ time 3–50 yd IDPA Defensive-style stages with cover, concealment garments, and set procedures Time plus penalties for points down 3–20 yd Steel Challenge Pure speed on fixed steel arrays Total time of best 4 of 5 strings 7–35 yd Bianchi Cup (NRA Action) Precision at speed, including movers Points with X-count 10–50 yd"},{"id":"drills","h":"Drills and standards","x":"15 Drills and standards Drill Setup Good / advanced Dot Torture 50 rounds on a sheet of 2-inch dots at 3 yd: slow fire, draws, one-handed strings 50/50 at 3 yd, then move back 5×5 5 shots in a 5-inch circle at 5 yd from the holster 5 s / 3 s Bill Drill From holster, 6 shots in the A-zone at 7 yd 3.0 s / 2.0 s Doubles From holster, 2 shots on an A-zone at 7 yd 1.8 s / 1.2 s El Presidente 3 targets at 10 yd, start facing away; turn, 2 each, reload, 2 each 10 s / 6 s, all A's Blake Drill 3 targets at 7 yd, 1 shot each, repeated 3 times (9 rounds) 4.0 s / 3.0 s FBI qualification 60 rounds from 3 to 25 yd with time limits on each stage Pass 80%; instructors 90%"},{"id":"errors","h":"Common errors","x":"16 Common errors Error What it does Fix Low grip More muzzle rise and slower return; gun shifts in the hand over a string Drive the web of the hand high on the draw every time Target focus on precise shots Front sight drifts without you noticing Hard front sight focus at distance or on small targets Flinch or anticipation Low hits Ball-and-dummy drill; dry fire Milking the grip All fingers tighten with the trigger; low-left hits Isolate the trigger finger; more support-hand pressure Riding the reset too fast Trigger released fully, slapped on the next press Practice reset in dry fire; feel the click Looking at the magazine on reloads Slow and fumbled insertion Eyes on the magazine well; gun in the line of sight Fast holstering Negligent discharges from clothing or fingers in the guard Slow, look it in, finger straight"}]},{"u":"kits.html","t":"Kits and Go-Bags","s":[{"id":"","h":"Kits and Go-Bags","x":"Preparedness · Kits Updated Nov 27, 2026 Kits and Go-Bags What to pack and where to keep it: a go-bag for leaving in a hurry, a home kit for riding it out, a vehicle kit, first-aid kits sized from pocket to household, and a schedule that keeps them all from quietly expiring. A checklist is a starting point, not a finished kit. Pack for your own household, climate, medical needs, and plans, and practice with what you carry. A kit you can't lift or don't know how to use won't help."},{"id":"principles","h":"Principles","x":"01 Principles Layers, not one bag Everyday carry on you, a go-bag by the door, a vehicle kit, and a home kit. Each one covers a different situation, and together they overlap. Weight is the limit Pack the go-bag, then walk a mile with it. As a guide, keep it under about 20% of your body weight. Water is the heaviest item and the most important. Skills over stuff Every item should be something you've used. A tourniquet you've never applied, a filter you've never run, or a radio you've never programmed is dead weight. One per person Each adult carries their own essentials. Kids can carry a small pack with water, a snack, a jacket, a whistle, and a card with contact details."},{"id":"go-bag","h":"Go-bag (72 hours)","x":"02 Go-bag (72 hours) For evacuating fast: fire, flood, gas leak, a road out of a storm's path. Grab it and go in under five minutes. Category Contents Water 2–3 liters carried, a filter or tablets (see Water ), a collapsible bottle Food Three days of no-cook food: bars, nut butter packets, jerky, dried fruit Shelter and clothing Rain shell, warm layer, hat, gloves, spare socks, emergency bivy or space blanket, a small tarp Light and fire Headlamp with spare batteries, lighter, ferro rod, waterproof matches, tinder First aid An IFAK plus personal medications for at least a week, glasses or contacts Navigation and comms Paper map of your area and evacuation routes, compass, phone charger and power bank, a small radio (see Analogue Comms ) Tools Fixed-blade or folding knife, multi-tool, duct tape, cordage, work gloves, N95 masks Hygiene Toothbrush, small soap, hand sanitizer, toilet paper, wet wipes, trash bags, feminine products Documents and cash See section 06"},{"id":"home","h":"Home kit (two weeks)","x":"03 Home kit (two weeks) For staying put through an outage, storm, or quarantine. It lives in one closet or bin so everyone knows where it is. Water and food Two weeks of each (see Water and Food Storage ), manual can opener, paper plates and utensils. Light and power LED lanterns and flashlights with spare batteries, a power station or generator plan (see Off-Grid Configurations ), and a battery or crank radio. Heat and shelter Sleeping bags or extra blankets, plastic sheeting and duct tape to close off rooms or broken windows, and an indoor-safe heat plan. Sanitation Buckets, heavy trash bags, cat litter, bleach, soap, and hand sanitizer. Safety Fire extinguisher, smoke and battery-powered CO alarms, a wrench for the gas valve (only if you smell gas; let the utility turn it back on), and work gloves. Medical The household first-aid kit, a supply of prescriptions, and anything powered (CPAP, oxygen) on a backup power plan."},{"id":"vehicle","h":"Vehicle kit","x":"04 Vehicle kit Getting moving again Jump pack or cables, tire inflator and plug kit, tow strap, a small shovel, traction mats or kitty litter in winter. Staying put safely Water, snacks, blanket or sleeping bag, warm clothes, flashlight, first-aid kit, phone charger, reflective triangles or flares. Getting home on foot Comfortable shoes, a small pack, water and a filter, a paper map, a light, and a rain layer: for when the car stays behind. Keep the tank half full Fuel stations need power to pump. Don't let the tank drop below half before a storm or in winter."},{"id":"first-aid","h":"First-aid kits","x":"05 First-aid kits Two different kits do two different jobs: one stops bleeding that kills in minutes, the other handles everything else. See Field Medical for how to use them. Kit Carry Contents IFAK (trauma) On you, in the go-bag, in the car Tourniquet, hemostatic or plain packing gauze, pressure bandage, chest seals, gloves, trauma shears, marker Everyday kit Go-bag, car, at work Adhesive bandages, gauze pads, tape, blister care, antiseptic wipes, tweezers, pain and fever reliever, antihistamine, anti-diarrheal, electrolyte packets Household kit At home Everything above in bulk, plus a thermometer, splint (SAM-type), elastic wraps, burn dressings, eye wash, a first-aid manual, and spare prescriptions"},{"id":"documents","h":"Documents and money","x":"06 Documents and money Copies of what matters IDs, insurance policies, deeds or lease, medical and prescription lists, vaccination records, and pet records: paper copies in a waterproof bag plus an encrypted USB drive. Contacts on paper A printed list of family phone numbers, an out-of-area contact, and your meeting places. Phones die. See the printable family plan card . Cash Small bills. Card readers and ATMs need power and a network. Spare keys House and vehicle keys in the go-bag."},{"id":"upkeep","h":"Keeping kits current","x":"07 Keeping kits current Check every kit twice a year. An easy reminder: when the clocks change. Rotate Water, food, batteries, and medications by their dates. Use the old ones at home. Swap for the season Winter layers and hand warmers in for the cold months; extra water and sun protection in for summer. Re-fit the people Kids outgrow clothes and shoes, prescriptions change, and new family members or pets need their own items. Test the gear Charge power banks and radios, run the filter, check the headlamp, and make sure the stove still lights."}]},{"u":"maintenance.html","t":"Cleaning and Maintenance","s":[{"id":"","h":"Cleaning and Maintenance","x":"Firearm training · Maintenance Updated Nov 27, 2026 Cleaning and Maintenance Keeping a gun reliable without wearing it out: making it safe first, a basic cleaning kit, how often to clean, where lubricant actually goes, parts that wear, checking your optic and mounts, function checks, and long-term storage for guns and ammunition. Your firearm's manual is the authority. Intervals and lubrication points below are general guidance and vary by make and model. Anything beyond field-stripping (trigger work, headspace, barrel or bolt replacement) belongs with a qualified gunsmith."},{"id":"safe","h":"Make it safe first","x":"01 Make it safe first No ammunition in the room Unload, then take every round out of the room you're cleaning in. Most \"it went off while I was cleaning it\" stories start with ammunition on the bench. Check twice, by eye and by touch Remove the magazine first, lock the action open, then look into the chamber and magazine well and feel with a finger. Do it again before you start. Lead and solvents Clean in a ventilated space, wear nitrile gloves and eye protection, don't eat or drink at the bench, and wash your hands afterwards. Keep kids and pets away from solvents and fouling. Muzzle discipline still applies Point it in a safe direction the whole time, even unloaded, and especially while reassembling and function-checking."},{"id":"kit","h":"The cleaning kit","x":"02 The cleaning kit Item Use Bore snake or one-piece coated rod with a jag Pulling patches and brushes through the bore. A one-piece rod is kinder to the bore than jointed steel sections. Bore brushes (bronze or nylon) in your calibers Loosening fouling. Never use stainless brushes in a bore. Patches Wet patch, brush, then dry patches until they come out clean enough. Solvent and lubricant (or a CLP) A CLP cleans, lubricates, and protects in one bottle and covers most needs. Nylon utility brush, picks, cotton swabs, rags Actions, slides, bolt faces, and corners. Bore guide (for rifles) Keeps the rod centered and solvent out of the action. Gloves, eye protection, a mat Protects you and catches small parts and springs."},{"id":"when","h":"How often","x":"03 How often Defensive and carry guns Check and wipe down weekly; carry guns collect lint and sweat. Clean and re-lube after every range session so the gun you rely on is always ready. After rain, salt air, or snow Same day. Moisture starts rust within hours on carbon steel. After corrosive surplus ammunition Some older military surplus ammunition uses corrosive primers. Clean the bore, bolt, and gas system the same day with hot water or a water-based cleaner to dissolve the salts, dry thoroughly, then oil. Oil-based solvent alone doesn't remove them. Don't over-clean A properly lubricated modern gun runs thousands of rounds between deep cleanings. Excess scrubbing, especially from the muzzle end, can wear the crown and hurt accuracy."},{"id":"how","h":"A basic cleaning","x":"04 A basic cleaning Step What to do 1 Make it safe (section 01), then field-strip as the manual shows. Don't go further than the manual's field strip. 2 Bore: a wet patch, let the solvent sit a few minutes, brush a few passes, then dry patches. Work from the chamber end when the design allows. 3 Bolt, slide, and action: brush carbon off the bolt face, extractor, and feed ramp; wipe the slide rails and frame. 4 Wipe everything dry, then lubricate only where section 05 says. 5 Reassemble and do a function check (section 08). 6 Wipe the outside with a lightly oiled cloth to remove fingerprints, which cause rust on blued steel."},{"id":"lube","h":"Where lubricant goes","x":"05 Where lubricant goes The rule: lubricate where metal slides on metal, which you can see from the shiny wear marks. Everywhere else gets a light protective film at most. Too much oil collects dirt and carbon. Platform Lubricate Keep dry AR-15 Generously on the bolt carrier rails, the cam pin, and the bolt and its gas rings; a light film on the charging handle. ARs run best wet. Firing pin channel, magazines Striker-fired pistol (Glock-type) A drop each on the slide rails, the outside of the barrel and barrel hood, and where the trigger bar meets the connector. Firing pin channel (oil there gums up and causes light strikes), magazines Hammer-fired pistol (1911, CZ, etc.) Slide and frame rails, barrel bushing or barrel hood, the disconnector and hammer contact points. Magazines, grips Pump shotgun Action bars, the bolt slide, and the magazine tube where the forend rides. Chamber, shells Bolt-action rifle Bolt lugs, lug recesses, and the cocking cam, with a little grease on the lugs. Chamber, trigger group (unless the manual says otherwise) Never oil ammunition or let solvent touch primers: it can deactivate them."},{"id":"wear","h":"Parts that wear","x":"06 Parts that wear Springs and small parts wear long before barrels and frames. Many makers publish round-count intervals; your manual's number wins. Keep a log of round counts and replace parts before they fail, not after. Recoil springs (pistols) The most common wear item. A weak spring causes failures to feed and battering. Many makers suggest replacing every few thousand rounds. Extractors and their springs Failures to extract, or brass ejecting erratically, often point here. Inexpensive to replace. Magazine springs and followers Replace when a magazine starts causing feeding problems. Number your magazines so you can track the bad one. AR bolts and gas rings Inspect the bolt lugs for cracks and the cam pin hole for wear at cleanings; gas rings wear gradually."},{"id":"optics","h":"Optics, lights, and mounts","x":"07 Optics, lights, and mounts Batteries on a calendar Replace red dot and light batteries on a schedule (an annual date is easy to remember), not when they die. Keep a spare in the grip or on the sling. Torque and witness marks Mount screws to the maker's torque spec with a torque driver, then paint a thin line across each screw head and the mount. If the line breaks, the screw moved. Clean lenses gently Blow off grit first, then a lens pen or microfiber. Rubbing sand across the glass scratches the coatings. Clean the light Carbon from shooting builds up on a weapon light's lens and cuts its output. Wipe it at every cleaning."},{"id":"function","h":"Function checks","x":"08 Function checks After reassembly, with the gun confirmed empty and pointed in a safe direction, check that every control works as designed. Example for an AR-15: Step Expected result Charge the rifle, put it on safe , press the trigger The hammer does not fall Switch to fire , press the trigger and hold it The hammer falls Still holding the trigger, charge the rifle again, then release the trigger slowly A click as the hammer resets onto the trigger Press the trigger again The hammer falls Every platform has its own function check in its manual. If any step fails, don't load it: take it to a gunsmith."},{"id":"storage","h":"Storage","x":"09 Storage Control humidity Aim for about 30–50% relative humidity in a safe. A rechargeable desiccant canister or a safe dehumidifier rod plus a cheap hygrometer tells you if it's working. Light oil film Wipe metal surfaces with a thin coat of protectant before long-term storage. Silicone-treated gun socks help; foam-lined cases hold moisture against steel and shouldn't be used for long-term storage. Ammunition Cool and dry, in its original boxes or sealed ammo cans with a desiccant pack, away from solvents and oils. Avoid big temperature swings. Secure it Locked and out of reach of children and unauthorized people. Many states have safe-storage laws; know yours."}]},{"u":"medical.html","t":"Field Medical","s":[{"id":"","h":"Field Medical","x":"Medical & fieldcraft · Medical Updated Nov 27, 2026 Field Medical Keeping a hurt person alive until real medical care takes over: working in the right order, stopping bleeding that kills in minutes, managing the airway and chest, carrying a kit you can reach, handling an unresponsive adult or child, protecting a casualty from cold, and moving them without making things worse. This is a reference, not training, and it is not medical advice. None of it replaces a hands-on course and a provider's judgment. Take a Stop the Bleed class, then a TCCC-based or wilderness first-aid course, practice with your own kit, and call emergency services the moment you can. In an emergency, do what a dispatcher or a more qualified person on scene tells you."},{"id":"order","h":"Work in order: MARCH","x":"01 Work in order: MARCH Most people who die from trauma before reaching a hospital die of bleeding, and most of that is survivable with simple actions taken fast. The hard part under stress is doing things in the order that saves the most lives, instead of fixing the first injury you see. The military sequence MARCH is the civilian's best shortcut: it puts the fastest killers first. Before any of it, make the scene safe. You are no use as a second casualty: stop the threat, move out of a line of fire or off a road, and put on gloves if you have them. Then move down the letters. Letter What it stands for What you do M Massive hemorrhage Stop life-threatening bleeding first: tourniquet, wound packing, or hard direct pressure. A Airway Open the airway; roll an unconscious but breathing person onto their side so they don't choke. R Respiration Look for chest wounds and trouble breathing; seal open chest wounds. C Circulation Recheck your bleeding control, look for other wounds, keep them still. H Hypothermia / head Keep the casualty warm; protect a possible head or spine injury. Say the letters out loud if it helps. The order is the whole point: a sealed chest does no good if an arm is still pumping blood onto the ground."},{"id":"bleed","h":"Massive hemorrhage","x":"02 Massive hemorrhage Life-threatening bleeding is blood that spurts, pools, soaks clothing fast, or won't stop. You have minutes. Which tool you reach for depends on where the wound is: a limb, or somewhere a tourniquet can't go. Tourniquet (arms and legs) For serious bleeding from an arm or leg, a tourniquet is first, not last. Put it high and tight on the limb if you're under threat or can't see the wound clearly, over clothing if you have to. When you can see the wound, put it 2–3 inches above it on bare skin, never over a joint. Tighten until the bleeding stops and you can no longer feel a pulse below it; it will hurt, which is normal. If one doesn't stop it, add a second just above the first. Write the time you applied it on the casualty or the tourniquet. Once it's on, leave it on for help to manage. Use a real windlass tourniquet (CAT or SOF-T); improvised ones rarely work. Wound packing (where a tourniquet can't go) For deep bleeding at the groin, armpit, neck, or a limb junction, pack the wound. Push hemostatic gauze (or plain gauze, or clean cloth) straight into the wound, right down onto the spot that's bleeding, and keep stuffing it in tightly until the wound is full. Then hold firm pressure without peeking: at least three minutes with hemostatic gauze, about ten minutes with plain gauze or cloth. If it's still bleeding, pack more and hold again. Pack over the bleeder, not just into the hole. Direct pressure and bundling Direct pressure stops or slows almost anything while you set up. Press hard, with your whole weight through a gloved hand or a wad of gauze, right on the wound. Don't trade working pressure for a loose wrap. When you do dress it, a pressure bandage holds the packing in and frees your hands. What not to do Don't waste time looking for a pressure point or elevating a limb instead of packing and pressing. Don't loosen a tourniquet to \"let blood through.\" Don't pour hemostatic powder into a deep wound where you can't pack it. Don't assume a small entry wound is the whole story: look for an exit and for blood pooling under the body."},{"id":"junction","h":"Junctional and chest wounds","x":"03 Junctional and chest wounds Some bleeding is in a place you can neither tourniquet nor fully pack. These are the wounds that kill fast and need rapid evacuation above all else. Junctional wounds The groin, the armpit, the base of the neck: a limb joins the trunk and a big vessel runs shallow. A limb tourniquet won't reach. Pack hard with hemostatic gauze and hold deep, firm pressure, aiming your push toward the bleeding vessel. For a neck wound, do not wrap anything all the way around the neck. These casualties need a surgeon soon. Open chest wounds A hole in the chest can let air into the space around the lung and collapse it. Cover any open chest wound with a vented chest seal. If you don't have one, use a non-vented seal or plastic taped down on all four sides; the old three-sided taped dressing is no longer recommended. Watch for breathing that gets worse after sealing: if it does, lift a corner of the seal for a second to let trapped air out, then reseal. Bleeding you can't see or reach, inside the chest, belly, or pelvis, is not something you fix on scene. Control what you can from the outside, keep the casualty still and warm, and get them to a hospital as fast as is safe."},{"id":"airway","h":"Airway and breathing","x":"04 Airway and breathing Once the bleeding is handled, make sure air is moving. An unconscious person's biggest airway threat is their own tongue and their own vomit. The breathing but unconscious casualty If they're breathing on their own but out cold and you don't suspect a spine injury that rules it out, roll them onto their side in the recovery position. It keeps the tongue off the airway and lets fluid drain instead of choking them. Recheck that they keep breathing. Nasal airway A nasopharyngeal airway (a soft lubricated tube through the nose) holds the airway open on someone who's unconscious but breathing and tolerates it. It's a trained skill and a kit item, not a guess; learn it before you carry it."},{"id":"ifak","h":"The IFAK","x":"05 The IFAK An individual first-aid kit is for the fast killers, carried where you can reach it with either hand. Keep it separate from your \"boo-boo\" kit of blister pads and painkillers: when you need the IFAK, you need it now, and you don't want to be digging past band-aids. Item For Windlass tourniquet (CAT / SOF-T) Limb bleeding; carry at least one, ideally two Hemostatic gauze Packing deep and junctional wounds Pressure / trauma bandage Holding packing in and keeping pressure Vented chest seals (pair) Open chest wounds, entry and exit Nitrile gloves Your protection and theirs Trauma shears Cutting away clothing to find wounds Tape and a marker Securing dressings; writing the tourniquet time Buy name-brand, in-date components, not a bargain pouch of look-alikes. Know where every item is by feel. A kit you've never opened is a kit you can't use under stress."},{"id":"cpr","h":"Unresponsive and choking","x":"06 Unresponsive and choking These are the medical emergencies, as opposed to trauma, most likely to land in front of you: someone collapses, or someone can't breathe at the table. Current guidance changed recently, so check the date on whatever you were taught. Unresponsive, not breathing: CPR No response and no normal breathing means start CPR and get someone calling emergency services and finding an AED. Push hard and fast in the center of the chest, about two inches deep for an adult, letting the chest come all the way back up between pushes, around 100 to 120 a minute. Don't stop for long. Use the AED the moment it arrives and follow its voice prompts. Infants and small children For an infant, current guidance is to compress with two thumbs (hands wrapping the chest) or the heel of one hand, not two fingers, about a third of the depth of the chest. Children and infants far more often stop breathing from an airway or breathing problem than from the heart, so rescue breaths matter: take a child or infant CPR class, because the technique differs from an adult's. Choking If a choking adult or child is conscious and can't cough, speak, or breathe, current guidance is to alternate five back blows between the shoulder blades with five abdominal thrusts, and keep repeating until the object comes out or they go unconscious. For an infant, alternate five back blows with five chest thrusts using the heel of one hand; do not use abdominal thrusts on an infant. If they go limp, lower them down, call emergency services, and start CPR. The specifics above reflect the 2025 CPR and first-aid guidance, which added back blows for choking and changed infant technique. Guidance is revised periodically. A current, hands-on CPR course is the only way to actually be ready; reading it is not."},{"id":"environ","h":"Cold and heat","x":"07 Cold and heat Weather finishes casualties that the injury alone wouldn't. A hurt, bleeding, still person loses heat fast, even in mild conditions, and cold blood clots poorly, which makes the bleeding worse. Hypothermia Treat every serious trauma casualty as a hypothermia risk: get them off the cold ground, strip wet clothing, and wrap them in whatever holds heat, head included. Shivering, fumbling, and slurred speech are early signs; as it deepens, shivering stops and they get confused and drowsy. Rewarm gently, add heat at the core and armpits, and handle them carefully, because a very cold heart is easy to tip into a bad rhythm. Heat illness Heat exhaustion is heavy sweating, weakness, nausea, and cramps: move them to shade, cool them, and give water if they're fully alert. Heat stroke, where they stop making sense, stop sweating, and run hot, is a true emergency: cool them aggressively, ideally in cold water, and get help now. Don't wait to see if it passes."},{"id":"move","h":"Moving and handing off","x":"08 Moving and handing off Treat what kills in place first, then move. Moving a casualty is covered in detail on the Fieldcraft page: the drags, carries, and improvised litters, and when the move is worth the cost. Finish your bleeding control before you move anyone; a carry that undoes a tourniquet or a pack is a step backward. Keep a possible spine injury in line if the situation lets you, but don't let fear of moving someone leave them in a fire or a flooding car. Write it down: the time each tourniquet went on, what you gave, how they've changed. The next provider will ask. Hand off cleanly. Tell arriving help what happened, what you found, and what you did, in that order, then get out of their way."},{"id":"train","h":"Train for real","x":"09 Train for real Everything here is muscle memory you don't have until you've done it with your hands, under a clock, on a mannequin and a training tourniquet. Build it in this order: Stop the Bleed. A short, widely available class on tourniquets, packing, and pressure. Start here. CPR / AED. A current certification course, with a separate infant and child class if you have kids around. TCCC-based or wilderness first aid. The next step up: MARCH, airway, chest, and working away from help. Practice your own kit. Open it, lay it out, and run the motions until you can find each item blind. Replace what expires."}]},{"u":"modifications.html","t":"Weapon Modifications","s":[{"id":"","h":"Weapon Modifications","x":"Firearm training · Modifications Updated Nov 27, 2026 Common Weapon Modifications The modifications that change how a gun actually works in use: short barrels, suppressors, weapon lights, aiming lasers (visible and infrared), and optics. For each one, what it does, what it costs you, and how it behaves on the AR-15, the pump shotgun, the handgun, and the bolt rifle. The legal section describes U.S. federal law as of October 2026, which changed significantly this year and is still in litigation. State and local law is separate and often stricter. This is not legal advice: confirm the current rules with ATF, your state, and an attorney before you buy, build, or travel."},{"id":"law","h":"The legal picture","x":"01 The legal picture Item Federal definition Short-barreled rifle (SBR) A rifle with a barrel under 16 inches, or an overall length under 26 inches Short-barreled shotgun (SBS) A shotgun with a barrel under 18 inches, or an overall length under 26 inches Silencer (suppressor) Any device for silencing, muffling, or diminishing the report of a portable firearm, including its parts What changed in 2026 January 1: the federal tax on making or transferring an SBR, SBS, suppressor, or \"any other weapon\" dropped from $200 to $0. Machine guns and destructive devices still carry the $200 tax. August 5: a federal court in Texas ( Silencer Shop Foundation v. ATF ) held that, with no tax left, the registration and approval requirements for those items were no longer supported by Congress's taxing power. The order covered the plaintiffs. October 9: ATF announced it would no longer require approval or registration for SBRs, SBSs, suppressors, and AOWs from anyone, as a matter of enforcement discretion. What didn't change The National Firearms Act is still law. The October policy is enforcement discretion and can be reversed, and the case is still being litigated. State and local law. Several states and D.C. ban civilian suppressors, SBRs, or both, and many allow them only when federally registered. Background checks at dealers, prohibited-person rules, and dealer recordkeeping under the Gun Control Act. Moving an SBR or SBS across state lines still needs an approved ATF Form 5320.20. Under ATF's November 16, 2026 guidance, file it through eForms only for firearms already registered; for an unregistered SBR or SBS, fill in the paper form, sign it in ink, and email it to NFAFax@atf.gov. Don't put registered and unregistered firearms on the same form. Suppressors don't need a Form 5320.20 (an SBR host does). Machine guns and destructive devices remain fully regulated. Before you buy or build: check your state's law, check ATF's current guidance, and keep records of how and when you acquired the item. If the federal policy reverses, you will want to be able to register, and a mistake about state law can be a felony. Pistol stabilizing braces: ATF's 2023 rule treating many braced pistols as SBRs was vacated by a federal court in 2024. Braced AR pistols are again sold as pistols; a brace used as a stock is a practical, not a legal, distinction that may still matter under state law."},{"id":"short","h":"Short barrels (SBR / SBS)","x":"02 Short barrels (SBR / SBS) A shorter barrel makes the gun handier in vehicles, hallways, and doorways, and lighter at the muzzle so it swings and stops faster. In exchange, you lose velocity, gain blast and flash, and sometimes reliability. 5.56 barrel length 55 gr M193 velocity (approx.) Notes 20\" 3,240 ft/s Rifle-length gas, full ballistic performance 16\" 3,050 ft/s Standard carbine; the shortest legal rifle barrel without a pinned muzzle device 14.5\" 2,990 ft/s M4 length; legal at 16\" only with a permanently attached muzzle device 11.5\" 2,800 ft/s Common SBR length; noticeably louder 10.3\" 2,700 ft/s Mk 18 length; needs careful gas tuning 7.5\" 2,400 ft/s Severe blast; fragmentation range of most 5.56 loads drops sharply AR-15 The most common SBR. Under about 12 inches, many loads lose the velocity needed to expand or fragment reliably past 50–100 yards. Shorter gas systems run harder; adjustable gas blocks and heavier buffers help. A 300 Blackout upper keeps more of its performance in a short barrel. Pump shotgun A 14-inch barrel is the common SBS length. Buckshot patterns change little with barrel length, but magazine capacity drops with the shorter tube, and the muzzle is easier to short-stroke when the gun is light. A Mossberg 590 with a 14-inch barrel holds about 5 shells in the tube, against 8 for a 20-inch 590A1. Handgun Handguns are already short. Adding a vertical foregrip to a pistol makes it an \"any other weapon\" (AOW); adding a stock makes it an SBR. A pistol-caliber carbine with a short barrel gains little velocity but is much easier to shoot accurately than a pistol. Bolt rifle A 16–18 inch bolt gun is common and legal without any registration. Shorter is rarely worth it: magnum and long-range cartridges lose much of their performance."},{"id":"suppressors","h":"Suppressors","x":"03 Suppressors A suppressor traps and slows the expanding powder gas at the muzzle. It reduces the report by roughly 20–35 decibels, cuts most of the flash, and lowers recoil. It does not make a gun silent: a suppressed 5.56 rifle is still about as loud as a jackhammer, and any supersonic bullet still makes a sharp crack along its path. Exposure limits for impulse noise are usually set at 140 dB, and many suppressed rifles only get down to that level, so keep wearing ear protection when you can. Platform Typical peak, unsuppressed Suppressed What changes AR-15, 5.56 ≈165 dB ≈130–140 dB More gas back through the action: faster cycling, more fouling, gas in the shooter's face. An adjustable gas block or heavier buffer helps. Point of impact usually shifts slightly; re-zero with the can on. Pump shotgun ≈155–160 dB ≈135–140 dB Shotgun suppressors are large and heavy and need a threaded barrel or choke adapter. A pump action cycles normally because it isn't gas-operated. Handgun, 9mm ≈160 dB ≈125–135 dB Most tilting-barrel pistols need a booster (Nielsen device) to cycle. Taller sights are needed to see over the can. Subsonic 147 gr ammunition is quietest. Holster options are limited. Bolt rifle ≈165–170 dB ≈135–140 dB The best match: no gas comes back at the shooter, and the action is quiet. Adds 6–9 inches and some weight at the muzzle. Heat from the can causes mirage in the scope; a cover helps. These figures are measured near the muzzle. On a semi-automatic rifle like an AR-15, gas escaping the ejection port plus the supersonic crack of the bullet commonly keep the level above 140 dB at the shooter's ear . Wear hearing protection at the range even when suppressed. Mounting Direct-thread mounts are lighter and simpler. Quick-detach mounts let one can move between guns but add length and cost. Match the thread pitch (1/2×28 for most 5.56 and 9mm, 5/8×24 for most .30 caliber) and caliber rating, and check alignment before shooting to avoid a baffle strike. Heat A suppressor can reach several hundred degrees after a few magazines. Burns and melted gear are common. Use a cover, and don't lean the gun against anything with the can still hot."},{"id":"lights","h":"Weapon lights","x":"04 Weapon lights A light mounted on a defensive gun is for one job: identifying what you're about to shoot at. Use a handheld light for searching and navigating. A weapon light on in your hand is a muzzle pointed at everything it lights. Lumens and candela Lumens measure total output; candela measures the intensity of the beam's hotspot, which decides how far it reaches and how well it punches through shadows and smoke. A handgun needs about 500–1,000 lumens; a rifle used outdoors benefits from 10,000+ candela more than from more lumens. Activation Momentary on is the default: light on when you need it, off when you move. A pressure switch or tail cap must be reachable without changing your grip. Practice so the light comes on before you need it, not after. Platform Where it goes Notes AR-15 Forward on the handguard, at the 10–11 or 1–2 o'clock position Mount it far enough forward that the barrel and muzzle device don't cast a shadow. Thumb activation on the support side. Pump shotgun Integrated light forend, or a clamp/M-LOK mount on the forend The light has to move with the pumping hand; switches on the receiver are hard to reach mid-cycle. Handgun Accessory rail under the slide Requires a holster built for that exact gun and light. Activate with the support thumb or trigger-finger switch, never by pressing near the trigger. Bolt rifle Forend rail or barrel/scope clamp mount A clamp mount on a free-floated barrel can shift the point of impact. Mount it to the stock or scope instead."},{"id":"lasers","h":"Visible lasers","x":"05 Visible lasers A red or green aiming laser shows where the bullet will land at the distance it's zeroed. It helps when you can't get your eye behind the sights: around cover, from awkward positions, wearing a gas mask, or shooting one-handed. It doesn't replace sights. Daylight washes it out, and chasing the dot under stress can make the shooter slower. Zero it for the distance you'll use it, usually 10–25 yards for a handgun or 25–50 yards for a long gun. The laser sits below or beside the bore, so the dot and point of impact only agree at the zero distance. Green is easier to see in daylight; red uses less battery. On a handgun, grip- or frame-activated lasers come on as you grip the gun. On long guns they're usually combined with the light in a single unit. Never point a laser at an aircraft. It's a federal crime (18 U.S.C. § 39A), and even a few milliwatts can blind a pilot."},{"id":"ir","h":"Infrared lasers and illuminators","x":"06 Infrared lasers and illuminators Infrared aiming lasers and illuminators work only with night vision devices; the beam is invisible to the naked eye. Under night vision, the shooter can aim with the IR laser while the device is on their head, without putting a rifle optic to their eye, and the IR illuminator lights the area for the night vision without visible light. Where it works Mainly the AR-15 and other carbines, used with a helmet-mounted night vision device. Some handgun units exist; on shotguns and bolt rifles it's rare. Civilian units are lower power than military versions. Cautions Anyone else with night vision can see your laser and trace it back to you. IR lasers can damage eyes without the target or bystanders seeing anything. Treat it like the muzzle. Zero the IR laser to the rifle's optic, and confirm it under night vision before relying on it."},{"id":"optics","h":"Optics","x":"07 Optics An optic puts the aiming point on the same focal plane as the target, so you can look at the threat instead of focusing on a front sight. Which kind is right depends on distance, light, and how much weight and battery dependence you'll accept. Type How it works Strengths Costs Best for Red dot (tube or open emitter) An LED reflects a dot off a coated lens Fast, light, unlimited eye relief, both eyes open; battery life of years on many models 1× only; with astigmatism the dot can look smeared or starred Carbines and shotguns inside about 200 yards Holographic A laser projects a recorded reticle, usually a 68 MOA ring around a 1 MOA dot Large, fast ring for close work; the reticle stays usable through a damaged window; many shooters with astigmatism see it more cleanly Heavier, and battery life in hundreds of hours instead of years Close-range carbines, especially with a magnifier or night vision Prism Glass prism with an etched reticle, fixed power from 1× to about 4× The reticle is visible with the battery dead or no battery at all; compact; a diopter on some corrects focus for your eye Short eye relief and a tight eye box, fixed magnification Durable all-round rifles; a good choice for astigmatism LPVO (1–6×, 1–8×, 1–10×) A variable riflescope that goes down to 1× One optic for close and far; precise holds and ranging at distance Heavy (often 1.5 lb or more mounted), tight eye box, and slower than a dot at 1× Rifles used both up close and past 300 yards Magnifier (3–6×) A flip-to-side scope behind a red dot or holographic Adds magnification only when you want it Extra weight and rail space; the dot gets no smaller, so it covers more of a distant target Upgrading an existing dot for identifying targets at distance Riflescope (3–15×, 5–25×) Variable magnification with turrets for dialing elevation and wind Precise aiming and dialing at long range; parallax adjustment Slow and narrow up close; heavy Bolt rifles and precision semi-autos Dot size A 2 MOA dot covers about 2 inches at 100 yards and allows precise shots on a rifle. Larger dots (3–6.5 MOA) are faster to find on a pistol. A circle-dot reticle gives a big reference for speed and a small dot for precision. Mount height and co-witness Absolute co-witness (about 1.41\" over an AR's rail) puts the iron sights in the center of the window; lower 1/3 (about 1.54\") puts them in the bottom of it. Taller 1.93\" mounts suit a heads-up posture, helmets, and night vision, but add mechanical offset: the bullet hits farther below the dot up close. Eye relief and eye box Dots and holographics have unlimited eye relief. Prisms (about 1.5–2.5\") and LPVOs (about 3.5–4\") need your eye in one spot; if it isn't, the picture blacks out at the edges. Mount them so the full picture appears with your head in its natural place on the stock. First or second focal plane A first-focal-plane reticle grows and shrinks with the magnification, so its holds are correct at every power. A second-focal-plane reticle stays the same size, which keeps it bold at 1×, but its holds are correct at only one power, usually the highest. Match the units Use a reticle and turrets in the same unit: a mil reticle with 0.1-mil clicks, or an MOA reticle with 1/4-MOA clicks. Mixing them means converting every correction under pressure. See the Ballistics Calculator for holds in both. Mounting and batteries Torque to the maker's numbers (often 15–25 in-lb for ring screws and 35–65 in-lb for a rail clamp), level the reticle, and mark screws with a paint pen so you can see if they move. Change batteries on a fixed date each year, carry a spare, and keep iron sights on a defensive gun. AR-15 A red dot or holographic for most defensive use, an LPVO when you need distance, and a prism for a fixed, battery-proof setup. Set the mount height so your head stays upright. Pump shotgun A small red dot on the receiver helps most with slugs past 50 yards. Ghost-ring irons are the durable alternative. Magnification adds little. Ha"},{"id":"other","h":"Other common changes","x":"08 Other common changes Change What it does Watch for Compensator or muzzle brake Redirects gas to reduce recoil and muzzle rise Much louder for anyone beside you; harsh indoors Aftermarket trigger Lighter, cleaner break Reliability and legal scrutiny on a defensive gun; install correctly and test Sling Carries the gun and stabilizes positions Snag points; practice transitions with it on Sidesaddle / extended tube More ready ammunition on a shotgun Weight and balance; a tube extension needs a longer barrel"},{"id":"matrix","h":"By platform","x":"09 By platform AR-15 Pump shotgun Handgun Bolt rifle Short barrel Common 10.3–11.5\" Common 14\" N/A AOW or SBR if foregrip or stock added Rare 16–18\" is enough Suppressor Common tune the gas Uncommon bulky Common needs a booster Ideal Weapon light Standard Standard on the forend Common matching holster Situational Visible laser Optional Optional Useful for odd positions Rare Optic Standard dot, prism, or LPVO Common small dot for slugs Common slide-mounted dot Standard variable scope IR laser Primary use with night vision Rare Specialized Rare"}]},{"u":"offgrid.html","t":"Off-Grid Environment Configurations","s":[{"id":"","h":"Off-Grid Environment Configurations","x":"Preparedness · Power Updated Nov 27, 2026 Off-Grid Environment Configurations Keeping a home running when the grid is down: knowing your loads, battery backup from a portable power station to a whole-home system, portable and standby generators, the fuels that run them and how long each keeps, connecting it all to the house safely, recharging from solar, heating and cooling without the grid, and four configurations from a single room to a fully off-grid property. This is a reference, not electrical or fire-code advice. Connecting a generator or battery to house wiring is work for a licensed electrician and usually needs a permit. Generators make carbon monoxide, which kills quietly: never run one indoors, in a garage, or near a window, door, or vent, and keep battery-powered CO alarms in the house. Check your local fire code before storing fuel."},{"id":"think","h":"Three numbers","x":"01 Three numbers Every backup power decision comes down to three numbers about the things you need to keep running. Work them out first, then buy equipment to match. Buying a generator or battery first and hoping it covers the house is how people end up with a freezer that won't start or a battery that's flat by midnight. Running watts What everything you want on at the same time draws while it's running. This sets the continuous output your inverter or generator needs. Starting (surge) watts Motors and compressors (refrigerators, freezers, well and sump pumps, air conditioners) draw two to three times their running watts for a second or two as they start. Your source has to carry everything already running plus the biggest motor starting. Energy per day Watts multiplied by hours, in kilowatt-hours (kWh). A 100 W load for 10 hours is 1 kWh. This sets battery size and how much fuel you burn. A typical U.S. home uses around 29 kWh a day; a backup plan usually covers a fraction of that. Cut the list The cheapest kilowatt-hour is the one you don't need. Decide what's critical (refrigeration, water, heat, medical devices, communications, a few lights) and what waits for the grid (electric heat, water heater, dryer, oven, central air)."},{"id":"loads","h":"Know your loads","x":"02 Know your loads Typical figures for common household loads. Your own appliances' nameplates, or a plug-in watt meter, are better than any table: read them and use your numbers. Load Running watts Starting watts Typical energy per day Refrigerator 100–250 W while the compressor runs 800–1,500 W 1–2 kWh Chest freezer 50–150 W while running 500–800 W 0.5–1 kWh Well pump, ½–1 HP (240 V) 750–1,500 W 2,000–4,000 W Depends on water use; 1–3 kWh Sump pump, ⅓–½ HP 800–1,000 W 1,300–2,200 W Depends on rain and water table Gas furnace blower 300–800 W Up to 2× running 2–6 kWh in cold weather Window air conditioner 500–1,200 W 1,200–2,500 W 4–10 kWh in summer Central air, 3 ton 3,000–4,000 W 6,000–9,000 W (less with a soft starter) 20+ kWh on a hot day Space heater 1,500 W None Very high: avoid on backup power Electric water heater 4,500 W None Very high: avoid on backup power Microwave 1,000–1,500 W None Short bursts; under 0.5 kWh CPAP (no heated humidifier) 30–60 W None 0.3–0.5 kWh a night Internet modem and router 10–20 W None 0.3–0.5 kWh HF radio (100 W) transmitting 250–300 W while transmitting None Low; receiving draws far less Laptop, phones, handheld radios 10–65 W each while charging None Under 0.3 kWh LED bulb 8–12 W None About 0.05 kWh for 5 hours Television 50–150 W None 0.2–0.6 kWh Load estimator Set how many of each you'll run and for how many hours a day. Refrigeration and pumps cycle on and off, so their daily energy uses an average draw, not the running figure. The result is a planning estimate, not a design: check it against your own appliances. Load How many Hours a day Days on battery"},{"id":"batteries","h":"Battery backup","x":"03 Battery backup Battery power stations, often sold as \"solar generators\" when they're bundled with panels, have two big advantages over a fuel generator: they're silent , and they need no fuel . No exhaust, so they can run indoors and right next to a bed; no fuel to buy, store, rotate, or run out of; no engine to start or maintain; and nothing to hear from outside the house. Paired with solar, they refill themselves every sunny day. The trade-off is that they store a fixed amount of energy: when it's gone, you need sun, a generator, or the grid to refill it. Type Typical capacity Output Best for Portable power station 0.5–5 kWh Built-in AC outlets, USB and 12 V; 300–3,600 W Phones, radios, CPAP, router, lights, a fridge for part of a day. Plug-and-play, no installation. Whole-home battery system About 10–15 kWh per unit, stackable Wired into the house panel; typically 5–11+ kW continuous Critical-load panels or the whole house, switching over automatically, usually paired with solar. 12 V DC system (DIY or kit) 1–10+ kWh of LiFePO4 Fused 12 V DC plus an inverter if needed Radio stations, lighting, and small loads; vehicles, sheds and cabins. Chemistry LiFePO4 (lithium iron phosphate) The usual choice for home backup now. Thousands of charge cycles, most of the capacity usable, stable and hard to set on fire compared with other lithium types. Most can't safely charge below freezing unless they have built-in heaters: keep them inside, or buy a self-heating model. NMC lithium Lighter for its capacity, so common in smaller portables, but fewer cycles and less tolerant of heat and abuse than LiFePO4. Lead-acid (AGM, flooded) Cheap and familiar, but heavy, and you should only use about half the rated capacity or the battery wears out fast. Flooded batteries vent hydrogen while charging and need ventilation and topping up. Sizing rule Battery kWh needed is roughly your daily kWh times the number of days, divided by about 0.8 to cover inverter losses and keep some reserve. The estimator above does this for you. Common brands These are the brands you'll see most on Amazon and in the big-box stores. Every one makes LiFePO4 models, sells matching solar panels, and has a range from small to home-backup size. Model lines change every year, so treat the models below as examples of each size and check the maker's current spec sheet before you buy. Brand Small (about 250–800 Wh) Mid (about 1–2 kWh) Home backup (3+ kWh) Anker SOLIX C300, C800 series C1000 (about 1,056 Wh, 1,800 W), F2000 F3800 (about 3,840 Wh, 6,000 W, 120/240 V), expandable with extra batteries and a home panel BLUETTI EB3A, AC70 AC180; AC200L (about 2,048 Wh, 2,400 W, expandable); Elite 200 AC300 and AC500 with B300 battery modules; Apex home systems EcoFlow River series DELTA 2 (about 1,024 Wh, 1,800 W); DELTA 3 series DELTA Pro series with the Smart Home Panel for whole-circuit backup Jackery Explorer 300, 500 Explorer 1000 v2 (about 1,070 Wh, 1,500 W); 1000 Plus Explorer 2000 Plus and 3000 series, expandable Goal Zero Yeti 500 class Yeti 1500 class Yeti 6000 class with a home integration kit What to compare Capacity in watt-hours (how long it runs), continuous and surge output in watts (what it can start and run), LiFePO4 chemistry and rated cycle count, solar input in watts and its voltage range, and whether it can add expansion batteries. For home backup Look for a UPS or pass-through mode (switches over in milliseconds when the grid drops), 120/240 V output if you need a well pump or other 240 V load, and a transfer switch or home panel kit from the same maker for hardwired circuits."},{"id":"generators","h":"Generators","x":"04 Generators A generator turns fuel into power for as long as you can feed it, so it covers long outages and heavy loads that a battery can't. The trade-offs are noise, exhaust, fuel storage, and maintenance. Type Typical output Strengths Weaknesses Inverter portable 1,000–7,000 W Quiet, fuel-efficient at light load (it throttles down), clean power that's safe for electronics, often parallel-capable Costs more per watt; smaller units can't start a well pump or central air Open-frame portable 3,000–12,000 W Lots of watts for the money; can start big motors Loud, runs at full speed whatever the load, so it burns more fuel; rougher power on some models Home standby 10–26+ kW Permanently installed, runs on natural gas or propane, starts itself with an automatic transfer switch, exercises itself weekly Expensive, needs permits and professional installation, and depends on a gas supply or a large propane tank Sizing Pick a generator whose running watts cover your running load with some headroom (plan to use no more than about 80% continuously), and whose starting watts cover the running load plus your largest motor starting. Dual and tri-fuel Many portables run on gasoline and propane, and some on natural gas as well. Expect roughly 10–20% less output on propane or natural gas than on gasoline. Being able to switch fuels is worth a lot in a long outage. Run it less A generator burns fuel even at light load. Pairing it with a battery lets you run the generator hard for a few hours to charge the battery and run big loads, then shut it off and run quietly on the battery. That saves fuel, noise, and engine hours. Fuel burn A portable generator at half load typically burns somewhere around 0.3–0.8 gallons of gasoline an hour depending on size. Check your model's runtime figures and plan fuel from them, not from a guess."},{"id":"fuel","h":"Fuel types","x":"05 Fuel types The fuel decides how long you can keep going, how you store it, and what happens in cold weather. Approximate energy content is per U.S. gallon (natural gas per 100 cubic feet). Fuel Energy Storage life Cold weather Notes Gasoline About 114,000–120,000 BTU 3–6 months untreated; about 1–2 years with stabilizer, longer if ethanol-free Starts well Easy to buy and the most common generator fuel. Degrades and gums up carburetors; ethanol blends absorb water. Very flammable vapor: store in approved cans, outside the living space. Diesel About 137,000 BTU About 6–12 months; longer with stabilizer and biocide, kept cool, dry and full Gels in hard cold unless it's a winter blend or treated Most energy per gallon and much less flammable than gasoline. Used by larger and longer-running generators. Water and microbial growth in the tank are the main problems. Propane About 91,500 BTU Doesn't go bad Pressure drops in deep cold, which limits what small tanks can deliver Stores indefinitely in cylinders or a large tank, burns clean, and keeps carburetors clean. Less energy per gallon, so you burn more of it. Cylinders need periodic requalification; never store them indoors. Natural gas About 103,000 BTU per 100 cu ft No storage: piped in Reliable Unlimited runtime while the utility keeps gas flowing, which is why standby generators use it. Supply can be shut off after earthquakes, floods, or line damage, and output is lower than on gasoline. Kerosene About 135,000 BTU Several years if sealed and kept clean Good Mainly for kerosene heaters and lamps, not most generators. Use only heaters rated for indoor use, with ventilation and a CO alarm. Rotate, don't hoard Use stored gasoline and diesel in vehicles and replace it on a schedule (write the fill date on every can) so the reserve is always fresh. Storage rules Fire codes often limit how much gasoline you can keep at a home and where. Store fuel in approved containers in a detached shed or well-ventilated space away from ignition sources, never in the house or attached living space. Plan the quantity Work out gallons per day from your generator's runtime figures at your expected load, then multiply by the days you want to cover. Remember fuel stations need power to pump too. Have a second fuel A dual-fuel generator with both gasoline and propane on hand means one fuel running short or going stale doesn't stop you."},{"id":"connect","h":"Connecting to the house","x":"06 Connecting to the house There are three ways to get backup power to your loads. Only two of them are safe. Extension cords The simplest: run heavy-duty outdoor cords from the generator or power station straight to the appliances. Fine for a fridge, a freezer, and some lights. Can't reach hardwired loads like a well pump or furnace. Use cords rated for the load, and don't run them through doors or windows that then can't close. Transfer switch or interlock An electrician installs a power inlet outside and either a transfer switch or an interlock kit on the main panel. The house is disconnected from the grid before the generator feeds it, so you can run hardwired circuits safely. Required by electrical code for connecting a generator to house wiring. Never backfeed Never plug a generator into a wall outlet with a double-male cord. It sends power back out through your meter into the utility lines, where it can kill a lineworker or a neighbor, and it can burn up your wiring or generator when the grid comes back. Whole-home batteries Installed systems include their own disconnect and switch over automatically. They're tied into the panel by an installer, usually on a backed-up subpanel of critical circuits."},{"id":"solar","h":"Solar and recharging","x":"07 Solar and recharging Solar is what turns a battery from a one-time reserve into something that keeps going: silent, fuel-free power every day the sun is up. It only produces when the sun is up and much less in winter, under cloud, and in shade. What to expect A panel delivers roughly its rated watts times 3–5 \"peak sun hours\" a day, less losses. A 400 W panel is good for about 1–2 kWh on a decent day, and much less on a gray winter day. Portable panels Folding panels plug into most power stations. Enough to keep phones, radios, a router, and lights going; usually not enough to run a fridge and freezer indefinitely. Charge controller Panels feed a battery through a charge controller (MPPT is the efficient type). Power stations and whole-home systems have one built in; a 12 V DIY system needs its own. Grid-tied panels go dark Ordinary grid-tied solar shuts off during an outage for lineworker safety, even in full sun. To run from your panels in an outage, the system needs a battery or an inverter with a backup or islanding mode. Panel brands Type Common brands Use Folding portable panels (100–400 W) Anker SOLIX, BLUETTI, EcoFlow, Jackery SolarSaga, Renogy Set out in the sun when needed and plugged into a power station; pack away between uses Rigid panels (100–450 W) Renogy, plus the power-station brands' own rigid panels Permanent mounting on a roof, shed, or ground rack for a 12 V system or a power station that stays put Panels and stations from the same brand plug together without adapters. Mixing brands works if the panel's open-circuit voltage stays inside the station's solar input range and you have the right connector (most use MC4 or XT60, some Anderson or DC7909). Two panels wired in series double the voltage: check that against the input limit first."},{"id":"climate","h":"Heating and cooling","x":"08 Heating and cooling Heat and cooling are the biggest loads in most homes, and the hardest to run from a battery. A 1,500 W space heater empties a 1 kWh power station in about 40 minutes. The answer off-grid is to stop heating and cooling air with electricity where you can: store heat in mass, use the sun and evaporating water directly, and save electric power for fans, pumps, and the few devices that make heat efficiently. Heating Method How it works Power or fuel Watch out for Wood stove Burns wood in a sealed firebox; the stove and flue radiate heat. Modern EPA-certified stoves burn cleaner and need less wood. Wood; no electricity Chimney fires (clean the flue), CO, clearances to walls. Needs a seasoned, stored wood supply. Masonry heater / rocket mass heater A short, very hot fire heats a large mass of brick, stone, or cob, which gives the heat back slowly for 12–24 hours. Small amounts of wood Heavy; needs a floor that can carry it. Owner-built rocket mass heaters may not meet code or satisfy insurers. Sand battery Surplus solar electricity runs heating elements buried in an insulated bin of sand or crushed stone. The hot sand holds the heat for hours to days, and a fan or water loop draws it out when needed. Solar surplus, stored as heat Proven at utility scale; home versions are experimental and DIY. See the note below. Solar air heater and rock bed A dark, glazed collector on a south wall heats air; a small fan blows it into the room or through a bed of rock that stores it for the evening. Sun, plus a few watts for the fan Daytime only unless you add storage. Simple to build; the old, proven way to store solar heat. Passive solar South-facing windows let winter sun onto a dark masonry floor or wall (a Trombe wall), which releases the heat after dark. Overhangs shade the same glass in summer. None Mostly a design-time choice, but window coverings, thermal curtains, and adding mass help any house. Heat pump (mini-split) Moves heat instead of making it, giving two to four times as much heat as the electricity it uses. Some models run straight from solar panels and a battery (DC or hybrid solar mini-splits). Electricity, used efficiently Still a large load for a battery; size the battery and solar to it. Efficiency falls in deep cold. Indoor-rated propane or kerosene heater Vent-free heaters made for indoor use, with a low-oxygen shutoff sensor. Propane or kerosene Only models rated for indoor use. Crack a window, keep a CO alarm in the room, never run one while asleep unless the maker says it's safe to. Heat the person, not the house Close off one room, insulate windows, layer clothing, sleeping bags, hot water bottles. An electric blanket or heated pad draws 50–100 W instead of 1,500 W. Little or none The cheapest heat there is; start here. How much a sand battery holds Sand stores about 0.8 kJ per kg for every degree it's heated. A tonne of sand heated from 20 °C to 200 °C holds roughly 40 kWh of heat, about what a small home needs on a cold day. Getting it in takes a lot of solar: 1 kW of panels puts in roughly 3–5 kWh on a good winter day. Sand battery reality check The idea is proven: a commercial sand battery built by Polar Night Energy in Kankaanpää, Finland, has heated a district heating network since 2022, holding sand at around 500 °C. Home builds are another matter: they need high-temperature insulation, thermal cutoffs on the elements, a fire-safe location, and an electrician for the wiring. Running a heat pump straight from solar usually gets more heat per panel; a sand battery's advantage is storing summer-afternoon surplus as heat without a large electrical battery. Cooling Method How it works Power Works best Water over brick or clay (evaporative walls) Water trickles over porous terracotta, brick, or clay, soaking it. Air drawn through or past the wet surface gives up heat to evaporate the water and comes out cooler. Traditional across the Middle East and India, from clay pots to modern terracotta cooli"},{"id":"safety","h":"Safety","x":"09 Safety Carbon monoxide Run generators outside only, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows, and vents. Never in a garage, even with the door open. Battery-powered CO alarms on every level, and get to fresh air at the first headache, dizziness, or nausea. Refueling Shut the generator off and let it cool before you refuel. Gasoline spilled on a hot engine can ignite. Weather Keep generators dry, under an open canopy or a purpose-made cover, and never touch one with wet hands or while standing in water. Batteries Use chargers made for the battery chemistry, fuse every DC circuit near the battery, keep batteries out of extreme heat, and give lead-acid batteries ventilation."},{"id":"configs","h":"Configurations","x":"10 Configurations Four setups, from smallest to largest. Each one builds on the one before. Configuration Equipment Covers Limits 1. Essentials (apartment or single room) Portable power station (1–3 kWh) and a folding solar panel; spare batteries for lights and radios Phones, radios, router, CPAP, lights, a fan, short fridge runs No heat or air conditioning; won't keep a fridge and freezer going for days without sun 2. Critical loads (house) Inverter or dual-fuel portable generator (3–7 kW), inlet with transfer switch or interlock, stored fuel, plus the Level 1 battery Refrigeration, furnace blower, well or sump pump, lights, communications Needs refueling and someone to run it; noise; fuel storage 3. Whole home, automatic Home standby generator on natural gas or propane with an automatic transfer switch, or a whole-home battery with solar Most or all of the house, switching over by itself within seconds Cost and installation; standby depends on gas supply; batteries depend on sun for long outages 4. Off grid (property) Solar array sized for winter, a large LiFePO4 bank, a generator as backup charger, propane for cooking and heat, wood heat Indefinite operation with careful load management Highest cost; needs ongoing attention to loads, weather, and maintenance Whatever you choose, pair it with heat, cooling, and water plans that don't need much electricity (see Heating and cooling ), and with the communications on the Analogue Comms page."},{"id":"maintain","h":"Maintenance and testing","x":"11 Maintenance and testing Monthly Start the generator and run it under load for 20–30 minutes. Check that the power station and batteries are charged. Test the CO alarms. Every few months Rotate stored fuel. Top up stabilizer. Charge stored batteries to the level the manufacturer recommends for storage. Yearly, or by hours Change the generator's oil, air filter, and spark plug per the manual (the first oil change comes early, after break-in). Inspect cords, inlets, and the transfer equipment. Test it for real Once or twice a year, shut off the main breaker and run the house on backup for a few hours. You'll find the circuit you forgot and the load that trips the generator before an outage finds them for you."}]},{"u":"rifle.html","t":"Professional Rifle Shooting","s":[{"id":"","h":"Professional Rifle Shooting","x":"Firearm training · Professional Updated Nov 27, 2026 Professional Rifle Shooting Precision fundamentals for the rifle and carbine: the shot process, positions, natural point of aim, trigger control, zeroing, MOA and mils, wind, and reading your groups. The animations show what the shooter controls and what changes on the target. Built on U.S. Army TC 3-22.9 Rifle and Carbine and standard known-distance marksmanship practice. Trajectory and wind figures come from a G1 drag-model solver for a 5.56 mm, 55-grain load from a 20-inch barrel with a 2.6-inch sight height. The ballistic calculator runs the same solver for any load you pick."},{"id":"safety","h":"Safety","x":"01 Safety The four rules Treat every firearm as if it's loaded. Never point the muzzle at anything you aren't willing to destroy. Keep your finger off the trigger until your sights are on the target and you've decided to fire. Be sure of your target and what's beyond it. Rifle-specific Rifle rounds carry far. A 5.56 mm bullet can travel over 2 miles; a .308 farther. Every shot needs a backstop. Use a chamber flag or empty-chamber indicator whenever the rifle is benched or cased. Check the bore after any squib (weak report, no visible impact) before firing again. Hearing and eye protection every time. A muzzle brake roughly doubles the noise at the shooter's side. On the range A shared range runs on commands from the range safety officer (RSO). Listen for them the whole time you're on the line. With hearing protection on, commands are easy to miss: electronic muffs make speech easier to hear, and you should watch the RSO, the range lights or flags, and the shooters on either side. If everyone around you has stopped, stop and find out why. Command What it means What you do \"The range is hot\" Live fire is allowed. Nobody is forward of the firing line. Eye and ear protection on, including behind the line. Handle firearms only at your position on the line, muzzle downrange. \"Commence fire\" You may load and shoot. Load only now, and only at the line. Wait for this even when the range has been called hot. \"Cease fire\" Stop shooting immediately. Anyone who sees a danger can call it, and everyone obeys it no matter who called it. Finger off the trigger and out of the guard, muzzle downrange, and stay where you are. Don't load, unload, or step back until the RSO tells you what to do next. \"Unload and show clear\" Make your firearm safe and let the RSO check it. Remove the ammunition, open and lock the action, and show the empty chamber to the RSO. \"Is the line clear?\" / \"The line is clear\" The RSO is confirming, then announcing, that every firearm on the line is unloaded with its action open. Your gun is benched or racked: action open, chamber flag in, magazines out. Step back behind the safety line. \"The range is cold\" Nobody touches a firearm. People may go downrange to check or change targets. Hands off guns and ammunition, including loading magazines at the bench, until the range is called hot again. Stay behind the safety line unless you're going downrange. Wording varies by range and organization; some use \"the line is safe,\" whistles, or lights. Read the posted range rules and listen to the briefing before you shoot. The principle doesn't change: firing stops on a cease fire, and no one goes forward until every gun is verified clear. Clear the gun before you step off the line Finger off the trigger, muzzle downrange. Remove the ammunition source first: magazine out, or the magazine tube emptied. Open the action and lock it open. Any round in the chamber comes out. Look and feel: check the chamber and the magazine well or tube by eye, then with a finger. Do the check every time, even when you're sure it's empty. Insert a chamber flag if the range requires one. Bench, rack, case, or holster it the way the range rules say. Never walk away from a loaded gun or carry one off the line. Rifle: drop the magazine before you pull the charging handle. In the other order, the bolt chambers a fresh round as it closes. Lock the bolt to the rear and look into both the chamber and the magazine well."},{"id":"process","h":"The shot process","x":"02 The shot process TC 3-22.9 describes every shot through four functional elements, applied in a pre-shot, shot, and post-shot sequence. Each element is something you can observe and correct. Four functional elements Stability : a position that holds the rifle steady with bone support and minimal muscle. Aim : sight alignment, sight picture, and point of aim on the target. Control : everything you manage at the moment of firing: trigger, breathing, follow-through, and the decision to fire. Movement : moving to and between positions while keeping the rifle ready. Shot sequence Pre-shot Build the position, check natural point of aim, confirm the sight setting and wind hold. Shot Settle at the respiratory pause, refine the aim, press the trigger straight to the rear. Post-shot Hold the trigger to the rear, keep the sight picture through recoil, call the shot, reset."},{"id":"ammo","h":"Ammunition","x":"03 Ammunition Bullet weight is measured in grains; there are 7,000 grains in a pound. For a given rifle cartridge, lighter bullets leave faster and shoot flatter up close; heavier bullets carry their velocity farther, resist wind better at distance, and usually penetrate deeper. Changing weight or type moves your point of impact, so re-confirm your zero when you change loads. Cartridge Common weights Common types Typical velocity Typical use .223 Rem / 5.56×45 NATO 40–77 gr 55 · 62 · 69 · 75 · 77 55 gr FMJ (M193), 62 gr steel-core FMJ (M855), bonded soft point, open-tip match (OTM), polymer tip 2,750–3,250 ft/s (16–20\" barrel) Training, defense, varmint, match to 600 yd .300 Blackout 110–125 gr supersonic 190–220 gr subsonic V-Max, OTM, bonded soft point, subsonic expanding 2,200–2,350 ft/s super ~1,000 ft/s sub (16\") Short barrels and suppressors; hunting to about 200 yd 7.62×39 mm 122–125 gr FMJ (often steel case), soft point, polymer tip 2,300–2,400 ft/s (16\") AK and AR-pattern rifles; deer-size game at moderate range 6.5 Grendel 100–130 gr OTM, polymer tip, bonded soft point 2,400–2,600 ft/s (18–24\") Long range from an AR-15 size rifle, deer .308 Win / 7.62×51 NATO 110–180 gr 147 · 150 · 165 · 168 · 175 147 gr FMJ (M80), soft point, bonded, hollow-point boat-tail match (M118LR 175 gr) 2,550–2,850 ft/s (20–24\" barrel) AR-10 and battle rifles; medium and large game, precision to 1,000 yd Bullet type What it is Use FMJ (full metal jacket, \"ball\") Lead core covered by a copper jacket at the nose Training and practice; at rifle velocity, light 5.56 FMJ may yaw and fragment, but results vary widely SP (soft point) Exposed lead tip on a jacketed bullet Hunting; controlled expansion Bonded Core chemically bonded to the jacket so they don't separate Defense and hunting through barriers such as glass OTM / HPBT (open-tip match / hollow-point boat-tail) Small opening from manufacturing, tapered base; very consistent Precision and match; the 75–77 gr 5.56 loads also fragment reliably Polymer tip Plastic tip on a hollow point; flatter trajectory, starts expansion Hunting, varmint, some defense loads Steel-core (M855 \"green tip\") Small steel penetrator ahead of a lead core Military general purpose; not a hunting bullet, banned at some ranges Frangible Compressed copper powder that breaks apart on hard surfaces Steel targets at close range, shoot houses .223 vs. 5.56 The cases look the same, but 5.56 NATO is loaded to higher pressure and the 5.56 chamber has a longer throat. .223 is safe in a 5.56 or .223 Wylde chamber. 5.56 in a chamber marked .223 Rem only can produce excess pressure. Check the barrel marking. .300 Blackout in a 5.56 gun A .300 Blackout round fits into a 5.56 magazine and chamber, and firing it forces a .30-caliber bullet into a .22-caliber barrel. The rifle can blow apart. Mark .300 BLK magazines clearly and keep the two cartridges physically separate. .308 vs. 7.62 NATO Close but not identical in chamber dimensions and pressure. Most modern .308 rifles handle 7.62 NATO; check the manufacturer's guidance, especially on older or surplus rifles."},{"id":"positions","h":"Positions","x":"04 Positions Stability comes from bone, not muscle. The lower and more supported the position, the smaller your wobble. Use the most stable position the terrain and time allow. Position Key points Typical use Prone (supported) Bipod or bag under the handguard, body straight behind the rifle or at a slight angle, firing-side leg drawn up to lift the chest off the ground. Known-distance shooting, zeroing, long range Prone (unsupported) Support elbow directly under the rifle, sling tension if available, firing elbow planted. No support available, still the steadiest field position Sitting Open-leg, crossed-leg, or crossed-ankle. Flat of the upper arms rests against the inside of the knees, not elbow point on kneecap. Over grass or low cover Kneeling Support elbow just ahead of the knee, buttock on the heel, firing-side foot under you. Shift weight forward into the rifle. Quick, gets over obstacles, transitions well Standing Feet shoulder-width, support arm under the rifle or extended on the handguard, firing elbow comfortable. Use any available support. Close range, movement, no time"},{"id":"npa","h":"Natural point of aim and breathing","x":"05 Natural point of aim and breathing Natural point of aim (NPA) is where the rifle points when your body is relaxed in position. If you have to muscle the rifle onto the target, it drifts back toward NPA as the shot breaks. Adjust the position until the rifle rests on the target by itself. Checking NPA Get into position and aim at the target. Close your eyes, take a few normal breaths, and relax. Open your eyes. If the reticle has moved off the target, shift your whole body, not just the arms. For left and right, pivot around the support elbow (prone) or move your feet. For elevation, slide your body forward or back or adjust the support. Breathing The chest rises and falls with every breath, which moves the reticle up and down. After you exhale there's a natural pause of 2–3 seconds when the body is still. Break the shot in that pause. If it runs long and your vision starts to blur, breathe again and restart; don't hold your breath past it. Arc of movement through the breathing cycle"},{"id":"sights","h":"Sights and optics","x":"06 Sights and optics Sighting system Strengths Limits Fit Iron sights No batteries, durable, light Three focal planes (rear, front, target); harder for older eyes and at distance Backup; learning fundamentals Red dot Both eyes open, fast, forgiving of head position No magnification; dot size covers small targets at distance Out to about 300 yd on man-size targets Low-power variable (LPVO) 1× for close work, 6–10× for distance Heavier; eye box tighter at high power General-purpose carbine Magnified scope Precise aim, reticle holds and ranging Narrow field of view, slower up close Precision rifle, beyond 300 yd First vs. second focal plane First focal plane (FFP) : the reticle grows with magnification, so mil and MOA holds are correct at every power. Second focal plane (SFP) : the reticle stays the same size, so holds are correct only at the power the maker specifies, usually the highest. Eye relief and parallax Set eye relief so you see a full, shadow-free image with your head in a natural spot on the stock. Adjust parallax at the target distance until the reticle doesn't shift on the target when you move your head slightly."},{"id":"trigger","h":"Trigger control and follow-through","x":"07 Trigger control and follow-through The trigger press is the one moment where small errors become misses. Press straight to the rear with steadily increasing pressure, so the moment the shot breaks surprises you a little. Then hold it to the rear through recoil and let it out only to the reset. Trigger press, pressure, and the impact it produces Finger placement Use the pad of the index finger, between the tip and first joint, so the press is straight back. Too little finger pushes the shot; too much pulls it. Keep the rest of the hand from tightening as the finger moves. Follow-through Keep the trigger pinned, keep your eye on the sight picture, and stay in position for a second after the shot. Then let the trigger forward until it clicks (the reset) and call the shot: say where the sights were when it broke."},{"id":"zero","h":"Zeroing and trajectory","x":"08 Zeroing and trajectory The sights look along a straight line. The bullet leaves the bore below that line, climbs across it, and falls back across it. A zero sets the angle between the two so the bullet crosses the line of sight at the distances you choose. Bullet path vs. line of sight Zero 25 yd 50 yd 100 yd 200 yd 300 yd 400 yd 50 yd (far zero ≈240) −1.2\" 0.0\" +1.6\" +1.5\" −4.5\" −18.3\" 100 yd −1.6\" −0.8\" 0.0\" −1.8\" −9.4\" −24.9\" 300 yd (near zero ≈38) −0.8\" +0.7\" +3.1\" +4.5\" 0.0\" −12.3\" Computed with the G1 drag model for 55 gr M193 (BC 0.243) at 3,240 ft/s from a 20-inch barrel, 2.6-inch sight height, sea-level standard conditions. From a 14.5–16 inch carbine, the 50-yard zero's far zero moves in to roughly 200–225 yd, which is where the \"50/200\" name comes from. Run your own load in the calculator below. Zeroing procedure Bore-sight or start close (25 yd) to get on paper. From a supported prone position, fire a 3- or 5-shot group. Don't adjust off single shots. Measure from the group's center to the point of aim, in inches. Convert to clicks: inches ÷ (click value × distance ÷ 100). A ¼-MOA click moves about 0.26\" at 100 yd. Adjust in the direction you want the group to move (\"up\" moves impact up). Fire another group. Repeat until the group center is on the point of aim, then confirm at the far zero distance. Choosing a zero 50 yd (\"50/200\") : bullet stays within about 2.2 inches of the line of sight from 25 yd out past 250 yd. Common general-purpose carbine zero. 100 yd : standard for magnified optics and dialing. Simplest to use with a ballistic table. 300 yd (\"36/300\") : maximum point-blank reach on a man-size target; up to about 4.5 inches high around 175–200 yd."},{"id":"calc","h":"Ballistic calculator","x":"09 Ballistic calculator Pick a cartridge and load, or type your own chronographed velocity and the bullet's published G1 ballistic coefficient. The table and chart update as you change anything. Set \"Hold at\" to a distance to get the exact elevation and wind hold."},{"id":"angles","h":"MOA, mils, and range","x":"10 MOA, mils, and range Both are angles. An angle covers more inches the farther away the target is, which is why scope adjustments and holds are expressed as angles instead of inches. Unit At 100 yd Common click Ranging formula MOA 1.047\" ¼ MOA = 0.26\" Range (yd) = size (in) × 95.5 ÷ MOA Mil 3.6\" 0.1 mil = 0.36\" Range (yd) = size (in) × 27.78 ÷ mils Range from a reticle reading Measure a target of known size in your reticle, then enter it here. A standard IPSC/USPSA target is about 18 inches wide; an average adult torso is about 18–20 inches wide. Target size (in) Reading Unit mils MOA Range 333 yd"},{"id":"wind","h":"Wind","x":"11 Wind Wind pushes the bullet sideways for its whole time of flight, so drift grows faster than distance. Only the crosswind component matters for drift. The wind clock tells you how much of the wind's speed counts. Wind clock: direction, value, and drift Clock direction Value Drift at 300 yd, 10 mph, 55 gr M193 3 or 9 o'clock Full (100%) ≈ 11.9\" 2, 4, 8, or 10 o'clock ≈ 87% ≈ 10.3\" 1, 5, 7, or 11 o'clock Half (50%) ≈ 5.9\" 12 or 6 o'clock No value ≈ 0\" Estimating speed Under 3 mph: hardly felt; smoke drifts. 3–5 mph: felt on the face. 5–8 mph: leaves and grass in constant motion. 8–12 mph: loose paper moves, small branches sway. 12–15 mph: small trees sway. Reading mirage Through a scope focused just short of the target, heat shimmer shows wind between you and the target. Boiling straight up means little or no crosswind. A gentle lean means light wind; flat, fast-running mirage means strong wind. Mirage that suddenly boils after running usually means the wind is changing direction."},{"id":"groups","h":"Reading your groups","x":"12 Reading your groups The shape of a group tells you more than any single shot. Shoot groups of five, then diagnose. These are starting points; confirm with your shot calls. Pattern Likely causes Fix Tight, centered Good fundamentals and zero Sustain; move back or shrink the target Tight, off center Zero, or a consistent hold or wind error Adjust sights only after confirming with a second group Vertical string Breaking shots at different points in the breath, NPA off vertically, inconsistent cheek weld or bag pressure Fire in the respiratory pause; check NPA elevation; same cheek weld each shot Horizontal string Trigger pushed or pulled, rifle canted, changing wind, NPA off left or right Finger placement; level the rifle; watch wind indicators; check NPA Large and round General inconsistency: position, focus, trigger Slow down; dry-fire; rebuild the position from the ground up One flyer One bad shot, usually called as bad Ignore it if you called it; investigate if you didn't"},{"id":"dope","h":"The data book","x":"13 The data book A data book (DOPE, for \"data on previous engagements\") records what the rifle actually did. Over time it becomes the most accurate ballistic table you have, because it's built from your rifle, ammunition, and conditions. Field Example entry Date, time, location 10/24, 0930, home range, 840 ft elevation Rifle, optic, ammunition, lot 16\" carbine, 1–6× LPVO, 77 gr OTM, lot 4417 Weather 54°F, 29.92 inHg, 60% humidity Distance and target 300 yd, 10\" steel Elevation used +1.6 mil from 100-yd zero Wind call and hold 5–7 mph from 9 o'clock, held 0.6 mil left Shot calls and results 5 shots, 4 hits, one called low-left Notes First shot from cold bore 0.5\" high"},{"id":"drills","h":"Drills and standards","x":"14 Drills and standards Drill Setup Standard Builds 5-shot groups 100 yd, supported prone, no time Under 2 MOA with service ammunition; under 1 MOA with match ammunition and a precision rifle Baseline accuracy, trigger Dot drill Ten 1-inch dots at 25 yd, one shot each 10 of 10 inside the dots Trigger control and focus Position ladder 3 shots each from prone, sitting, kneeling, standing on the same 8\" plate at 100 yd All hits in 90 seconds total Building positions quickly Known distance Plates at 100, 200, 300 yd First-round hit at each distance from a 100-yd zero using your data book Holds, data book use Wind ladder Same 300-yd target across a day with changing wind Log every call and correction Wind reading Ready-up From low ready, one shot on a 3×5 card at 50 yd Under 2.0 s with a red dot Mount, sight acquisition"},{"id":"maint","h":"Maintenance","x":"15 Maintenance Routine Clear the rifle and verify empty before you start. Clean the bore from the chamber end toward the muzzle with a rod guide to protect the throat. Wipe the bolt and carrier; scrape carbon off the bolt tail and inside the carrier. Lubricate per the manufacturer: on an AR-pattern rifle, the bolt carrier rails, cam pin, and bolt rings run wet. Do a function check after reassembly. Checks Optic and mount screws witness-marked and checked against the maker's torque values. Gas key staking intact; extractor spring strong. Magazines: clean, feed lips straight, springs strong. Number them so you can track a bad one. Record round count. Barrels and some parts have expected service lives."},{"id":"errors","h":"Common errors","x":"16 Common errors Error What it does Fix Muscling the rifle onto target Rifle drifts back to natural point of aim as the shot breaks Check NPA; move the body, not the arms Holding your breath too long Wobble grows, vision blurs, shooter rushes the press Break inside the 2–3 s pause or breathe and restart Jerking or slapping the trigger Shots low and to the side opposite the firing hand Smooth press; dry-fire with a coin or dummy round Anticipating recoil Pushing into the stock before the break; low shots Ball-and-dummy drill; let the shot surprise you Lifting the head to look Loses follow-through and the shot call Stay in the gun for a full second after the shot Canting the rifle Shots go low and to the side of the cant at distance Use a bubble level; same cheek weld every shot Adjusting off one shot Chases random error Adjust only off the center of a group"}]},{"u":"scenarios.html","t":"Engagement Breakdowns","s":[{"id":"","h":"Engagement Breakdowns","x":"Medical & fieldcraft · Incident studies Updated Nov 27, 2026 Scenario-Based Engagement Breakdowns These incident studies are intended as discussion pieces rather than doctrine. The focus is on how small groups respond when plans change, information becomes incomplete, personnel are lost, and the environment begins limiting the options that seemed available at the start."},{"id":"s01","h":"Armed Offenders Conducting an Urban Fighting Withdrawal","x":"01 Armed Offenders Conducting an Urban Fighting Withdrawal A heavily armed group attempting to leave an urban objective encountered a rapidly developing local police response. Their original objective was already complete, so from that point forward their primary concern was escape. The offenders possessed rifles, body armor, vehicles, and enough coordination to maintain movement during the early portion of the response. Their immediate advantage was firepower and initiative. Law enforcement had a different advantage: the response could continue to grow. The longer the incident lasted, the more officers could arrive, the more information could be shared, and the fewer uncontested routes would remain available. Early in the engagement, the offenders were repeatedly able to create temporary freedom of movement. They engaged responding officers, moved to another position, and continued trying to leave the area. This worked for a time, but each movement addressed only the immediate problem. It did not improve their overall situation. They remained in a populated urban environment where vehicles restricted movement and buildings and intersections repeatedly broke visual contact between members of the group. At the same time, officers were entering the area from additional directions. As the response expanded, the offenders gradually became separated from one another. That fragmentation became more significant than any individual exchange of gunfire. While the offenders remained together, they could communicate directly, observe the same problems, and react as a group. Once separated, each individual was working with less information about where the others were, what condition they were in, and which routes were still available. Casualties made that problem worse. A wounded or lost member did not simply reduce the group by one person; it changed how the remaining members moved and what they were capable of doing together. Individual offenders continued to act aggressively and, in several instances, effectively, but the group as a whole was becoming less coordinated. This is where the distinction between performing well in an individual confrontation and controlling the larger incident becomes important. The offenders were still able to gain another intersection, reach another vehicle, force officers to reposition, or temporarily open a route. Those were meaningful short-term successes, but none of them restored the initiative they had at the beginning. As the incident continued, time increasingly favored law enforcement. Additional officers were arriving while the offenders were losing people, routes, mobility, and contact with one another. Eventually the original withdrawal stopped functioning as a coordinated plan and became a series of individual attempts to leave the area. The useful point is that the group did not suddenly collapse. Its ability to function deteriorated progressively as delays, casualties, separation, and the expanding police response accumulated. By the time the offenders were operating largely as individuals, they were dealing with a very different situation from the one they had encountered at the start."},{"id":"s02","h":"Security Convoy Contact at a Congested Border Checkpoint","x":"02 Security Convoy Contact at a Congested Border Checkpoint A protected vehicle convoy transporting a detainee became immobilized in heavy traffic while approaching an international border checkpoint. Civilian vehicles surrounded the convoy, traffic prevented meaningful maneuver, and adjacent vehicles restricted visibility. If a threat developed, there was very little room to create distance or change the formation of the convoy. Because mobility was limited, observation became increasingly important. Before any overt attack occurred, members of the security element began noticing behavior that differed from the surrounding traffic. Certain vehicles drew additional attention, some occupants appeared unusually interested in the convoy, and people began opening doors and moving in ways that caused the security personnel to pay closer attention. At that point, the situation was still uncertain. Nobody had yet done anything that clearly established hostile intent. That period of uncertainty is probably the most useful part of the incident because the security personnel had to prepare for a possible confrontation without treating everyone around them as a threat. There was a noticeable difference in how quickly various people recognized that the situation might be changing. Some members of the convoy were still trying to determine whether anything significant was actually occurring, while others had already seen enough indicators to increase their level of readiness. Once weapons were presented and the threat became clear, the confrontation developed and ended very quickly. The physical distances were short, the vehicles provided little maneuver space, and the security personnel were already paying attention to the people who became involved. What makes the incident particularly useful for discussion is how little of it consisted of actual gunfire. Most of the significant decision-making occurred beforehand, while the convoy was stationary and the situation remained ambiguous. Personnel were observing behavior, comparing what they were seeing against the surrounding environment, communicating their concerns, and preparing themselves without unnecessarily escalating the situation. After the confrontation ended, the surrounding environment returned almost immediately to normal activity. Traffic remained backed up, civilians were still present, and the convoy continued toward the checkpoint. That abrupt transition reinforces how quickly a violent encounter can develop inside an otherwise routine environment. The convoy had very few options for physical movement, so its greatest advantage was recognizing that the situation was changing before the threat became completely obvious. The incident is therefore less about the brief exchange itself and more about the period immediately before it, when personnel were attempting to distinguish unusual behavior from an actual developing threat."},{"id":"s03","h":"Isolated Position Under Repeated Assault","x":"03 Isolated Position Under Repeated Assault A lightly equipped security element occupied an isolated compound in an increasingly hostile area with limited prospects for immediate reinforcement. The location was exposed, support was distant, resupply was uncertain, and the surrounding terrain allowed an attacking force to approach from several directions. Before the first major attack occurred, the local commander recognized that the position needed improvement and directed personnel to prepare it accordingly. That decision did not eliminate the problems associated with the location, but it gave the defenders more options once the attacks began. The attacking force possessed a substantial numerical advantage and made repeated attempts to overwhelm the position. The defenders were rarely in a position to control the larger fight. Instead, they dealt with whichever part of the perimeter was under the most pressure at a given moment, shifting personnel and attention as conditions changed. That approach worked for a considerable period of time, but it came at a cost. Every successful defense consumed ammunition, water, medical resources, energy, and time. Those losses were difficult or impossible to replace because the position remained isolated. This created a situation where the defenders could perform well in the immediate engagement while their overall condition continued to deteriorate. Stopping another assault was important, but it did not necessarily leave them in a better position afterward. In some cases, it simply meant they were still holding the same ground with fewer resources available for the next attack. As the engagement continued, the central question gradually changed. It was no longer just whether the defenders could repel another assault. It became whether they could continue doing so with the supplies and support that remained. That distinction is important because a position does not have to be physically overrun before it becomes unsustainable. Personnel may still be organized, communicating, and capable of responding effectively while the range of available options is steadily narrowing. The useful discussion point is the difference between solving the immediate tactical problem and improving the overall situation. The defenders were very good at the first. The isolation of the position made the second much harder."},{"id":"s04","h":"Fragmented Ground Force Responding to Aircraft Losses in an Urban Area","x":"04 Fragmented Ground Force Responding to Aircraft Losses in an Urban Area A combined assault and security force entered a dense urban area expecting to conduct a relatively short operation. The original objective was achieved, but the situation changed almost immediately when an aircraft went down inside the city. Ground elements that had been positioned around the original objective were redirected toward the crash location. Vehicle columns attempted to reach the same area through narrow streets while hostile personnel established roadblocks and firing positions along the likely routes. The first aircraft loss already required the force to reorganize around a problem that had not existed in the original plan. When a second aircraft went down at another location, the operation became even more fragmented. At that point, there was no longer one clear problem. Personnel at the original objective still required security. The first crash site needed to be reached and protected. The second crash created another isolated group requiring assistance. Casualties needed treatment and evacuation, and vehicle elements were still trying to move through streets that were becoming increasingly difficult to navigate. Every new problem consumed resources that had already been assigned somewhere else. A casualty did not only remove one person from the fight. Someone had to treat that person, someone might have to move him, and additional personnel might be required to provide security while that happened. The same effect occurred with the downed aircraft. The aircraft itself was only part of the problem. The crew, the crash site, the recovery effort, and the personnel trying to reach it all became additional responsibilities. Urban terrain amplified those difficulties. Friendly elements could be physically close to each other while still being unable to make contact. A few city blocks could contain barricades, hostile fire, damaged vehicles, unfamiliar streets, crowds, and casualties. On a map, the distance might appear minor. On the ground, it could be enough to keep two friendly groups effectively separated. Communication also became increasingly difficult because events were developing faster than information could be collected and passed. A report could be accurate when transmitted and already outdated by the time another element acted on it. By the later stages of the engagement, the force was spending most of its effort dealing with the consequences of the original mission rather than the original objective itself. The incident is useful because it shows how quickly one unexpected event can generate several additional problems, each of which competes for the same limited people and resources."},{"id":"s05","h":"Security Element Responding to a Compromised Diplomatic Facility","x":"05 Security Element Responding to a Compromised Diplomatic Facility A small security element stationed at one facility received reports that a nearby diplomatic compound was under attack. The information coming from the compound was incomplete, friendly personnel were still believed to be inside, and the strength and organization of the attacking force were unclear. The security element also had responsibility for its own location. Moving personnel toward the compromised compound would necessarily reduce the number of people available to protect the facility they were leaving. That created a legitimate conflict between two responsibilities. Remaining in place preserved security at one location while conditions worsened at another. Moving toward the attack increased the chance of recovering personnel at the compromised compound but exposed the responding element and reduced security elsewhere. When the security personnel eventually moved, they entered a situation that had already developed considerably. The compound had been penetrated, buildings were burning, friendly personnel were scattered, and armed individuals were present in the surrounding area without a clean or easily understood front line. The responding group therefore had to build its understanding of the incident while already operating inside it. The immediate problem was no longer simply defending the compound. It had become a personnel recovery effort. Once surviving personnel were located and moved, the mission changed again. The recovery became a withdrawal, and the withdrawal brought the same personnel back to another facility that now had to be defended. The same small group moved through several different roles without a meaningful break between them. They began as a security element, became a response force, shifted into a recovery effort, withdrew under pressure, and then returned to a defensive role. That progression is what makes the incident useful for discussion. The original mission description stopped being particularly relevant because the problem kept changing faster than personnel could be reorganized around it. The people involved did not receive a fresh team for each new task. They carried fatigue, incomplete information, and the consequences of the previous phase directly into the next one."},{"id":"s06","h":"Investigative Convoy Ambush and Personnel Recovery","x":"06 Investigative Convoy Ambush and Personnel Recovery A small investigative team traveling with security personnel was attacked while moving through an urban corridor. The team was not primarily configured as an assault element, and the initial attack disrupted the convoy before everyone involved had a clear understanding of what was happening. During the attack, one member of the group was taken. The remaining personnel were immediately dealing with two separate priorities: surviving the contact and preventing the captured member from being moved beyond their ability to recover him. Those priorities pulled the group in different directions. Consolidating the surviving personnel would have provided better control and allowed more time to gather information, but delay also increased the likelihood that the captured member would be moved farther away. The surviving group chose to continue after him. As they moved deeper into the urban environment, their control over the situation decreased. The opposing group had greater familiarity with the area, while the pursuing personnel were moving through terrain they did not fully understand. The recovery effort eventually broke into several smaller confrontations. Different members of the team were handling different parts of the problem at the same time, and not everyone had a complete picture of what the others were doing. That makes the incident useful because it presents a situation where neither obvious option is particularly good. Slowing down improves coordination but increases the chance of losing the captured person. Moving quickly preserves pressure but increases uncertainty and the likelihood that the responding group will become separated. The value of the scenario is not in declaring one choice correct. It is in recognizing that some incidents present competing risks rather than a single safe option. The loss of one person changed the priorities of the entire group and introduced a time constraint that shaped every decision afterward."},{"id":"s07","h":"Reconnaissance Element Attempting to Disengage in Mountainous Terrain","x":"07 Reconnaissance Element Attempting to Disengage in Mountainous Terrain A four-person reconnaissance element operating in mountainous terrain was compromised and contacted by a substantially larger hostile force. Once that occurred, the original purpose of the mission became secondary to disengagement. The terrain had initially helped conceal the team, but after contact it restricted their ability to leave. There were relatively few easy routes away, and movement required negotiating steep ground while attempting to remain together under pressure. As the engagement continued, personnel were injured and communication became less reliable. The team's spacing changed, movement slowed, and the terrain itself caused additional physical injuries. This produced a cycle that became increasingly difficult to break. Injuries reduced mobility, reduced mobility made it easier for the pursuing force to maintain pressure, and continued pressure created additional opportunities for more injuries. The team was still capable of responding to immediate threats, but that did not necessarily solve the larger problem. Successful exchanges did not create a clear route out of the terrain or restore the mobility the group had lost. Over time, the reconnaissance element became less like the four-person team that had begun the mission and more like a group of injured individuals attempting to remain together while moving away from a larger force. The scenario is particularly useful for understanding the relationship between terrain and mission. The same ground that provided concealment during observation became restrictive once the team needed to withdraw quickly. Terrain is not automatically an advantage simply because it offers cover or concealment. Its value depends on what the group needs to do at that particular moment."},{"id":"s08","h":"Small Police Assault Element Moving Through a Contested City","x":"08 Small Police Assault Element Moving Through a Contested City A small police assault element moved through a heavily damaged urban area where an organized hostile force controlled portions of the city. Threats could emerge from rooftops, damaged buildings, vehicles, roadblocks, explosive hazards, or individuals who were difficult to distinguish from the surrounding population. Under those conditions, movement was not simply the space between engagements. Movement was part of the problem. Vehicles provided speed and some protection, but they also tied the group to usable roads. Buildings could offer temporary security while simultaneously limiting visibility and exit options. Routes that appeared acceptable earlier could become unusable later as conditions changed. The team repeatedly shifted between mounted and dismounted movement depending on what the environment allowed. Over time, casualties reduced the number of people available and vehicles were lost or abandoned. What makes the incident useful is that the unit's capability did not disappear all at once. It eroded gradually. When one person was lost, the team did not simply become smaller. Other people had to absorb that individual's responsibilities. Observation sectors changed, leadership responsibilities shifted, and tasks that had previously been divided among several people were now concentrated among fewer. The same was true when a vehicle was lost. The effect was not limited to transportation. It changed how quickly the group could move, what equipment could be carried, and which routes remained practical. By the later stages of the operation, the team was still attempting to perform much of the same work it had been doing at the beginning, but with substantially less margin for error. That gradual redistribution of responsibilities is probably the most useful part of the scenario for a law-enforcement audience. In a small group, every person represents more than one set of hands. Each person also carries information, experience, equipment, and responsibilities that someone else may have to assume if that person is no longer available."},{"id":"s09","h":"Small Defensive Element Operating Inside a Penetrated Position","x":"09 Small Defensive Element Operating Inside a Penetrated Position Personnel at an isolated outpost came under a coordinated attack from hostile forces occupying advantageous terrain around the position. During the engagement, portions of the perimeter were penetrated. Once that occurred, the problem changed significantly. The defenders were no longer dealing only with an external threat. Friendly and hostile personnel were now operating inside the same general area. The outpost stopped functioning as one continuous defensive position. Instead, it became a collection of smaller engagements involving isolated groups and individual fighting positions. Some personnel were cut off from others. Some were trapped in areas they could not easily leave, while other members of the force attempted to move between positions without having a complete understanding of who controlled the ground between them. Casualties and communication problems made that uncertainty worse. At that point, the formal perimeter mattered less than knowing where friendly personnel were located and whether those positions could still support each other. The situation began to improve when surviving personnel started reconnecting those isolated locations. The larger conditions had not changed. The hostile force was still present, the terrain was still unfavorable, and the defenders had already suffered losses. What changed was that small groups began restoring contact with one another and recovering usable portions of the position. This is an important distinction because a perimeter can fail without every person inside it immediately becoming ineffective. Once the original boundary stops being meaningful, the problem may need to be understood in terms of people, occupied positions, and whether those positions can still communicate and support one another. The incident is useful because it shows that the loss of organizational structure does not necessarily mean the complete loss of capability. In this case, capability was rebuilt locally before the larger position began functioning coherently again."},{"id":"s10","h":"Night Operation Inside a Walled Residential Compound","x":"10 Night Operation Inside a Walled Residential Compound A specialized assault force entered a walled residential compound at night searching for a high-value individual whose exact location inside the property was unknown. A significant problem developed during insertion when one aircraft became disabled. That immediately changed the extraction picture, but the ground element still had an unresolved objective inside the compound. The property contained multiple structures, enclosed spaces, and occupants whose locations and intentions were not completely known. The team could not understand the entire environment at once. Information became available as personnel moved through the compound. Each new area answered some questions while creating others. Personnel had to continue updating their understanding of where occupants were located, whether additional people remained elsewhere on the property, and whether the person they were looking for had already moved. The disabled aircraft remained a serious issue throughout the operation, but it was not necessarily the most immediate issue at every moment. The ground force continued addressing the part of the situation directly in front of it while maintaining awareness that the aircraft and eventual withdrawal would still require attention. That separation of problems is what makes the scenario useful. Several significant issues existed simultaneously, but they did not all require the same amount of attention at the same time. The original plan changed almost immediately because of the aircraft problem and continued changing as new information became available inside the compound. The purpose of the operation, however, remained clear enough that personnel could continue working toward it despite those changes. The incident provides a good example of the difference between preserving a plan and preserving an objective. The first became impossible fairly early. The second remained achievable."},{"id":"s11","h":"Two-Person Survival Movement After a Patrol Ambush","x":"11 Two-Person Survival Movement After a Patrol Ambush A patrol was ambushed in remote terrain and rapidly lost most of its personnel. Two individuals survived the immediate action, but one was seriously wounded and the other remained responsible for moving both of them away from the threat. At that point, the patrol's original mission had effectively ended. The immediate objective became survival and movement. The condition of the wounded person imposed a permanent limitation on everything that followed. The mobile survivor could have moved more quickly alone, but remaining with the casualty dramatically reduced the pace of movement. That meant ordinary terrain became more difficult, distance became more significant, and periods of rest became unavoidable. Any new contact also carried greater consequences because there was almost no reserve capability left. The most interesting part of the scenario is how the definition of success changes as the situation deteriorates. At the beginning, success is tied to the original patrol mission. After the ambush, success becomes breaking contact. After the casualties, it becomes keeping two people alive and continuing to move. Eventually, progress may be measured in relatively small distances rather than completion of any larger objective. That reduction in scope is not necessarily indecision or defeatism. It can simply reflect an accurate understanding of what the remaining people are physically capable of accomplishing. For law enforcement, that translates well beyond the military setting. Serious incidents sometimes force people to abandon the original objective and concentrate on preserving life, maintaining communication, or reaching a location where the situation can be reassessed. The ability to recognize that the mission has changed can matter as much as the ability to continue pursuing the original one."},{"id":"s12","h":"Escort Element Withdrawing With Noncombatants","x":"12 Escort Element Withdrawing With Noncombatants A small security element moving through hostile territory became responsible for a group of noncombatants while attempting to reach friendly control. The presence of civilians changed the movement problem immediately. The security personnel could not move at whatever pace was most convenient for them. Their practical speed became the speed of the people they were responsible for protecting. Route selection was affected in the same way. Terrain that presented little difficulty for trained personnel might be considerably harder for children, older adults, injured people, or civilians who were already exhausted. As the possibility of contact increased, the security element had to divide its attention between the surrounding threat and the people it was trying to move. Those responsibilities competed for personnel and attention. Someone helping civilians maintain movement was not giving the same level of attention to the surrounding environment. Someone focused entirely on security was less available to keep the protected group organized. The security element could therefore perform well during an individual confrontation and still lose ground on the larger problem if the civilians stopped moving or became separated. That is what makes the scenario different from a normal withdrawal. The security group's mobility was directly tied to people who did not possess the same training, equipment, physical ability, or understanding of the situation. For a law-enforcement audience, the parallel is straightforward. Evacuations and protective movements rarely involve only trained personnel. Officers may be responsible for children, injured civilians, elderly people, intoxicated individuals, or people who simply do not understand what is happening. The protected group is not separate from the tactical problem. Its condition and behavior help define what options are realistically available."},{"id":"s13","h":"Reconnaissance Element Pinned Inside a Civilian Structure","x":"13 Reconnaissance Element Pinned Inside a Civilian Structure A small reconnaissance element occupied a civilian residence in order to observe activity in the surrounding urban area. Initially, the structure served that purpose well by providing concealment, elevation, and separation from activity outside. Once the team's presence was discovered, the purpose of the building changed. The same location that had been useful for observation now had to be defended. After personnel were wounded, it changed again. The structure became a place where casualties had to be treated while the remaining members of the team continued monitoring the surrounding threat and coordinating outside assistance. That progression is what makes the incident useful. Nothing about the building itself changed, but the team's requirements changed dramatically. As casualties accumulated, personnel who had previously been available for security tasks were redirected toward medical care, communication, and casualty movement. The number of people physically inside the structure may not have changed much, but the number available to watch approaches and respond to threats had been reduced. Eventually, remaining in the building stopped offering the same advantages it had provided earlier. A position selected for observation had limited options for moving casualties and became increasingly restrictive once the team needed to leave. The broader point is that a location should not be considered good or bad in the abstract. Its value depends on what personnel need from it at a particular moment. A building that is excellent for observation may be poor for evacuation. A location that offers concealment may have limited exits. A position that works well while nobody knows you are there may become far less useful after compromise. For law enforcement, that is a familiar issue. Houses, apartments, businesses, vehicles, stairwells, and parking structures can all change from useful locations into restrictive ones as the purpose of the response changes."},{"id":"s14","h":"Patrol Immobilized by Explosive Hazards During Casualty Recovery","x":"14 Patrol Immobilized by Explosive Hazards During Casualty Recovery A small patrol moving through open terrain encountered an explosive device that immediately caused a serious casualty. Personnel naturally attempted to reach the injured individual, but the casualty was located inside an area where additional explosive hazards were believed to be present. That created a direct conflict between medical urgency and movement safety. The need to reach the casualty encouraged personnel to move quickly. The possibility of additional devices meant that careless movement could create more casualties. As more people entered the area attempting to assist, additional explosive hazards were encountered. What began as one injured person gradually developed into a larger casualty problem. Unlike many of the other incidents in this collection, there was no visible opponent whose actions could be observed. The threat was embedded in the environment. That removed many of the responses people normally associate with an armed confrontation. Speed was not automatically helpful. Adding more personnel was not automatically helpful. Aggressive action could make the problem worse rather than better. Previously traveled ground became valuable because it represented information. Unknown ground represented uncertainty. Every additional casualty also changed the requirements of the recovery effort. Personnel who entered intending to help someone else could themselves become people who needed assistance. The scenario is useful because it demonstrates how strong emotional pressure can conflict with the safest available course of action. Seeing an injured person naturally creates an immediate desire to reach that person. In some environments, however, moving without enough information can increase the number of people who need rescue. For law enforcement, the specific hazard may be different, but the underlying issue is familiar. Officers sometimes encounter scenes where the first visible casualty is not the entire problem. The conditions that injured that person may still be present, and the desire to immediately help has to be balanced against the possibility of creating additional victims."},{"id":"s15","h":"Armed Criminal Group Contacted During an Urban Escape","x":"15 Armed Criminal Group Contacted During an Urban Escape A heavily armed criminal group attempting to leave an urban area became trapped in traffic while a pursuing law-enforcement element closed on its position. The offenders recognized that a confrontation was developing before most of the surrounding civilian population did, but the physical environment limited what they could do with that information. Traffic occupied the roadway and civilian vehicles left little room for movement. The offenders could not simply accelerate away, while pursuing officers also had to operate around uninvolved people and vehicles. Once the confrontation began, the offenders' objective remained escape. Staying in place became increasingly disadvantageous because every additional delay created more opportunity for law-enforcement resources to arrive. When vehicle movement became impractical, the remaining offenders transitioned to movement on foot. That solved the immediate problem of being trapped inside the vehicle, but it removed something else the vehicle had been providing: cohesion. While everyone remained in the same vehicle, the group had a common location and generally shared the same understanding of what was happening. Once they left it, individuals began moving through the environment at different speeds and with different information. Casualties made that separation worse. The group became progressively smaller and less coordinated until the withdrawal no longer functioned as one organized effort. The useful distinction is between mobility and firepower. The offenders retained significant ability to engage pursuing officers, but that did not mean they had somewhere useful to go. Being able to create temporary space is not the same as recovering a viable route out of the area. For law enforcement, that distinction matters when looking at armed offenders who are attempting to flee rather than occupy ground. Their behavior may be driven less by an effort to defeat responding officers than by an effort to recover enough mobility to continue escaping. Understanding that objective can help make sense of movement that might otherwise appear inconsistent or disorganized."},{"id":"close","h":"When the Original Plan Stops Matching the Incident","x":"Closing Observation When the Original Plan Stops Matching the Incident Across all of these scenarios, the common issue is not a particular weapon, tactic, or type of unit. It is the way small groups respond when the assumptions behind the original plan begin to fail. A group may start with a clear objective, a known number of personnel, working vehicles, established communications, and an expected route. Then an aircraft goes down, a vehicle becomes trapped, a member is captured, a position is penetrated, a casualty cannot move, or a route becomes unusable. The important part is that these problems rarely remain isolated. A casualty reduces manpower, but it may also require medical attention, transportation, additional security, and somebody else to assume the injured person's responsibilities. A disabled vehicle changes mobility, but it may also change available equipment, communication, and the ability to move casualties. A lost route affects movement, but it can also separate personnel and disrupt the information available to different parts of the group. This matters even more in small teams because each person represents a relatively large portion of the group's total capability. Losing one person from a four-person group is not simply a twenty-five percent reduction in headcount. The remaining three people also have to absorb whatever that fourth person had been responsible for. The same principle applies to law enforcement incidents. An officer who moves to provide medical care is still present, but for that period he may no longer be available for another task. An officer assigned to a prisoner, witness, evacuation, perimeter point, or casualty has effectively changed roles, and the rest of the group has to adjust around that. That is why these scenarios are useful to discuss without reducing them to whether somebody made the \"right\" or \"wrong\" move. The more useful questions are usually what information was available at the time, what the group believed the problem was, what changed after the decision was made, and whether everyone recognized that change at roughly the same time. In many of these incidents, the people involved continue trying to execute a plan that made sense only a few minutes earlier. The groups that remain functional are generally the ones that recognize when the problem has changed and begin reorganizing around the situation they actually have rather than the one they expected. That is probably the common thread worth carrying through the entire collection. The shooting may be the most visible part of an incident, but the harder problem is often maintaining a shared understanding of what is happening while the situation, the available personnel, and the objective are all changing at the same time."}]},{"u":"shotgun.html","t":"Professional Shotgun Shooting","s":[{"id":"","h":"Professional Shotgun Shooting","x":"Firearm training · Professional Updated Oct 27, 2026 Professional Shotgun Shooting Shooting a moving target with a pattern instead of a single bullet. This page covers actions and gauges, shells, chokes and patterning, gun fit, the mount, stance, lead methods, the shot string, and the three main clay disciplines. Built on NRA Basics of Shotgun Shooting, NSCA and NSSA practice, and standard choke and patterning data. Pattern figures are typical values for lead shot at 40 yards. Pattern your own gun and load before relying on them."},{"id":"safety","h":"Safety","x":"01 Safety The four rules Treat every firearm as if it's loaded. Never point the muzzle at anything you aren't willing to destroy. Keep your finger off the trigger until your sights are on the target and you've decided to fire. Be sure of your target and what's beyond it. Shotgun-specific Never mix gauges in your pockets. A 20-gauge shell fits into a 12-gauge chamber and slides down to lodge in the barrel. Loading a 12 behind it can burst the barrel. Carry break-action guns open between stations. Carry pumps and semi-autos with the action open and the muzzle up or down range. Look through the bore before shooting and after any shot with a weak report. Shot falls back to earth hundreds of yards away. #7½ lead shot can carry about 250 yards; buckshot and slugs much farther. Load only when on the station and ready to call for the target. On the range A shared range runs on commands from the range safety officer (RSO). Listen for them the whole time you're on the line. With hearing protection on, commands are easy to miss: electronic muffs make speech easier to hear, and you should watch the RSO, the range lights or flags, and the shooters on either side. If everyone around you has stopped, stop and find out why. Command What it means What you do \"The range is hot\" Live fire is allowed. Nobody is forward of the firing line. Eye and ear protection on, including behind the line. Handle firearms only at your position on the line, muzzle downrange. \"Commence fire\" You may load and shoot. Load only now, and only at the line. Wait for this even when the range has been called hot. \"Cease fire\" Stop shooting immediately. Anyone who sees a danger can call it, and everyone obeys it no matter who called it. Finger off the trigger and out of the guard, muzzle downrange, and stay where you are. Don't load, unload, or step back until the RSO tells you what to do next. \"Unload and show clear\" Make your firearm safe and let the RSO check it. Remove the ammunition, open and lock the action, and show the empty chamber to the RSO. \"Is the line clear?\" / \"The line is clear\" The RSO is confirming, then announcing, that every firearm on the line is unloaded with its action open. Your gun is benched or racked: action open, chamber flag in, magazines out. Step back behind the safety line. \"The range is cold\" Nobody touches a firearm. People may go downrange to check or change targets. Hands off guns and ammunition, including loading magazines at the bench, until the range is called hot again. Stay behind the safety line unless you're going downrange. Wording varies by range and organization; some use \"the line is safe,\" whistles, or lights. Read the posted range rules and listen to the briefing before you shoot. The principle doesn't change: firing stops on a cease fire, and no one goes forward until every gun is verified clear. Clear the gun before you step off the line Finger off the trigger, muzzle downrange. Remove the ammunition source first: magazine out, or the magazine tube emptied. Open the action and lock it open. Any round in the chamber comes out. Look and feel: check the chamber and the magazine well or tube by eye, then with a finger. Do the check every time, even when you're sure it's empty. Insert a chamber flag if the range requires one. Bench, rack, case, or holster it the way the range rules say. Never walk away from a loaded gun or carry one off the line. Shotgun: empty the magazine tube completely by cycling the action or using the shell release, then look into both the chamber and the tube or lifter. Count the shells you took out against the number you loaded. A break-action gun leaves the line broken open with both chambers empty."},{"id":"actions","h":"Actions and gauges","x":"02 Actions and gauges Action How it works Strengths Watch for Over/under Two barrels stacked; break open to load Two chokes on tap, single sighting plane, easy to show clear. Standard for clays. Only two shots; higher cost Side-by-side Two barrels side by side Light, fast-handling, classic for upland Wider sighting plane takes adjustment Semi-automatic Gas or inertia cycles the action Softer felt recoil (gas), quick follow-ups Needs cleaning; light loads may not cycle inertia guns Pump Shooter cycles the forend Reliable with any load, inexpensive Short-stroking under stress Single-shot One barrel, break-action Simple, safe for beginners; dedicated trap singles guns One shot"},{"id":"shells","h":"Shells and shot","x":"03 Shells and shot Shot size Diameter Pellet weight Pellets per oz (lead) Typical use #9 0.080\" 0.75 gr ≈585 Skeet, close sporting targets #8 0.090\" 1.07 gr ≈410 Skeet, trap (16 yd), sporting #7½ 0.095\" 1.25 gr ≈350 Trap handicap, longer sporting, dove #6 0.110\" 1.95 gr ≈225 Pheasant, squirrel, rabbit #4 0.130\" 3.24 gr ≈135 Turkey, ducks (non-toxic shot required for waterfowl) 00 buck 0.330\" 53.8 gr 9 per 2¾\" shell Deer where legal, defensive use Slug bore size 437 gr (1 oz) 1 (≈1 oz) Deer, single projectile out to about 100 yd Reading the box Shell length (2¾\", 3\", 3½\"): must not exceed the chamber length stamped on the barrel. Shot weight (⅞ oz, 1 oz, 1⅛ oz): heavier loads put more pellets in the pattern and kick more. Velocity (1,145–1,250 ft/s for most target loads). Dram equivalent : an old powder-charge comparison. Compare velocity instead. Clay rule limits Registered trap, skeet, and sporting clays in the U.S. cap 12-gauge loads at 1⅛ oz of shot no larger than #7½ (#8 in some cases). Check the current NSSA, NSCA, or ATA rulebook for your event."},{"id":"gauges","h":"Loads by gauge","x":"04 Loads by gauge Shot charges are listed in ounces; 1 ounce is 437.5 grains. Smaller gauges throw lighter charges, so they put fewer pellets in the air even with the same choke. The 16 gauge sits between the 12 and 20: nearly 12-gauge payloads in a lighter gun. Gauge Target loads Field loads Buckshot Slugs 12 ⅞–1⅛ oz (383–492 gr), #7½–#9, 1,145–1,250 ft/s 1¼–1⅝ oz, #4–#6 00 buck: 8–9 pellets of 53.8 gr #1 buck: 16 of 40 gr #4 buck: 27 of 20.7 gr 1 oz (437 gr) Foster, 1,600 ft/s 300 gr sabot, 2,000 ft/s 16 ⅞–1 oz (383–437 gr), #7½–#8, 1,165–1,200 ft/s 1–1⅛ oz, #5–#7½ #1 buck: 12 pellets of 40 gr, 1,225 ft/s ⅘ oz (350 gr) Foster, 1,600 ft/s 1 oz Brenneke-style, 1,450 ft/s 20 ⅞ oz (383 gr), #7½–#9, 1,200 ft/s 1–1¼ oz, #4–#6 #3 buck: 20 pellets of 23.4 gr ¾ oz (328 gr) Foster, 1,600 ft/s 250 gr sabot, 1,800 ft/s 28 ¾ oz (328 gr), #8–#9, 1,200 ft/s ¾–1 oz, #6–#7½ rare rare .410 ½ oz (219 gr), #8–#9, 1,200–1,250 ft/s 11/16 oz, #6–#7½ 000 buck: 3–5 pellets (3\") ¼ oz (109 gr), 1,800 ft/s Load explorer Pick a gauge and load to see the payload, pellet count, and energy per pellet at the muzzle. Gauge 12 16 20 28 .410 Load"},{"id":"chokes","h":"Chokes and patterns","x":"05 Chokes and patterns A choke is a slight constriction at the muzzle that controls how quickly the pattern spreads. Tighter chokes keep more pellets in a 30-inch circle at 40 yards. They also leave less room for error up close. Pattern spread to 40 yards Choke 12-ga constriction % in 30\" circle at 40 yd Effective range for clays Cylinder 0.000\" ≈40% Under 20 yd Skeet 0.005\" ≈45% Skeet, 20–25 yd Improved cylinder 0.010\" ≈50% 20–30 yd Light modified 0.015\" ≈55% 25–35 yd Modified 0.020\" ≈60% 30–40 yd; trap singles Improved modified 0.025\" ≈65% 35–45 yd Full 0.030–0.035\" ≈70% 40 yd+; trap handicap Steel shot patterns tighter than lead through the same choke. Use improved cylinder or modified with steel, and never fire steel through a choke marked lead-only or tighter than the maker allows."},{"id":"slugs","h":"Slug trajectory","x":"06 Slug trajectory Slugs are heavy and slow and lose velocity quickly. Most smoothbore Foster slugs are practical to about 75–100 yards; sabot slugs from a rifled barrel reach 150 yards or more. Pick a gauge and slug to see drop and holds."},{"id":"patterning","h":"Patterning your gun","x":"07 Patterning your gun Procedure Hang paper at least 40 inches square at 40 yards (25 yards for skeet chokes). Mark an aiming point in the center. Fire one shot from a rest, aiming as you would at a target. Draw the densest 30-inch circle on the sheet, centered on the pattern, not on the aiming point. Count the pellet holes inside it and divide by the number of pellets in the shell (from the box or by cutting one open). Fire at least 3–5 sheets per choke and load, and average them. Single patterns vary a lot. What else to check Point of impact : where the pattern center is versus the aiming point. Many trap guns shoot 60/40 or 70/30 (more pattern above the aiming point) on purpose for rising targets. Gaps : holes in the pattern big enough for a clay to slip through. Each barrel on an O/U or SxS. They can shoot to slightly different points."},{"id":"fit","h":"Gun fit and eye dominance","x":"08 Gun fit and eye dominance With a shotgun your eye is the rear sight. If the gun doesn't put your eye in the same spot over the rib every time, the pattern goes somewhere else. Fit is what makes a good mount produce a good sight picture. Quick fit checks Length of pull : with the gun mounted, about two fingers' width between the thumb of the trigger hand and your nose. A common starting point is 14–14¾\". Comb height : mount with eyes closed, then open. You should see a little of the rib with the bead sitting on it (a \"figure 8\" with mid-bead guns). Seeing a lot of rib means the gun shoots high; seeing the back of the receiver means it shoots low. Pattern the gun at 16 yd aiming at a dot, mounting quickly, 10 shots. The center of the shots shows where the gun really shoots for you. Eye dominance Extend both arms and make a small triangle with your hands. With both eyes open, look through it at a distant object. Close one eye at a time. The eye that still sees the object is dominant. If your dominant eye is opposite your shooting shoulder, either shoot from the other shoulder, or put a small dot of tape on that side of your shooting glasses so the off eye can't take over."},{"id":"mount","h":"Mount and stance","x":"09 Mount and stance Mount Start with the muzzle at the hold point and your eyes on the visual pickup point. Push the gun out toward the target with the front hand; the muzzle leads the motion. Bring the comb up to your cheek. Your head stays still; the gun comes to the face, not the face to the gun. The butt settles into the shoulder pocket last. The mount and the swing happen together, so the muzzle is on the line of the target as the gun reaches the face. Stance Front foot points toward the break point (where you intend to hit the target). Feet about shoulder-width, weight about 60% on the front foot, front knee slightly bent. Rotate at the hips and waist to swing, not just the arms. Set up so you're comfortable at the break point and twist back to the hold point. Front hand points at the target, index finger along the forend."},{"id":"lead","h":"Lead methods","x":"10 Lead methods Shot takes time to reach the target, so you shoot where the target will be. All three methods get the muzzle ahead of the target; they differ in where the muzzle starts and how it gets there. Keep the swing going after the shot (follow-through) with every method. Muzzle path relative to a crossing target Method Muzzle starts Strength Weak point Swing-through Behind the target Natural, speed of the swing creates lead Inconsistent on long crossers; swing speed varies Pull-away On the target Accurate, easy to read the line Muzzle can stall on the target Maintained lead Ahead of the target Most consistent at long range Requires knowing the lead picture; tempts measuring"},{"id":"string","h":"The shot string","x":"11 The shot string Pellets don't arrive as a flat disc. Slower and deformed pellets lag behind, stretching the charge into a column several feet long by 40 yards. A crossing target moving through that column can be hit by trailing pellets even if the lead was slightly long, which is one reason a little extra lead often works better than too little. Shot string from above"},{"id":"disciplines","h":"Trap, skeet, sporting clays","x":"12 Trap, skeet, sporting clays Skeet: eight stations, two houses Discipline Layout Targets Typical choke and load Trap 5 stations in an arc 16 yd behind one trap house (handicap: 17–27 yd) Going-away targets at an unknown angle within about 44° (22° each side) Modified to full; 1⅛ oz #7½ or #8 Skeet 8 stations; high house left, low house right, 40 yd apart Fixed crossing targets; singles and doubles from stations 1, 2, 6, 7 Skeet or IC; #9 Sporting clays 10–15 stations along a course Varied: crossers, rabbits, teal, incomers, report pairs, true pairs Changes by station; carry several chokes 5-Stand 5 cages, 6–8 traps Sporting-style menu at each cage IC or light modified"},{"id":"handling","h":"Gun handling","x":"13 Gun handling On the station Load only when it's your turn and you're on the station. Set the feet, set the hold point, set the eyes, then call \"Pull\" (or your call word). Unload before stepping off. With a break-action, open it and leave it open. Malfunctions Pump short-stroke : the forend wasn't run fully back and forward. Run it hard, all the way, both directions. Semi-auto fails to cycle : often a light load, a dirty gas system, or a loose hold on inertia guns. Clean the gas piston and try a heavier load. Click, no bang : keep the muzzle downrange for 30 seconds in case of a hang fire, then open the action."},{"id":"maint","h":"Maintenance","x":"14 Maintenance After each session Wipe the bores; plastic fouling from wads builds up in the choke area. Remove choke tubes, clean the threads in the barrel and on the tube, and apply a thin coat of choke-tube grease. Wipe and lightly oil exterior metal, especially in humid weather. Periodically Over/under: clean and grease the hinge pins and monobloc contact points. Gas guns: clean the piston, gas ports, and magazine tube. Check choke tubes are tight before shooting. Tubes loosen under recoil and a loose tube can be damaged."},{"id":"errors","h":"Common errors","x":"15 Common errors Error What it does Fix Stopping the gun Muzzle stalls at the shot; misses behind Follow through; keep swinging after the shot as if there's a second target Lifting the head Eye rises above the rib; shot goes high and the shooter loses the target Keep the cheek on the comb until the target breaks Looking at the barrel or bead Swing slows, lead becomes measured Hard focus on the leading edge of the target; see the muzzle only in peripheral vision Mounting then moving Gun mounted off the line, then chases the target Mount and move together Muzzle too close to the trap Gun has to chase the target; swing becomes a jerk Set the hold point closer to the break point Arm-only swing Swing stops when the arms run out of range Turn from the hips; set feet for the break point Wrong choke for the distance Pattern too thin at range or too tight up close Match choke to break distance (table above)"}]},{"u":"water.html","t":"Water","s":[{"id":"","h":"Water","x":"Preparedness · Water Updated Nov 27, 2026 Water Water runs out first and matters most: how much to store, how to store it so it stays safe, how to make questionable water drinkable, where the water already hiding in your house is, and how to handle sanitation when the taps stop. This is a reference, not public-health advice. When your local water authority issues a boil or do-not-drink notice, follow it. No home method removes chemical contamination such as fuel, pesticides, or industrial runoff: if water may be chemically contaminated, don't drink it, whatever you do to it."},{"id":"how-much","h":"How much you need","x":"01 How much you need The standard planning figure is one gallon per person per day : about half a gallon to drink and the rest for food preparation and basic hygiene. That's a minimum, not a comfortable amount. Store at least two weeks Ready.gov's floor is three days; two weeks covers most real outages, boil notices, and storm recoveries. For a family of four that's 56 gallons. Plan for more Hot weather can double what people need. So can heavy work, illness, pregnancy, and nursing. Children and older adults dehydrate faster. Don't forget pets Dogs and cats need water too; a medium dog drinks roughly a quart or more a day, more in heat. Count them in the total. Drink it, don't ration it Drink what your body needs today and look for more tomorrow. Rationing water while it's still available causes dehydration that impairs judgment exactly when you need it."},{"id":"storing","h":"Storing water","x":"02 Storing water Container Good for Notes Commercially bottled water Easiest start Sealed and safe until opened. Keep it in its original packaging; rotate by the date on it. Food-grade water jugs (2–7 gal) Most households Look for HDPE plastic marked #2 and sold for drinking water. Portable, stackable, easy to rotate. Food-grade barrels (30–55 gal) Large volumes at home Too heavy to move once full (a 55-gal barrel weighs about 460 lb). Store on wood or a pallet, not bare concrete, and keep a siphon or barrel pump with it. Bathtub bladder (WaterBOB-type) Short notice before a storm Fill the tub with a liner bag when a storm is forecast. Holds 60–100 gallons for the following days. Don't use Milk and juice jugs (they're hard to clean and break down within months), containers that held chemicals, and anything not made for food. Fill and seal it right Wash containers with soap and water, rinse, sanitize with a teaspoon of unscented bleach per quart of water, rinse again, then fill from a safe tap. Municipal tap water already contains chlorine; well water should be treated as in section 04. Where to keep it Cool, dark, and away from gasoline, paint, pesticides, and solvents: plastic lets vapors through. Not in direct sun, which warms it and grows algae. Rotate it Replace home-filled water every six months (an easy rule: when the clocks change). Write the fill date on every container."},{"id":"treating","h":"Making water safe","x":"03 Making water safe Any water that isn't sealed bottled water or stored tap water should be treated before drinking, brushing teeth, washing food, or making baby formula. Method Kills or removes Does not handle How Boiling (best) Bacteria, viruses, protozoa Chemicals, sediment Bring to a rolling boil for 1 minute ( 3 minutes above 6,500 ft). Let it cool covered. Unscented household bleach Bacteria, viruses; some protozoa Cryptosporidium, chemicals See the dosing table in section 04. Wait 30 minutes. Chlorine dioxide tablets Bacteria, viruses, Giardia; Cryptosporidium with a long wait Chemicals Follow the package; usually 30 minutes, up to 4 hours for Cryptosporidium or cold water. Microfilter (0.1–0.2 micron) Bacteria, protozoa, sediment Most viruses, chemicals Pair with chemical treatment where viruses are a concern (flooding, sewage). Purifier (filter + virus stage) Bacteria, protozoa, viruses Most chemicals Check the label for a virus claim. UV pen Bacteria, viruses, protozoa Cloudy water, chemicals Only works in clear water; needs batteries. Cloudy water: let it settle, then pour the clear water off or strain it through a clean cloth, paper towel, or coffee filter before any treatment. Sediment shields germs from chlorine and UV and clogs filters."},{"id":"bleach","h":"Bleach dosing","x":"04 Bleach dosing Use regular, unscented chlorine bleach with no additives (not \"splash-less\", scented, or color-safe), and check the label for its strength. Bleach loses strength over time; use a bottle less than a year old. Water volume 6% bleach 8.25% bleach 1 quart / 1 liter 2 drops 2 drops 1 gallon 8 drops (about 1/8 tsp) 6 drops 2 gallons 16 drops (1/4 tsp) 12 drops (1/8 tsp) 4 gallons 1/3 tsp 1/4 tsp 8 gallons 2/3 tsp 1/2 tsp Most regular bleach sold in the U.S. is now 7.5%. For anything between 5% and 9%, the CDC's simple rule works: 8 drops (a little under 1/8 teaspoon) per gallon of clear water, 16 drops (1/4 teaspoon) per gallon of cloudy water. Stir, then wait 30 minutes . The water should have a slight chlorine smell. If it doesn't, repeat the dose and wait another 15 minutes. If the water was cloudy and you couldn't clear it, double the dose (CDC and EPA)."},{"id":"filters","h":"Filters and tablets","x":"05 Filters and tablets Personal straw or squeeze filter Light and cheap (Sawyer Squeeze, LifeStraw). Good in a go-bag. Filters bacteria and protozoa, not viruses or chemicals. Don't let a used hollow-fiber filter freeze: ice cracks the fibers invisibly. Gravity filter A dirty bag hung above a clean one, or a countertop unit (Berkey-type). Filters gallons without pumping; good for a household. Check what the specific model is certified to remove. Tablets Chlorine dioxide tablets (Katadyn Micropur, Aquamira) weigh almost nothing and keep for years. Iodine tablets don't kill Cryptosporidium and aren't for pregnant women or people with thyroid problems. Belt and suspenders For water from floods, ditches, or near livestock: settle, filter, then treat chemically or boil. Each step covers what the others miss."},{"id":"hidden","h":"Water already in your house","x":"06 Water already in your house Water heater (40–80 gal) Turn off the power or gas first. Close the cold supply valve, open a hot tap upstairs to let air in, and drain from the valve at the bottom. The first water out may carry sediment: let it settle. Don't turn the heater back on until it's full again. Pipes Shut the main valve so contaminated water can't flow back in, open the highest faucet in the house, then collect from the lowest one. Toilet tank (not the bowl) The tank behind the bowl holds clean supply water, unless you use in-tank cleaning tablets. Treat it before drinking. Ice and canned food Melted ice cubes and the liquid in canned fruit and vegetables count. Swimming pools and hot tubs: use for flushing toilets and washing, not drinking. Pool chemicals aren't safe to drink and boiling concentrates them."},{"id":"outside","h":"Sources outside","x":"07 Sources outside Rain A clean tarp or a gutter downspout into a covered, food-grade barrel. Divert the first few minutes of rain (\"first flush\"), which carries roof dirt and droppings. Treat before drinking. Streams, lakes, springs Moving water is better than still. Collect upstream of roads, farms, and towns where you can. Always treat: clear water still carries Giardia and bacteria. Floodwater Avoid it. It's mixed with sewage, fuel, and chemicals that no home method removes. Carry capacity Water weighs 8.3 lb per gallon. A collapsible jug or a few clean buckets with lids make a trip worth the effort."},{"id":"sanitation","h":"Sanitation without running water","x":"08 Sanitation without running water Disease from poor sanitation kills more people after disasters than the disaster itself. Plan this before you need it. Flushing with a bucket If the sewer still works, pour a gallon or two of non-drinking water quickly into the bowl to flush. Don't do this if sewers are backing up. Bucket toilet A 5-gallon bucket lined with heavy trash bags, a toilet seat lid made for it, and something absorbent to cover each use (cat litter, sawdust, or peat). Seal and store bags outside away from water sources. Hand washing Keep a jug with a spigot, soap, and a catch basin by the bucket toilet and the kitchen. Hand sanitizer is a backup, not a replacement, when hands are visibly dirty. Disinfecting surfaces For surfaces (not drinking water), about 1/3 cup of unscented bleach per gallon of water. Never mix bleach with ammonia or other cleaners."}]}];
